window.COURSE.notes["csfe"] = [{"n": 1, "title": "Listening (CO1)", "tagline": "Listening looks like the laziest of the four skills — you just sit there and let sound hit your ears.", "minutes": 41, "sections": [{"kind": "big", "num": "", "title": "Big picture, 30 seconds", "html": "<p>Listening looks like the laziest of the four skills — you just sit there and let sound hit your ears. It is actually the hardest, because you cannot slow the speaker down, you cannot re-read a sentence, and the words disappear the moment they are spoken.</p>\n<p>Here is the analogy I want you to keep for the whole module. Think of listening as <strong>catching a cricket ball in the dark</strong>. Your hands (your ears plus your expectations) have to meet the ball exactly where it will be, not where it was. A good listener is not someone with better ears; it is someone who <em>predicts</em> where the sentence is going and is already waiting there. Everything in this module — note-taking, accent training, critical listening — is a way of improving that prediction.</p>\n<p>Where you will actually use this: a 50-minute lecture where the professor says the exam-relevant line once, a lab viva where the examiner's tone tells you whether your answer is on track, an IELTS or TOEFL listening section, a project meeting where a client's hesitation means \"I am not convinced\", and a phone call with a recruiter where you must catch a date and a time correctly the first time.</p>\n<p>One honest warning before we start: <strong>listening is not hearing.</strong> Hearing is the ear registering sound. Listening is the brain assigning meaning, and it is an active, effortful, trainable skill. That is why this module has techniques in it, not just advice to \"pay attention\".</p>"}, {"kind": "basics", "num": "", "title": "Basics you must know first", "html": "<ul><li><strong>Phoneme</strong> — the smallest sound unit that changes meaning in a language. English <em>ship</em> and <em>sheep</em> differ by one phoneme.</li><li><strong>Stress</strong> — the extra loudness, length and pitch on one syllable of a word (as in <em>TEAcher</em>) or one word of a sentence.</li><li><strong>Accent vs dialect</strong> — accent is only pronunciation; dialect is pronunciation plus vocabulary plus grammar. Two accents of English are both correct English.</li><li><strong>Gist</strong> — the main idea of a stretch of speech, without the details.</li><li><strong>Register</strong> — how formal the language is. \"Please be seated\" is formal; \"sit down, yaar\" is informal.</li><li><strong>Signpost</strong> — a spoken marker that tells you the structure of what is coming (\"first\", \"on the other hand\", \"to sum up\").</li></ul>"}, {"kind": "concept", "num": "1.1", "title": "Listening purposes and types", "html": "<h4>Intuition</h4>\n<p>You do not listen the same way in every situation, and you should not. When your mother asks \"did you eat?\", you are not analysing evidence — you are receiving care. When a police officer asks \"where were you at 9 pm?\", you are being evaluated and you listen for what is being implied. Same ears, completely different job.</p>\n<p>So: <strong>purpose decides type.</strong> Get the purpose wrong and you will listen beautifully for the wrong thing. A student who listens to a lecture as pure entertainment remembers the professor's jokes and misses the definition.</p>\n<h4>The idea, step by step</h4>\n<p><strong>Step 1 — Know the four purposes.</strong> We listen to <strong>inform</strong> (get facts: a timetable announcement), to <strong>evaluate</strong> (judge: a project pitch), to <strong>empathise</strong> (share feeling: a friend's bad day), and to <strong>enjoy</strong> (pleasure: a song, a comic's set). Most real situations mix two. A viva is inform + evaluate.</p>\n<p><strong>Step 2 — Match the type to the purpose.</strong> Seven types come up in the syllabus:</p>\n<div class=\"tbl-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Type</th><th>What you do</th><th>Typical situation</th><th>What you are NOT doing</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Active</strong></td><td>Full attention, respond, ask, confirm</td><td>Interview, viva</td><td>Daydreaming while nodding</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Passive</strong></td><td>Background reception, low effort</td><td>TV in the hostel mess</td><td>Taking notes</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Selective</strong></td><td>Hunt for specific information only</td><td>Announcement: \"platform 4\"</td><td>Following the whole speech</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Empathetic</strong></td><td>Attend to feeling, not facts</td><td>Friend venting</td><td>Giving solutions</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Comprehensive</strong></td><td>Understand everything, retain it</td><td>Lecture you will be examined on</td><td>Skipping parts</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Critical</strong></td><td>Judge truth, logic, bias</td><td>Political speech, advertisement</td><td>Accepting the claim</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Appreciative</strong></td><td>Enjoy craft and beauty</td><td>Ghazal, film dialogue</td><td>Analysing evidence</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><strong>Step 3 — Notice that these are not ranked.</strong> Passive listening is not \"bad listening\". In the mess at dinner, passive is correct. The mistake is using passive when the situation demands comprehensive or critical.</p>\n<p><strong>Step 4 — Switch deliberately.</strong> Skilled listeners announce the switch to themselves: \"OK, the marks-relevant part starts now.\" This single habit is worth more than any other tip in this module.</p>\n<p><strong>Step 5 — Empathetic listening has one visible signature.</strong> You reflect feeling back instead of solving. <em>\"That sounds exhausting\"</em> — not <em>\"you should have complained to the HOD.\"</em> The second one is a solution, not empathy.</p>\n<h4>Formula / rule / table</h4>\n<p>Which type fits which cue:</p>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>hear \"the exam pattern has changed\"        -&gt; comprehensive (write it down)\nhear a number, name, date, platform        -&gt; selective (grab it, ignore the rest)\nhear \"I'm just so tired of this\"           -&gt; empathetic (reflect, don't fix)\nhear \"our product is 100% safe\"            -&gt; critical (ask: says who? compared to what?)\nhear a song you love                       -&gt; appreciative</code></pre>\n<div class=\"card\"><h4>Example question</h4><p>You are in a placement-preparation session. The trainer says: <em>\"For the first twenty minutes I will explain the format of the aptitude test, then we will do a mock round, and at the end I will tell you which companies are visiting in December.\"</em> Identify the purpose and the listening type for each of the three phases, and say what you would physically do in each.</p>\n<h4>Solution</h4>\n<ul><li><strong>Phase 1 (test format) — purpose: to inform; type: comprehensive.</strong> Reason: this is the part you will be examined on, so nothing here is optional. Physical action: pen out, Cornell page ready (see 1.3), write section names and timings, do not decorate.</li><li><strong>Phase 2 (mock round) — purpose: to evaluate; type: active.</strong> Reason: you are now performing and receiving feedback, so listening includes responding to instructions in real time. Physical action: keep eye contact with the trainer, confirm instructions back (\"so we attempt Section B first?\"), note the errors the trainer points out in your own work.</li><li><strong>Phase 3 (December companies) — purpose: to inform, but the information is a list; type: selective.</strong> Reason: you only need names, dates and eligibility, not the trainer's commentary. Physical action: write a quick three-column list — company / date / CGPA cut-off — and let the rest of the sentences pass.</li></ul>\n<div class=\"res\">Phase 1 = comprehensive, Phase 2 = active, Phase 3 = selective; all three are \"to inform/evaluate\" purposes served by different listening types.</div>\n<p><strong>Why this works:</strong> the answer does not just name labels, it justifies each label by what the listener physically does. In an exam, that justification is what earns the marks — naming the type alone is worth about half.</p>\n<div class=\"note note--trap\"><span class=\"tag\">Common trap</span><p>students label the <em>whole</em> session \"active listening\" and stop there. Real listening changes type inside a single hour. Also, \"critical listening\" does not mean \"finding faults in the speaker's personality\" — it means testing the <em>claim</em>.</p></div></div>"}, {"kind": "concept", "num": "1.2", "title": "Comprehension across accents and varieties of English", "html": "<h4>Intuition</h4>\n<p>Imagine three people say the word <em>schedule</em>: an Indian colleague says \"SKED-jool\", an American says \"SKED-jool\", a British colleague says \"SHED-yool\". Nothing is broken. They are all saying the same word in their own accent. The panic you feel is not a language problem — it is a <em>prediction</em> problem. Your brain had one sound shape stored and the sound arriving did not match it.</p>\n<p>So the fix is not \"listen harder\". The fix is <strong>widening the set of shapes your brain will accept</strong> for the same word. That is a memory job, not an effort job.</p>\n<h4>The idea, step by step</h4>\n<p><strong>Step 1 — Why accents differ at all.</strong> Three main reasons:</p>\n<ol><li><strong>Rhythm type.</strong> English is usually described as <strong>stress-timed</strong>: stressed syllables come at roughly even intervals and unstressed syllables get squeezed. Many Indian languages, including Hindi, are usually described as <strong>syllable-timed</strong>: every syllable gets similar time. (Linguists argue about how absolute this distinction is, so treat it as a strong tendency, not a law.) The practical effect is real: an Indian speaker of English may pronounce every syllable fully, while a British speaker crushes \"vegetable\" into three syllables — VEG-tuh-bul — or even \"VEJ-tuh-bul\".</li><li><strong>Vowel shifts.</strong> Vowels drift between varieties. The word <em>dance</em> is /dɑːns/ in much of southern England and /dæns/ in most of North America. <em>Better</em> is rhotic in the US (the r is pronounced) and non-rhotic in much of England (the r is dropped).</li><li><strong>Consonant and cluster habits.</strong> Some varieties drop or add sounds: word-final consonant clusters get simplified in many varieties (\"tes\" for \"test\" in some Caribbean and West African speech), and /θ/ becomes /t/ or /f/ in several.</li></ol>\n<p><strong>Step 2 — Accept Indian English as a legitimate variety.</strong> Indian English has its own stable, rule-governed features: retroflex /t/ and /d/, near-open /ɛ/ in words like <em>bad</em>, syllable-timing, \"isn't it?\" as a general tag question, and words like <em>prepone</em>, <em>batchmate</em>, <em>do the needful</em>. These are <strong>not errors</strong>; they are variety features. What <em>is</em> an error is a feature that damages intelligibility or a genuine grammar slip (for example, \"he don't know\"). Say this clearly in an exam: Indian English is a recognised variety (Kachru's \"Outer Circle\"), and standard Indian English is perfectly acceptable in Indian professional settings and increasingly abroad. Where it matters — an international client call — you <em>adapt</em> your accent for clarity; you do not erase your identity.</p>\n<p><strong>Step 3 — Build the \"shape set\" for problem words.</strong> Make a personal list of words where the accent difference bites you:</p>\n<div class=\"tbl-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Word</th><th>Common Indian English</th><th>British</th><th>American</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>schedule</td><td>SKED-jool</td><td>SHED-yool</td><td>SKED-jool</td></tr><tr><td>vitamin</td><td>VIT-uh-min</td><td>VIT-uh-min</td><td>VYE-tuh-min</td></tr><tr><td>either</td><td>EE-ther</td><td>EYE-ther</td><td>EE-ther</td></tr><tr><td>lieutenant</td><td>lef-TEN-ant</td><td>lef-TEN-ant</td><td>loo-TEN-ant</td></tr><tr><td>aluminium</td><td>al-yoo-MIN-ee-um</td><td>al-yoo-MIN-ee-um</td><td>uh-LOO-mih-num</td></tr><tr><td>garage</td><td>GAR-aaj</td><td>GAR-ij / GAR-aazh</td><td>guh-RAZH</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><strong>Step 4 — Use four repair strategies when an accent defeats you.</strong></p>\n<ol><li><strong>Predict from context.</strong> If the topic is a laboratory, \"we need to <em>cull-ih-brate</em> the sensor\" is almost certainly \"calibrate\".</li><li><strong>Listen for the stressed word, skip the rest.</strong> Content words (nouns, verbs, adjectives) carry the meaning; grammatical words get crushed. Catch <em>\"delivery … Tuesday … not Friday\"</em> and you have the message.</li><li><strong>Ask a specific question, not \"sorry?\".</strong> <em>\"Sorry, was that Tuesday or Thursday?\"</em> is answerable in one word. <em>\"Sorry?\"</em> invites a full repetition, which you may miss again.</li><li><strong>Re-anchor with a paraphrase.</strong> <em>\"So the deadline moves to the 12th — is that right?\"</em> Now the other person confirms or corrects, and you have certainty.</li></ol>\n<p><strong>Step 5 — Practise variety on purpose.</strong> One week of an Australian news podcast, one week of a Nigerian or Kenyan speaker, one week of BBC, one of NPR. Note five words per week where your prediction failed. That list is your accent dictionary.</p>\n<div class=\"card\"><h4>Example question</h4><p>In a video call, a Scottish client says something you transcribe as <em>\"We'll need the figures by the morrow, and can you mind the deets in the appendix?\"</em> You genuinely did not understand two words. Write (a) your in-call repair line, and (b) your explanation of why the two words were hard, using the concepts of this sub-topic.</p>\n<h4>Solution</h4>\n<ul><li>(a) Repair line: <em>\"Just to be sure I have it right — you need the figures by tomorrow morning, and you'd like me to check the details in the appendix?\"</em> This is a paraphrase-plus-check, not a bare \"sorry\". Reason: it gives the speaker something to confirm, so even a one-word \"yes\" resolves it, and it protects the meeting's flow.</li><li>(b) Why the words were hard:</li><li><em>\"the morrow\"</em> is a Scottish and northern-English word for \"tomorrow\" — a <strong>lexical</strong> difference (vocabulary), not an accent difference. This is a variety feature, and it is the kind of thing no amount of ear-training fixes; you fix it by asking.</li><li><em>\"deets\"</em> is informal clipping of \"details\" — again vocabulary, plus <strong>register</strong>: informal speech in a formal meeting.</li><li>If the client also <em>pronounced</em> \"figures\" as \"figures\" with a tapped or dropped /r/ (non-rhotic Scottish English is rhotic, but the vowel is different), that would be a <strong>vowel-shift</strong> difficulty. In this sentence the difficulty is mainly lexical.</li></ul>\n<div class=\"res\">repair by paraphrasing and checking (\"you need the figures by tomorrow morning and the details in the appendix — right?\"), and diagnose the difficulty as vocabulary/register variety rather than as an accent failure.</div>\n<p><strong>Why this works:</strong> the answer separates <em>accent</em> (sound) from <em>variety</em> (words and grammar). Most students blame \"his accent\" for what is actually an unfamiliar word, and that misdiagnosis means they never fix it.</p>\n<div class=\"note note--trap\"><span class=\"tag\">Common trap</span><p>writing \"Indian English is wrong English\" or \"we should speak like the British\". Both lose marks. Also do not claim English is <em>strictly</em> stress-timed and Hindi <em>strictly</em> syllable-timed as an absolute law — say \"usually described as\", and you are safe.</p></div></div>"}, {"kind": "concept", "num": "1.3", "title": "Effective note-taking", "html": "<h4>Intuition</h4>\n<p>Notes are not a transcript. A transcript is what the speaker said; notes are <strong>what you will need in three weeks</strong>. The difference is compression, and compression needs a system, otherwise you write fast for ten minutes, look down, and find a wall of sentences you never read again.</p>\n<p>The system I recommend is <strong>Cornell notes</strong>, because it forces the most valuable step — turning the page into questions — and it is what makes revision possible without re-listening.</p>\n<h4>The idea, step by step</h4>\n<p><strong>Step 1 — Set up the page before the lecture.</strong> Divide the page into three zones:</p>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>+----------------------+------------------------------+\n|  CUE COLUMN (~1/3)   |  NOTES AREA (~2/3)           |\n|  questions, keywords |  main points during lecture  |\n|  written AFTER       |  short phrases, arrows, eg.  |\n|                      |                              |\n\n+----------------------+------------------------------+\n|  SUMMARY (3-5 lines, written within 24 hours)       |\n\n+-----------------------------------------------------+</code></pre>\n<p><strong>Step 2 — In the notes area, write phrases, not sentences.</strong> \"Loudness + length + pitch → stress\" beats \"The professor explained that stress is created by a combination of loudness, length and pitch.\"</p>\n<p><strong>Step 3 — Build an abbreviation set you can actually read later.</strong> Two rules: (i) never abbreviate the <em>idea</em>, only the <em>word</em>; (ii) keep one fixed set so you do not decode it twice.</p>\n<div class=\"tbl-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Abbrev.</th><th>Means</th><th>Abbrev.</th><th>Means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>∴</td><td>therefore</td><td>eg</td><td>for example</td></tr><tr><td>∵</td><td>because</td><td>ie</td><td>that is</td></tr><tr><td>→</td><td>leads to / becomes</td><td>≠</td><td>is not the same as</td></tr><tr><td>↑ / ↓</td><td>increase / decrease</td><td>w/</td><td>with</td></tr><tr><td>esp.</td><td>especially</td><td>diff.</td><td>difference</td></tr><tr><td>def.</td><td>definition</td><td>Q</td><td>likely exam question</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>Personal ones are fine too: <code>comm.</code> = communication, <code>pron.</code> = pronunciation, <code>S</code> = speaker. Do not invent so many that you cannot read your own page.</p>\n<p><strong>Step 4 — Listen for signposts and let them shape the page.</strong> Signposts are the speaker's structural signals; catching them means you get the skeleton even if you miss some flesh.</p>\n<div class=\"tbl-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Signpost</th><th>What it tells you</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>\"There are three types of…\"</td><td>A numbered list is starting — number your page 1, 2, 3 immediately</td></tr><tr><td>\"First / Second / Finally\"</td><td>Move to the next numbered item</td></tr><tr><td>\"On the other hand\", \"However\"</td><td>A contrast is coming — write it opposite the earlier point</td></tr><tr><td>\"For example\", \"such as\"</td><td>Detail, not a new point — indent it</td></tr><tr><td>\"In other words\", \"that is to say\"</td><td>A re-statement: your second chance to catch the idea</td></tr><tr><td>\"This is important\", \"Note that\", \"You'll need this\"</td><td>Mark with a star — high exam probability</td></tr><tr><td>\"To sum up\", \"In conclusion\"</td><td>The summary line writes itself; copy it</td></tr><tr><td>\"Actually\", \"sorry, I mean\"</td><td>A correction: cross out the old value, do not just add</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><strong>Step 5 — Within 24 hours, fill the cue column and summary.</strong> Cue column = one question per chunk (\"Why does stress-timing matter for listening?\"). Summary = three to five lines in your own words. This step is what converts notes into memory; skipping it is why students \"have notes\" and still fail.</p>\n<div class=\"card\"><h4>Example question</h4><p>You hear this in a lecture: <em>\"Now, listening is often confused with hearing. Hearing is physiological; listening is psychological. There are three barriers to listening — physical, psychological and semantic. Semantic barriers, that is, problems with meaning, are the ones students underestimate. And this will be in your test.\"</em> Produce a Cornell-style page for this passage using the abbreviation set, and write the cue column and summary.</p>\n<h4>Solution</h4>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>CUE COLUMN                          NOTES AREA\n                                    \n1. Hearing vs listening?            Listening ≠ hearing\n                                    - hearing = physiological\n                                    - listening = psychological\n2. 3 barriers?                      3 barriers to listening:\n                                    1. physical\n                                    2. psychological\n                                    3. semantic ★ (students\n                                       underestimate)\n3. Why does semantic matter?        semantic = problems w/ meaning\n                                    eg unknown word, jargon\n4. Exam?                            ★ \"this will be in your test\"\n                                    → expect a 3-mark Q on barriers\n\nSUMMARY: Listening is psychological, hearing is physiological.\nThree barriers: physical, psychological, semantic; semantic is the\nmost underestimated and is exam-relevant.</code></pre>\n<ul><li>Cue column: one question per numbered chunk — this is why it works, because each question can be self-tested in ten seconds later.</li><li>Notes area: numbered list created the moment \"three barriers\" was heard; the star marks the exam signal; \"eg\" indents the detail so it does not look like a fourth barrier.</li><li>Summary: written in the student's own words, which is what proves comprehension.</li></ul>\n<div class=\"res\">a three-zone page with a numbered barrier list, a starred exam signal, four cue questions and a three-line summary.</div>\n<p><strong>Why this works:</strong> the page preserves <em>structure</em> (the number three), <em>emphasis</em> (the star) and <em>testability</em> (the cue questions). A student who wrote the same content as flowing sentences would have lost all three.</p>\n<div class=\"note note--trap\"><span class=\"tag\">Common trap</span><p>copying full sentences and then having no time to listen. Also: writing the summary later \"when I have time\" — you will not, and the summary is the part that does the learning. A third trap: abbreviating so aggressively (<code>sem. bar. undst.</code>) that the page is unreadable in week 12.</p></div></div>"}, {"kind": "concept", "num": "1.4", "title": "Academic and professional listening", "html": "<h4>Intuition</h4>\n<p>Academic and professional listening are the same muscle used under different rules. In a lecture, the information is <em>packaged for you</em> — the professor repeats, summarises, writes on the board. In a meeting, the information is <em>unpackaged and shared</em> — half of it arrives in fragments, in the wrong order, from three people at once.</p>\n<p>So the shift is this: in a lecture you listen for <strong>content</strong>; in a meeting you listen for <strong>commitments</strong> — who agreed to do what, by when.</p>\n<h4>The idea, step by step</h4>\n<h4>Step 1 — Know what changes in each setting.</h4>\n<div class=\"tbl-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Setting</th><th>Speaker's pattern</th><th>What you listen for</th><th>Your output</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Lecture</strong></td><td>Linear, signposted, repeats key points</td><td>Definitions, examples, \"this is important\", exam hints</td><td>Cornell notes</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Seminar</strong></td><td>Interactive, tentative, multiple voices</td><td>The <em>question under discussion</em>, not every comment</td><td>Notes + one prepared question</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Meeting</strong></td><td>Non-linear, interruptions, decisions</td><td>Decisions, owners, deadlines, disagreements</td><td>Action items list</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Instructions</strong></td><td>Sequential, often one-time only</td><td>Sequence words, quantities, constraints</td><td>Read-back confirmation</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Phone / video call</strong></td><td>No visual cues, poor audio, accent spread</td><td>Names, numbers, dates; confirm everything</td><td>Repeat-back summary</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><strong>Step 2 — In lectures, use the three-pass method.</strong> Pass 1: listen to the <em>structure</em> (what are the sections?). Pass 2: fill in the <em>points</em> under each section. Pass 3: catch the <em>examples and numbers</em>. You cannot do all three simultaneously, so cycle — this is exactly what the Cornell layout allows.</p>\n<p><strong>Step 3 — In seminars, listen for the question, not the noise.</strong> A seminar is one long attempt to answer a question. Write the question at the top of the page and then slot each comment as \"supports / challenges / irrelevant\". That single act turns a confusing hour into a tidy debate map.</p>\n<p><strong>Step 4 — In meetings, listen in four buckets.</strong> Every utterance goes into one of: <strong>Decision</strong>, <strong>Action</strong>, <strong>Concern</strong>, <strong>Context</strong>. You only need to write the first three. Use this layout:</p>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>DECISION:  Use AWS for the pilot\nACTION:    Priya - cost sheet - by Fri 21 Nov\nCONCERN:   Data residency rules unclear (Rahul)\nCONTEXT:   Client wants demo in Dec</code></pre>\n<p><strong>Step 5 — For instructions, always read back.</strong> The sequence is: listen → note the numbers → <strong>repeat back in your own words</strong> → get a \"yes\". This is not weakness; it is standard practice in aviation, surgery and software deployment, because spoken instructions are the least reliable form of data transfer.</p>\n<p><strong>Step 6 — Handle the two hard cases.</strong> (i) <em>You missed a point</em>: do not stop the speaker mid-flow; mark a gap symbol <code>??</code> and ask at the next pause. (ii) <em>The speaker is too fast</em>: switch to selective listening — capture the nouns and numbers, and reconstruct the grammar later.</p>\n<div class=\"card\"><h4>Example question</h4><p>In a project meeting you hear: <em>\"OK so we were thinking of the React front end, but given the timeline, let's park that and go with plain HTML for the pilot. Priya, can you get the hosting cost by Friday? And someone needs to check whether the data has to stay in India — Rahul, that's you. Right, we'll review again next Wednesday.\"</em> Write the action-item record you would send after the meeting.</p>\n<h4>Solution</h4>\n<ul><li>Identify the buckets:</li><li>\"let's park React and go with plain HTML for the pilot\" → <strong>Decision</strong> (note the word \"let's\" plus a change of plan — that is the decision marker).</li><li>\"Priya, can you get the hosting cost by Friday?\" → <strong>Action</strong>, owner Priya, deadline Friday.</li><li>\"Rahul, check whether the data has to stay in India\" → <strong>Action</strong>, owner Rahul, deadline not stated → must be filled by asking, not invented.</li><li>\"review again next Wednesday\" → <strong>Action for all</strong> / next checkpoint.</li><li>\"given the timeline\" → <strong>Context</strong> (the reason), worth one line.</li><li>Model record:</li></ul>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>Decisions\n1. Pilot will use plain HTML, not React (reason: timeline).\nActions\n1. Priya  - hosting cost estimate          - due Fri\n2. Rahul  - data-residency requirement     - due: not stated, confirm\n3. All    - review meeting                 - next Wednesday\nOpen question: does Rahul's check block the Dec demo?</code></pre>\n<div class=\"res\">one decision, three actions (one with an unstated deadline that must be confirmed rather than assumed), and one open question.</div>\n<p><strong>Why this works:</strong> it converts speech into <em>accountability</em>. Note especially the honest \"due: not stated\" — inventing a deadline is the single most damaging meeting-notes error, because someone will rely on it.</p>\n<div class=\"note note--trap\"><span class=\"tag\">Common trap</span><p>writing a narrative summary of the meeting (\"we discussed the front end and Priya will look into costs\") instead of owners and dates. Also: in a lecture, writing down the <em>example</em> and missing the <em>principle</em>. Examples are replaceable; principles are not.</p></div></div>"}, {"kind": "concept", "num": "1.5", "title": "Interactive and critical listening", "html": "<h4>Intuition</h4>\n<p>Interactive listening is a <strong>dance</strong>, not a lecture. If one person talks and the other only waits for their turn, that is not a conversation — that is two monologues sharing air. Critical listening is the other half: while you dance, you also keep asking silently, <em>\"is this true, and how would I know?\"</em></p>\n<p>The two combine in almost every important conversation you will have: a negotiation, a client call, a viva, a disagreement with a friend.</p>\n<h4>The idea, step by step</h4>\n<p><strong>Step 1 — Master turn-taking signals.</strong> English signals turns with words, not silence.</p>\n<div class=\"tbl-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Function</th><th>Phrases</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Take a turn</td><td>\"Can I add something here?\" / \"Just to build on that…\" / \"Sorry to jump in, but…\"</td></tr><tr><td>Hold your turn</td><td>\"There are two parts to this — let me finish the first.\" / \"Hang on, one more point.\"</td></tr><tr><td>Give a turn</td><td>\"What do you think?\" / \"Ravi, you've done this before.\"</td></tr><tr><td>End a turn</td><td>\"That's my view — what about you?\" (a falling tone helps)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><strong>Step 2 — Use back-channelling to show you are following.</strong> Short signals: <em>mm</em>, <em>right</em>, <em>yes</em>, <em>I see</em>, <em>exactly</em>, <em>really?</em>, <em>got it</em>. In Indian English, a head wobble does this job too; on a call, replace it with sound, because silence reads as \"disconnected\".</p>\n<p>Two warnings. (i) Over-back-channel and you sound impatient. (ii) Back-channel with the wrong intonation and you sound sarcastic — a flat \"right\" can mean \"I do not believe you\".</p>\n<p><strong>Step 3 — Paraphrase to check, not to show off.</strong> Formula: <em>\"So what you're saying is ___ — have I got that right?\"</em> Use it when (a) the content is technical, (b) numbers are involved, (c) the person seems upset, (d) you are about to disagree. Point (d) is the professional trick: paraphrase first, then disagree, and the disagreement lands as reasoning rather than attack.</p>\n<p><strong>Step 4 — Separate fact from opinion.</strong> Four quick tests:</p>\n<div class=\"tbl-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Test</th><th>Fact</th><th>Opinion</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Verifiable?</td><td>Yes, by evidence</td><td>No, by preference</td></tr><tr><td>Markers</td><td>\"measured\", \"according to\", \"in 2024\"</td><td>\"I think\", \"should\", \"best\", \"obviously\"</td></tr><tr><td>Can a sane person deny it?</td><td>Not really</td><td>Easily</td></tr><tr><td>Failure mode</td><td>Outdated, wrong source</td><td>Unstated assumption</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>Careful: \"fact\" does not mean \"true\". <em>\"The earth is 6000 years old\"</em> is presented as a fact and is false. So the question is not only \"fact or opinion?\" but \"<strong>claim or evidence?</strong>\"</p>\n<h4>Step 5 — Spot bias with five questions.</h4>\n<ol><li><strong>Selection</strong> — what is left out?</li><li><strong>Framing</strong> — \"tax relief\" vs \"cut to public services\" for the same policy.</li><li><strong>Source</strong> — who benefits if I believe this?</li><li><strong>Emotion</strong> — is the feeling doing the arguing? (loaded words, urgency, fear)</li><li><strong>False balance / false dilemma</strong> — only two options offered, or a fringe view given equal weight to a consensus.</li></ol>\n<p><strong>Step 6 — Defer judgement, not attention.</strong> Critical listening means <em>withholding</em> the verdict until the evidence arrives, while listening with full attention. Students often flip this: they decide in the first ten seconds and then listen only for support.</p>\n<div class=\"card\"><h4>Example question</h4><p>A classmate argues in a seminar: <em>\"Everybody knows online classes are useless. Last semester my cousin failed two subjects because he never attended properly, so obviously the whole system is a waste.\"</em> Write (a) your paraphrase-check line, (b) your fact/opinion/bias analysis, and (c) your spoken response.</p>\n<h4>Solution</h4>\n<ul><li>(a) Paraphrase-check: <em>\"So your point is that online classes didn't work for your cousin, and you're concluding they don't work in general — is that the argument?\"</em> Reason: it restates the <em>structure</em> (one case → general rule), which lets the speaker hear the leap themselves.</li><li>(b) Analysis:</li><li><em>\"Online classes are useless\"</em> — an <strong>opinion</strong> presented as a fact (\"everybody knows\" is a popularity appeal, not evidence).</li><li><em>\"My cousin failed two subjects\"</em> — a <strong>factual claim</strong>, probably true, but it is a <strong>single case</strong> (anecdote).</li><li><em>\"because he never attended properly\"</em> — an <strong>opinion</strong> about causation, with no evidence.</li><li><em>\"so obviously the whole system is a waste\"</em> — a <strong>hasty generalisation</strong>: n = 1 → all.</li><li>Bias present: <strong>selection bias</strong> (one relative), <strong>emotional framing</strong> (\"useless\", \"waste\"), <strong>false dilemma</strong> (online vs nothing, ignoring blended formats).</li><li>(c) Spoken response: <em>\"That's a fair experience to raise — one bad case is worth understanding. Two things I'd want before generalising: how many students actually failed, and whether the cause was the format or the attendance. If attendance was the issue, the same thing happens in offline classes. So maybe the claim is 'online needs better structure', not 'online is useless'.\"</em></li></ul>\n<div class=\"res\">paraphrase the structure, label the anecdote as a single case, name the hasty generalisation and the selection bias, and offer a narrower, defensible version of the claim.</div>\n<p><strong>Why this works:</strong> it disagrees with the <em>argument</em>, not the <em>person</em>, and it ends by giving the speaker a version of their point they can accept. That is what makes it a professional response rather than an argument.</p>\n<div class=\"note note--trap\"><span class=\"tag\">Common trap</span><p>treating critical listening as contradiction. If your response begins \"No, that's wrong\", you have stopped listening and started defending. The other trap: marking an opinion as a fact just because it is stated confidently.</p></div></div>"}, {"kind": "concept", "num": "1.6", "title": "Proficiency-test listening tasks", "html": "<h4>Intuition</h4>\n<p>Every proficiency test is a <strong>game with known tricks</strong>. The listening section does not test whether you understand English in general; it tests whether you can extract a specific type of information while the audio plays exactly once. Once you know the trap inside each task type, the section becomes much less frightening — and this is genuinely the highest-return hour of preparation in the whole subject.</p>\n<h4>The idea, step by step</h4>\n<h4>Step 1 — Know the four task types and what each really tests.</h4>\n<div class=\"tbl-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Task type</th><th>Format</th><th>What it tests</th><th>The trap</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Form / note completion</strong></td><td>Fill gaps in a form, table or set of notes</td><td>Catching specific details: names, numbers, dates, spellings</td><td>Word limit, spelling, and the speaker <strong>correcting</strong> themselves</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Multiple choice</strong></td><td>3 options per question</td><td>Understanding of gist and detail; rejecting distractors</td><td>All three options are mentioned; only one answers the <em>question</em></td></tr><tr><td><strong>Matching</strong></td><td>Match items to a list (people to opinions, items to places)</td><td>Tracking who said what across several speakers</td><td>Voice confusion — you match the wrong speaker</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Map / diagram labelling</strong></td><td>Label places on a plan</td><td>Spatial language: \"opposite\", \"just past\", \"second left\"</td><td>Orientation: the map's \"up\" is not the walker's \"forward\"</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><strong>Step 2 — Form completion rules.</strong> Read the gap and predict the <em>word class</em> before the audio: \"Room number: ___\" needs a number; \"Name of tutor: ___\" needs a proper noun, spelled out. Respect the word limit exactly — if it says \"NO MORE THAN TWO WORDS\", \"a red bicycle\" (3 words) scores zero even if it is correct in meaning. Copy spelling from the audio; if a name is spelled out (\"K-A-U-S-H-I-K\"), write the letters as they come.</p>\n<p><strong>Step 3 — MCQ rules.</strong> Underline the <em>question word</em> before listening (\"What does the speaker say about the <em>cost</em>?\"). The distractor pattern is nearly always: option A is mentioned early and rejected, option B is mentioned with a <em>but</em>, option C is the conclusion. So listen for <strong>contrast markers</strong> — <em>but, however, actually, in fact, on second thoughts</em>.</p>\n<p><strong>Step 4 — Matching rules.</strong> Before audio, note the speakers' names and, as each speaks, jot one keyword next to their name. Do not re-read the whole option list after every sentence; keep your eyes on the two or three likely options.</p>\n<p><strong>Step 5 — Map labelling rules.</strong> Find the <strong>entrance/start point</strong> first and physically trace with your finger or pencil. Learn the fixed set: <em>opposite, next to, adjacent, at the end of, just past, before you reach, on your left, across from, in the corner, halfway along</em>. Then trace the route in the same order as the audio.</p>\n<p><strong>Step 6 — Universal exam mechanics.</strong> (i) Read the questions in the pause <em>before</em> the audio — this is the whole game. (ii) Never leave a blank; a guess costs nothing. (iii) If you miss one answer, abandon it instantly and move your eyes to the next question — chasing a lost item loses two more. (iv) Transfer answers carefully if there is a separate answer sheet.</p>\n<div class=\"card\"><h4>Example question</h4><p>You hear: <em>\"The workshop will be held in the Willoughby Room — sorry, that's the Willoughby Annex, the smaller one next door — on Thursday the twelfth, no, let me check, Thursday the nineteenth of March. It runs from half past two until five, and you'll need to bring your own laptop. Oh, and there's no charge, though we do ask for a five-pound donation for materials.\"</em> Answer these note-completion items under the instruction <strong>NO MORE THAN TWO WORDS AND/OR A NUMBER</strong>: (i) Venue: ______ (ii) Date: ______ (iii) Start time: ______ (iv) Bring: ______ (v) Cost: ______</p>\n<h4>Solution</h4>\n<ul><li>(i) <strong>Willoughby Annex.</strong> Reason: the speaker self-corrects with \"sorry, that's…\"; the correction wins. \"Room\" would be the trap answer.</li><li>(ii) <strong>19 March</strong> (or \"the nineteenth of March\"). Reason: the speaker says \"the twelfth, no, let me check, the nineteenth\" — the later figure is the final one. Note the instruction allows a number, so \"19 March\" is safe; \"Thursday the nineteenth of March\" is 5 words and would break the limit.</li><li>(iii) <strong>2.30</strong> / \"half past two\". Reason: \"from half past two\" — the <em>start</em>, not the end (five o'clock is the trap).</li><li>(iv) <strong>laptop</strong> / \"own laptop\". Reason: \"bring your own laptop\" — the content word; \"your\" is redundant.</li><li>(v) <strong>five-pound donation</strong> — or, if the item is \"Cost: £____\", then <strong>5</strong>. Reason: \"no charge, though… donation\" is a contrast pair; the <em>real</em> cost to you is the donation. The trap is writing \"free\".</li></ul>\n<div class=\"res\">Willoughby Annex | 19 March | 2.30 | laptop | five-pound donation (or 5).</div>\n<p><strong>Why this works:</strong> every item here had a deliberate trap — a correction, a date change, a start-vs-end confusion, a redundant word, and a \"free but not free\" contrast. Naming the trap is what makes the answer reliable under exam pressure.</p>\n<div class=\"note note--trap\"><span class=\"tag\">Common trap</span><p>writing the first number or name you hear. In listening tests the <em>last</em> stated value is almost always the answer, and the earlier one is the designed distractor. Second trap: exceeding the word limit. Third: losing your place because you kept thinking about a missed question.</p></div></div>"}, {"kind": "concept", "num": "1.7", "title": "AI-assisted practice and self-assessment", "html": "<h4>Intuition</h4>\n<p>The old problem with listening practice was that you could not check yourself. You listened, you thought you understood, and there was no way to know. Transcripts and AI tools solve exactly that: they let you <strong>see what you missed</strong>, which is the only information that actually improves you.</p>\n<p>But there is a catch worth saying out loud: an AI transcript is a <em>tool output</em>, not a teacher. It can mis-transcribe accents, it can be confidently wrong, and it will not know your syllabus. Use it for feedback, verify anything that matters.</p>\n<h4>The idea, step by step</h4>\n<p><strong>Step 1 — Use the four-step transcript cycle.</strong> This is the core drill; do it with a 2-minute clip.</p>\n<ol><li><strong>Listen blind</strong> once at normal speed. Write down everything you can (gist plus numbers).</li><li><strong>Listen again</strong> and add to your notes. Now compare with the transcript, marking every word you missed in one colour and every word you misheard in another.</li><li><strong>Classify your misses.</strong> Almost all of them fall into four buckets: <em>unknown word</em>, <em>connected speech</em> (words run together, e.g. \"an apple\" sounding like \"a napple\"), <em>accent feature</em> (vowel or consonant difference), <em>speed</em>. The bucket tells you the fix.</li><li><strong>Shadow</strong> the clip: play a phrase, pause, and repeat it aloud imitating the rhythm — not word by word, but in chunks with the same stress. Shadowing trains your ear and your mouth together, which is why it works better than passive re-listening.</li></ol>\n<p><strong>Step 2 — Use speed control deliberately.</strong> Start at 0.75× only to <em>decode</em> a hard passage. Then go back to 1.0×, and finally 1.25× for the same passage. If you can follow at 1.25×, the real exam at 1.0× feels generous. Do not live at 0.75× — that trains you for a world that does not exist.</p>\n<p><strong>Step 3 — Ask an AI tool for targeted feedback, not general help.</strong> Weak prompt: \"help me improve listening\". Strong prompt: \"Here is my transcript of a 2-minute talk and here is the correct transcript. List my errors by type — vocabulary, connected speech, numbers, or accent — and give me three similar sentences to practise.\"</p>\n<p><strong>Step 4 — Self-assess weekly with a fixed checklist.</strong> Score each item 1–5, once a week, on the same kind of audio, so the numbers are comparable:</p>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>Listening self-assessment (score 1 = never, 5 = always)\n1. I caught the main idea on one hearing, without pausing.\n2. I caught all numbers, names and dates correctly.\n3. I could follow a speaker with an unfamiliar accent.\n4. I wrote notes that I could still understand 3 days later.\n5. I noticed when a speaker corrected themselves.\n6. I could tell a fact from an opinion in what I heard.\n7. I asked a checking question instead of pretending to understand.</code></pre>\n<p><strong>Step 5 — Read the pattern, not the average.</strong> A total of 22/35 tells you nothing useful. Item 2 scoring 2 and item 6 scoring 4 tells you exactly what to practise this week: numbers and names, via dictation drills.</p>\n<p><strong>Step 6 — Keep a 20-minute daily loop.</strong> 10 minutes new audio, 5 minutes transcript comparison, 5 minutes shadowing. Consistency beats marathon sessions; listening improves through frequency, not duration.</p>\n<div class=\"card\"><h4>Example question</h4><p>After a week of practice, your checklist reads: 4, 2, 2, 4, 3, 3, 2. Diagnose your listening profile, name the two highest-priority drills, and write the exact AI prompt you would use for the next session.</p>\n<h4>Solution</h4>\n<ul><li>Read the scores item by item:</li><li>Item 1 = 4 → gist is fine. You are not \"bad at listening\".</li><li>Items 2 and 3 = 2 → <strong>detail capture</strong> (numbers, names, dates) and <strong>unfamiliar accents</strong> are the weak points.</li><li>Item 4 = 4 → your note-taking system is working; do not change it.</li><li>Item 5 = 3 → you sometimes miss self-corrections; this is a sub-case of detail capture.</li><li>Item 6 = 3 → critical listening is developing but not automatic.</li><li>Item 7 = 2 → you are still pretending to understand rather than asking. This is the one that costs marks in vivas and interviews.</li><li>Priorities: (1) <strong>number and name dictation</strong> — because items 2 and 5 share a root cause; (2) <strong>accent exposure</strong> — one non-Indian variety per week.</li><li>Model AI prompt: <em>\"Give me ten short audio-style dictation items in British and Australian English, each containing one number, one name spelled aloud, and one self-correction (e.g. 'on Tuesday — sorry, Wednesday'). Do not show the text until I ask. Then check my transcription and tell me which type of error each mistake was.\"</em></li></ul>\n<div class=\"res\">strong gist and note-taking; weak detail capture, accent flexibility and checking behaviour; drills = dictation of numbers/names with self-corrections, plus weekly accent rotation; and the prompt above.</div>\n<p><strong>Why this works:</strong> it turns a vague feeling (\"my listening is average\") into two specific drills, and it uses the checklist's <em>pattern</em> rather than its average. That is the whole point of self-assessment.</p>\n<div class=\"note note--trap\"><span class=\"tag\">Common trap</span><p>re-listening to the same easy clip until it feels comfortable. Comfort is not progress; the transcript comparison step — where you confront what you missed — is where the improvement happens. Second trap: trusting an AI transcript of a strong accent as ground truth. Third: scoring yourself generously, which makes the numbers useless.</p></div></div>"}, {"kind": "formula", "num": "", "title": "Formulas, rules and properties", "html": "<p>There are no formulas in this module, so this table is a \"which technique when\" guide instead.</p>\n<div class=\"tbl-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Situation</th><th>Use this</th><th>One-line rule</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Exam-relevant content is being spoken</td><td>Comprehensive listening + Cornell notes</td><td>Structure first, details second</td></tr><tr><td>Only a name/number/date matters</td><td>Selective listening</td><td>Grab the value, ignore the commentary</td></tr><tr><td>Someone is upset</td><td>Empathetic listening</td><td>Reflect feeling, do not solve</td></tr><tr><td>A claim is being made</td><td>Critical listening</td><td>Ask: says who, compared to what, what is left out?</td></tr><tr><td>Speaker's accent is unfamiliar</td><td>Prediction + paraphrase-check</td><td>Widen the shape set, confirm by restating</td></tr><tr><td>Speaker is fast</td><td>Selective + signposts</td><td>Nouns and numbers survive speed</td></tr><tr><td>Meeting</td><td>Four buckets: decision / action / concern / context</td><td>Convert speech into owners and dates</td></tr><tr><td>Instructions</td><td>Listen, note, <strong>read back</strong></td><td>Never trust a one-time spoken instruction</td></tr><tr><td>Test form completion</td><td>Predict word class, respect word limit</td><td>The last value stated is the answer</td></tr><tr><td>Test MCQ</td><td>Underline the question word</td><td>Distractors are mentioned and then rejected</td></tr><tr><td>Test map labelling</td><td>Find the start point, trace with a finger</td><td>Orientation beats vocabulary</td></tr><tr><td>Improving between classes</td><td>Transcript comparison + shadowing</td><td>20 minutes daily, not 3 hours weekly</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"}, {"kind": "example", "num": "", "title": "Solved examples", "html": "<p><strong>Example 1 (easy) — identify the listening type.</strong> <em>Situation:</em> At the railway station, you hear a long announcement about a train's route, coaches and pantry service. You only need your platform number. <em>Step 1:</em> Purpose = to inform, but only one item is needed → <strong>selective</strong>. <em>Step 2:</em> What to ignore: route, coach position, pantry. What to catch: \"platform\", a digit. <em>Step 3:</em> If you miss it, the fallback is the display board, not a re-announcement. <strong>Final answer:</strong> selective listening; catch the platform number only.</p>\n<p><strong>Example 2 (easy–medium) — fact or opinion.</strong> <em>Sentence:</em> \"According to the 2024 placement report, 78% of CSE students were placed by March.\" <em>Step 1:</em> Is it verifiable? Yes — a named report and a figure → <strong>factual claim</strong>. <em>Step 2:</em> Is it necessarily <em>true</em>? No — the report could be wrong or the base could be small. So: factual in form, still worth checking. <em>Step 3:</em> Contrast with \"Placements are excellent this year\", which is an <strong>opinion</strong> (evaluative word \"excellent\", no measure). <strong>Final answer:</strong> a factual claim with a source, not an opinion — but \"factual\" ≠ \"verified\".</p>\n<p><strong>Example 3 (medium) — Cornell page from a fast lecture segment.</strong> <em>Audio:</em> \"Two points about pronunciation. One, English spelling is a poor guide to sound — consider 'through', 'though', 'tough'. Two, stress can change meaning, not just sound: 'REcord' the noun versus 'reCORD' the verb. Practise the pairs, not the words.\" <em>Step 1:</em> Structure signal \"two points\" → number the page 1, 2. <em>Step 2:</em> Point 1 = spelling ≠ sound; examples <em>through / though / tough</em>. <em>Step 3:</em> Point 2 = stress changes meaning; example REcord (n) vs reCORD (v). <em>Step 4:</em> Instruction = practise pairs, not isolated words → this is a \"how to study\" line, easy to miss and often examined. <em>Step 5:</em> Cue column: \"Why is spelling unreliable?\", \"Does stress change meaning?\", \"How should I practise?\" <strong>Final answer:</strong> a two-point page with the example trio, the noun/verb stress pair, and the study instruction recorded — plus three cue questions.</p>\n<p><strong>Example 4 (exam level) — full analysis.</strong> <em>Task:</em> A speaker says: <em>\"Frankly, no serious engineer uses that framework anymore. I've seen maybe two projects in five years. Anyone recommending it is out of touch.\"</em> Analyse this as a critical listener, and write a response that would score well in a GD. <em>Step 1:</em> Separate claims. (a) \"no serious engineer uses it\" — absolute claim. (b) \"two projects in five years\" — personal experience, factual in form. (c) \"anyone recommending it is out of touch\" — personal attack (ad hominem). <em>Step 2:</em> Test the evidence. Claim (b) is n = 1 observer with no sampling method → weak support for the absolute in (a). \"Serious\" is undefined, so (a) is unfalsifiable as stated. <em>Step 3:</em> Name the fallacies. <strong>Hasty generalisation</strong> (small sample → \"no serious engineer\"), <strong>appeal to the speaker's own authority</strong> (\"I've seen\"), <strong>ad hominem</strong> (attacking the recommender), <strong>loaded language</strong> (\"frankly\", \"out of touch\"). <em>Step 4:</em> Find the defensible core. There may be a real point: adoption has fallen, or the framework has known limitations. Steel-man it: \"it's less used in production than it was\". <em>Step 5:</em> Write the GD response: <em>\"There's something in that — adoption has definitely dropped, and I'd trust your field experience on that. Where I'd push back is the word 'no serious engineer'. Two projects in five years is one data point, and a lot of teams use older tools because of legacy constraints, not ignorance. So maybe the honest version is: it's declining, and it's a poor default choice for new projects. That we could probably agree on.\"</em> <strong>Final answer:</strong> two unsupported generalisations plus one ad hominem; the defensible core is \"declining adoption\"; the model response concedes the core, challenges the absolute, and offers a narrower claim.</p>"}, {"kind": "trap", "num": "", "title": "Common traps and mistakes", "html": "<ul><li><strong>Writing a transcript instead of notes.</strong> Why it costs marks: you have no time left to listen, and no structure to revise from. Fix: phrases, arrows, numbers.</li><li><strong>Naming a listening type without justifying it.</strong> In an exam, \"it is active listening\" earns half; \"because the listener must respond in real time to instructions\" earns full.</li><li><strong>Calling Indian English \"wrong English\".</strong> Lose the whole answer. It is a recognised variety; the errors are intelligibility problems and grammar slips, not the accent itself.</li><li><strong>Claiming English is <em>strictly</em> stress-timed and Hindi <em>strictly</em> syllable-timed.</strong> Say \"usually described as\" — the strong version is debated by phoneticians.</li><li><strong>Inventing a deadline or an owner in meeting notes.</strong> Someone will act on it. Write \"not stated\" and follow up.</li><li><strong>Confusing critical listening with contradiction.</strong> If your first response is \"no\", you have stopped listening. Paraphrase, then disagree.</li><li><strong>Treating \"fact\" as \"true\".</strong> A confident statement can be a false factual claim. Distinguish claim from evidence.</li><li><strong>In form completion, ignoring the word limit or spelling.</strong> Both are marked mechanically; a correct answer with 3 words scores zero.</li><li><strong>Writing the first number/name you hear.</strong> Speakers correct themselves; the final value is the answer.</li><li><strong>Chasing a missed question.</strong> You lose the next two. Abandon and rejoin.</li><li><strong>Passive listening in a comprehensive-listening situation</strong> (e.g. nodding through a lecture you will be examined on). The mistake is the mismatch, not the type.</li><li><strong>Skipping the 24-hour summary step in Cornell notes.</strong> Notes without a summary are storage, not learning.</li><li><strong>Trusting an AI transcript of a heavy accent as ground truth.</strong> Verify anything that matters.</li><li><strong>Scoring your own self-assessment generously.</strong> The number becomes useless and you practise the wrong thing.</li></ul>"}, {"kind": "revision", "num": "", "title": "Quick revision box", "html": "<ul><li><strong>Hearing</strong> is physiological; <strong>listening</strong> is psychological, active and trainable. Listening is prediction, not just reception.</li><li>Purposes: <strong>inform, evaluate, empathise, enjoy</strong>. Types: active, passive, selective, empathetic, comprehensive, critical, appreciative. Purpose decides type — and type can change within one hour.</li><li>Accents differ mainly by <strong>rhythm type</strong> (stress- vs syllable-timed), <strong>vowel shifts</strong>, and <strong>consonant habits</strong>. Indian English is a legitimate variety; you adapt for clarity, you do not erase it.</li><li>Repair an unfamiliar accent with <strong>prediction, paraphrase-checks and specific questions</strong> (\"Tuesday or Thursday?\"), never a bare \"sorry?\".</li><li><strong>Cornell notes</strong>: cue column + notes area + summary within 24 hours. Listen for signposts (\"there are three types\", \"however\", \"this is important\", \"actually\").</li><li><strong>Lectures = content; meetings = commitments.</strong> Four buckets: decision, action, concern, context. Read instructions back.</li><li><strong>Interactive listening</strong>: turn-taking phrases, back-channelling, paraphrase-to-check. <strong>Critical listening</strong>: claim vs evidence, selection, framing, source, emotion, false dilemma.</li><li>Test traps: <strong>word limits and spelling</strong> (form completion), <strong>mentioned-then-rejected distractors</strong> (MCQ), <strong>speaker confusion</strong> (matching), <strong>orientation</strong> (map). The last value stated wins.</li><li>Practise with the <strong>transcript cycle + shadowing + speed control</strong> (0.75× to decode, 1.25× to stretch). Self-assess on the <em>pattern</em> of scores, not the average.</li></ul>"}], "practice": [{"q": "For each situation, name the listening type and one reason: (a) a friend tells you about a family problem, (b) a station announcement for your platform, (c) a lecture on the topics in the syllabus, (d) a political campaign speech you must vote on.", "a": "<p>(a) Empathetic — the aim is to share feeling, not to fix the problem. (b) Selective — only the platform number is needed. (c) Comprehensive — everything is potentially examinable, so retention matters. (d) Critical — you must judge the truth of claims and detect bias before acting on them.</p>", "tag": "easy", "qHtml": "For each situation, name the listening type and one reason: (a) a friend tells you about a family problem, (b) a station announcement for your platform, (c) a lecture on the topics in the syllabus, (d) a political campaign speech you must vote on."}, {"q": "Rewrite these as proper Cornell notes-area entries using at most 12 words each and at least one abbreviation: (a) \"The most important thing to remember is that intonation can change a statement into a question.\" (b) \"There are four components of speaking proficiency that examiners look at, and the first one is fluency.\"", "a": "<p>Key step: compress the <em>words</em>, keep the <em>idea</em> and the emphasis marker. (a) <code> Intonation can turn a statement → question. (key pt)</code> (b) <code>4 components of speaking prof.: 1. fluency (first one examiners check)</code> Both keep the number, the ordering and the \"important\" signal; neither keeps the original sentence structure.</p>", "tag": "medium", "qHtml": "Rewrite these as proper Cornell notes-area entries using at most 12 words each and at least one abbreviation: (a) \"The most important thing to remember is that intonation can change a statement into a question.\" (b) \"There are four components of speaking proficiency that examiners look at, and the first one is fluency.\""}, {"q": "A colleague on a call says: \"We'll push the release to the twenty-second — actually, make it the twenty-fifth, because QA needs the weekend.\" Write (i) the value you record, (ii) the reason, (iii) your confirming line, and (iv) why a bare \"okay\" is a bad response.", "a": "<p>(i) <strong>25th</strong> — the last stated value. (ii) Because \"actually, make it the twenty-fifth\" is a self-correction, and QA needing the weekend is the reason, not a new date. (iii) <em>\"So the 25th, because QA needs the weekend — correct?\"</em> (iv) A bare \"okay\" confirms nothing; if the speaker misspoke or you misheard, the error surfaces only at the deadline. The check costs three seconds.</p>", "tag": "medium", "qHtml": "A colleague on a call says: <em>\"We'll push the release to the twenty-second — actually, make it the twenty-fifth, because QA needs the weekend.\"</em> Write (i) the value you record, (ii) the reason, (iii) your confirming line, and (iv) why a bare \"okay\" is a bad response."}, {"q": "Identify the fallacy or bias in each: (a) \"Nine out of ten students in my hostel hate the new timetable, so the whole college hates it.\" (b) \"Either we ban mobile phones in class or we accept that no teaching happens.\" (c) \"This app is trusted by doctors\" (said by the app's own advertisement).", "a": "<p>(a) Hasty generalisation from a hostel sample (plus selection bias — hostel residents are not the college). (b) False dilemma — there are middle options (phones in a box, permitted for polls, etc.). (c) Appeal to authority with a <strong>conflict of interest</strong> — the source benefits from belief. (Also note it never says <em>what</em> the doctors trust it for.)</p>", "tag": "medium", "qHtml": "Identify the fallacy or bias in each: (a) \"Nine out of ten students in my hostel hate the new timetable, so the whole college hates it.\" (b) \"Either we ban mobile phones in class or we accept that no teaching happens.\" (c) \"This app is trusted by doctors\" (said by the app's own advertisement)."}, {"q": "You are asked to write a 150-word guidance note for juniors titled \"How to survive a fast lecturer with a strong accent\". It must use at least four techniques from this module and name them.", "a": "<p>Model answer (about 140 words):</p>\n<div class=\"note note--why\"><span class=\"tag\">Note</span><p>Four techniques, in order of usefulness. <strong>First, use selective listening on purpose:</strong> let the grammar go and catch the content words — nouns, verbs, numbers. Speed destroys grammar first. <strong>Second, listen for signposts:</strong> \"there are three types\", \"however\", \"this is important\", \"actually\" (which signals a correction — the corrected value is the answer). These give you the skeleton even when the flesh is lost. <strong>Third, keep a Cornell page:</strong> number your points the moment a number is said, star anything that sounds examinable, and write a three-line summary within 24 hours. <strong>Fourth, repair, don't panic:</strong> instead of \"sorry?\", ask a specific question — \"was that Tuesday or Thursday?\" — or paraphrase and check: \"so the deadline is the 12th, right?\" That gives the speaker a yes/no to answer. Practise 20 minutes daily with a transcript and shadowing; frequency beats marathon sessions.</p></div>", "tag": "exam", "qHtml": "You are asked to write a 150-word guidance note for juniors titled \"How to survive a fast lecturer with a strong accent\". It must use at least four techniques from this module and name them."}, {"q": "A meeting produces: \"So we'll drop the third module — no, keep it but make it optional; Anil, get the syllabus revised by Monday; and someone should talk to the exam cell about the lab slots.\" Write the action record, flag anything unstated, and name two things a weak note-taker would get wrong here.", "a": "<p>Action record:</p>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>Decisions\n1. Module 3 retained but made optional.   (corrected from \"drop\")\nActions\n1. Anil  - revised syllabus        - Monday\n2. ?     - speak to exam cell      - owner not stated\n3. All   - lab slots query         - deadline not stated</code></pre>\n<p>Unstated: who contacts the exam cell, and by when; also whether the optional module affects assessment. A weak note-taker would (i) record \"module 3 dropped\" — the self-correction \"no, keep it but make it optional\" reverses it — and (ii) write \"someone will talk to the exam cell\", which is not an action item because it has no owner.</p>", "tag": "exam", "qHtml": "A meeting produces: <em>\"So we'll drop the third module — no, keep it but make it optional; Anil, get the syllabus revised by Monday; and someone should talk to the exam cell about the lab slots.\"</em> Write the action record, flag anything unstated, and name two things a weak note-taker would get wrong here."}], "file": "Semester_2/CSFE-CommunicationSkills/CSFE-M01.md"}, {"n": 2, "title": "Speaking (CO2)", "tagline": "Speaking is the skill that punishes perfectionism. In writing you can stare at a sentence for five minutes; in speech you have about a quarter of a…", "minutes": 53, "sections": [{"kind": "big", "num": "", "title": "Big picture, 30 seconds", "html": "<p>Speaking is the skill that punishes perfectionism. In writing you can stare at a sentence for five minutes; in speech you have about a quarter of a second before silence becomes awkward. So good speakers are not people who never make mistakes — they are people whose <strong>mistakes are cheap and rare enough not to matter</strong>, and who keep the listener comfortable while they happen.</p>\n<p>Here is the analogy for this module. Think of a <strong>bus ride</strong>. Fluency is the bus moving — a steady speed, no long stops. Accuracy is staying in your lane. Range is having more than one route. Pronunciation is the tyres: nobody praises them, but everybody notices when they are flat. Coherence is the destination board: the listener must know where you are going. Interaction is checking that passengers are still on board. A bus that stops every ten seconds, or drives beautifully to the wrong town, is a bad bus.</p>\n<p>Where you will use this: a 2-minute answer in an interview, a group discussion with eleven other candidates, a lab viva where you must explain your code aloud, a project demo to a client, an IELTS speaking test, and the ordinary daily act of explaining an idea to a friend without confusing them.</p>\n<p>One thing to believe from the start: <strong>speaking improves by speaking, with feedback.</strong> Reading about speaking improves your knowledge of speaking, which is not the same thing. That is why every sub-topic here ends with a script you should say out loud, not just read.</p>"}, {"kind": "basics", "num": "", "title": "Basics you must know first", "html": "<ul><li><strong>Fluency</strong> — the ability to keep going at a reasonable speed with few long pauses; it is about <em>flow</em>, not speed.</li><li><strong>Accuracy</strong> — correct grammar, word choice and pronunciation.</li><li><strong>Register</strong> — the formality level suited to the situation. \"Give me the file\" vs \"Could you share the file, please?\"</li><li><strong>Stress</strong> — the emphasis on one syllable in a word, or one word in a sentence.</li><li><strong>Intonation</strong> — the rise and fall of pitch across a sentence, which carries meaning and attitude.</li><li><strong>Chunk</strong> — a group of words spoken as one unit, e.g. \"at the end of the day\" — the natural unit of fluent speech.</li><li><strong>Cue card</strong> — the prompt card in tests like IELTS Speaking Part 2 that gives a topic and bullet points for a 1–2 minute talk.</li></ul>"}, {"kind": "concept", "num": "2.1", "title": "Components of speaking proficiency", "html": "<h4>Intuition</h4>\n<p>Ask a student \"is your English good?\" and you usually get a shrug, because \"good\" is not one thing. It is at least six things, and they are partly independent. You have met the person with perfect grammar who cannot hold a conversation, and the person with shaky grammar whom everyone understands effortlessly and enjoys listening to.</p>\n<p>So the first move in improving your speaking is <strong>diagnosis</strong>: which of the six is actually weak for you? Fixing pronunciation when your real problem is coherence is wasted effort.</p>\n<h4>The idea, step by step</h4>\n<h4>Step 1 — Learn the six components.</h4>\n<div class=\"tbl-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Component</th><th>What it means</th><th>Weak version sounds like</th><th>Strong version sounds like</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Fluency</strong></td><td>Steady flow, few long pauses, few restarts</td><td>\"I was… I mean… actually… so… the thing is…\"</td><td>\"I'd split it into two parts — the cost, and the timeline.\"</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Accuracy</strong></td><td>Correct grammar and word choice</td><td>\"He don't have any idea about it.\"</td><td>\"He doesn't have any idea about it.\"</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Range</strong></td><td>Variety of vocabulary and structures</td><td>\"It was good. The food was good. The place was good.\"</td><td>\"The food was excellent, the place was spotless, and the staff were unusually patient.\"</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Pronunciation</strong></td><td>Individual sounds, word stress, intelligibility</td><td>\"I want to <em>wan</em>/<em>van</em> it\" (v/w merged), flat stress</td><td>Clear vowels, correct stress in <em>deVELop</em></td></tr><tr><td><strong>Coherence</strong></td><td>Logical order, linking, clear structure</td><td>Jumps between ideas, no signposting</td><td>\"First… which brings me to… so overall…\"</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Interaction</strong></td><td>Turn-taking, responding, checking</td><td>Talks over people, or goes silent</td><td>\"That's a fair point — can I add one thing?\"</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><strong>Step 2 — Notice that examiners score them separately.</strong> IELTS bands fluency and coherence together, lexical resource, grammatical range and accuracy, and pronunciation. A GD panel scores content, communication, and group behaviour. So a single weakness does not sink you — a <em>wide</em> weakness does. Know your profile.</p>\n<p><strong>Step 3 — Diagnose yourself with a 2-minute recording.</strong> Speak for two minutes on \"a skill I want to learn\". Then count: (i) long pauses over 2 seconds, (ii) grammar errors you can hear, (iii) number of different adjectives, (iv) whether you had a structure. Each count maps to one component.</p>\n<p><strong>Step 4 — Fix in the right order.</strong> For most Indian first-year students the highest-return order is: <strong>coherence → fluency → range → pronunciation polish</strong>. Reason: a listener forgives a wrong vowel far more easily than they forgive not knowing what you are saying. Students usually do the opposite and obsess over accent.</p>\n<p><strong>Step 5 — Understand the trade-off.</strong> Fluency and accuracy compete for attention. Under pressure, most people sacrifice one. Beginners freeze trying to be accurate; overconfident speakers become fluent and wrong. The professional balance: <strong>be accurate on the words that carry meaning</strong> (numbers, names, technical terms, verbs of obligation) and let small slips go.</p>\n<div class=\"card\"><h4>Example question</h4><p>A mock-interview recording of a student: <em>\"Actually I am… I am from… my background is basically… I have done my schooling from Delhi, and… like… I am interested in coding since… since school time. So yeah, that's… that's about it.\"</em> Identify which components are weak, which are relatively fine, and give one concrete fix per weak component.</p>\n<h4>Solution</h4>\n<ul><li><strong>Fluency — weak.</strong> Evidence: three restarts (\"I am… I am\"), filler \"like\", and an abandoned ending (\"that's… that's about it\"). Reason: the student is composing sentence-by-sentence instead of using ready-made chunks.</li><li><strong>Accuracy — mildly weak.</strong> \"I am interested in coding since school time\" should be \"I have been interested in coding since school\" (present perfect with \"since\"). \"I have done my schooling from Delhi\" → \"I did my schooling in Delhi\" is more natural; \"from\" is a common Indian-English transfer.</li><li><strong>Range — weak.</strong> \"basically\", \"like\", \"so yeah\" fill space without adding meaning; no specific noun or number anywhere.</li><li><strong>Pronunciation — not assessable from a transcript</strong>, and this is worth saying in an exam: you cannot judge sounds from written text. Do not invent a judgement.</li><li><strong>Coherence — weak.</strong> The order is schooling → interest → stop. There is no point, no example, no ending.</li><li><strong>Interaction — not testable here</strong> (monologue with no interlocutor).</li><li>Fixes:</li><li>Fluency: memorise five opening chunks (\"I'm a second-year CSE student at…\", \"The reason I'm interested in…\", \"A quick example…\"). Chunks remove the composing pause.</li><li>Accuracy: drill the \"since/for\" pair — <em>since</em> + point in time, <em>for</em> + duration.</li><li>Range: replace each filler with a specific — instead of \"interested in coding\", say \"I built a attendance-tracking script in Python last semester\".</li><li>Coherence: use point–example–point (see 2.3): \"I'll answer in two parts — my background, then why coding.\"</li></ul>\n<div class=\"res\">weak in fluency, range and coherence; mildly weak in accuracy (since/for, \"schooling from\"); pronunciation and interaction not assessable from a transcript.</div>\n<p><strong>Why this works:</strong> the answer separates components and refuses to guess at the ones the evidence cannot support. That last habit is what distinguishes a good analysis from a confident-sounding one.</p>\n<div class=\"note note--trap\"><span class=\"tag\">Common trap</span><p>saying \"his English is bad\" as a single verdict. Marks come from naming components and giving evidence per component. Second trap: judging pronunciation from a written transcript — you cannot.</p></div></div>"}, {"kind": "concept", "num": "2.2", "title": "Pronunciation, stress, rhythm and intonation", "html": "<h4>Intuition</h4>\n<p>Pronunciation is not about sounding British. It is about <strong>not making the listener work</strong>. If someone has to ask \"sorry, did you say <em>van</em> or <em>wan</em>?\", the conversation has already lost three seconds. That is the whole target: zero repair requests.</p>\n<p>Think of a word as a <strong>train with one loud coach</strong>. The loud coach is the stressed syllable; the other coaches are quieter and often shortened. If you make every coach equally loud (which syllable-timed habits encourage), the listener cannot tell which coach matters — and in English, the loud coach often carries the meaning.</p>\n<h4>The idea, step by step</h4>\n<p><strong>Step 1 — Fix the five sound problems that actually cause miscommunication for Indian speakers.</strong> These are the ones worth drilling; ignore the rest for now.</p>\n<div class=\"tbl-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Problem</th><th>What happens</th><th>Minimal pairs to drill</th><th>How to fix</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>/v/ vs /w/</strong></td><td>Both become a soft labiodental approximant, so <em>vest/west</em> merge</td><td>vest–west, vine–wine, veil–wail, verse–worse</td><td>/v/: top teeth touch bottom lip, and it buzzes. /w/: lips rounded, teeth nowhere near the lip</td></tr><tr><td><strong>/θ/ /ð/</strong> (\"th\")</td><td>Becomes dental /t/ or /d/, so <em>thin/tin</em>, <em>then/den</em> merge</td><td>thin–tin, thick–tick, then–den, breathe–breed</td><td>Tongue tip lightly between the teeth, air escaping; /ð/ adds voice</td></tr><tr><td><strong>/s/ vs /ʃ/</strong> (\"s\" vs \"sh\")</td><td>Confusion in spelling-driven words: <em>sugar</em>, <em>sure</em>, <em>measure</em></td><td>sue–shoe, see–she, sort–short, mass–mash</td><td>/ʃ/: lips slightly forward, tongue a bit further back; practise <em>sh</em> in <em>ship</em></td></tr><tr><td><strong>/ʒ/</strong> as in <em>measure, vision</em></td><td>Often replaced by /dʒ/ or /ʃ/</td><td>vision–vishun (avoid), pleasure, garage</td><td>Rare sound; treat word by word, not by rule</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Aspiration of /p t k/</strong></td><td>Word-initial <em>pin/pat/cap</em> may lack the puff of air, sounding like /b d g/ to some listeners</td><td>pin–bin, tin–din, cap–gap</td><td>Hold a paper in front of your mouth: it should flutter on initial p/t/k in a stressed syllable</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Retroflex /t/ /d/</strong></td><td>Indian English uses retroflex (tongue curled back); English /t d/ are alveolar (tongue behind the ridge)</td><td>—</td><td>Move the tongue tip forward to the ridge behind the upper teeth; not a \"fault\", but it affects intelligibility abroad</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>Two honest notes. (i) Retroflex /t/ and /d/ is a <strong>feature of Indian English, not an error</strong> — you change it for international clarity, not because it is wrong. (ii) Aspiration is the trickiest to hear in your own speech; record yourself saying \"pen–Ben, ten–den, cap–gap\" and listen back.</p>\n<p><strong>Step 2 — Word stress: learn where the beat falls, and how it moves.</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tbl-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Word</th><th>Stress</th><th>Shift pattern</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>PHO-to-graph</td><td>1st</td><td>base form</td></tr><tr><td>pho-TO-graph-y</td><td>2nd</td><td>-y added, stress moves one right</td></tr><tr><td>pho-to-GRAPH-ic</td><td>3rd</td><td>-ic pulls stress to the syllable before it</td></tr><tr><td>e-CON-o-my</td><td>2nd</td><td>—</td></tr><tr><td>ec-o-NOM-ics</td><td>3rd</td><td>-ics pulls stress right</td></tr><tr><td>MED-icine</td><td>1st</td><td>—</td></tr><tr><td>me-DIC-in-al</td><td>2nd</td><td>-al shifts it</td></tr><tr><td>a-CAD-e-my</td><td>2nd</td><td>—</td></tr><tr><td>ac-a-DEM-ic</td><td>3rd</td><td>-ic again</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>Rules of thumb (useful, with exceptions): <strong>-ic, -tion, -sion, -ity</strong> pull the stress to the syllable just before them (<em>pho-to-GRAPH-ic</em>, <em>in-for-MA-tion</em>, <em>a-BIL-i-ty</em>). <strong>-graphy, -logy, -metry</strong> put it on the syllable before the ending (<em>pho-TO-graphy</em>, <em>bi-OL-ogy</em>, <em>ge-OM-etry</em>). Compound nouns stress the first element (<em>BLACKboard</em>, <em>SOFTware</em>); compound verbs the second (<em>un-DERstand</em>, <em>over-COME</em>).</p>\n<p><strong>Step 3 — Stress also changes the word class.</strong> This is a favourite exam item:</p>\n<div class=\"tbl-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Noun (stress 1st)</th><th>Verb (stress 2nd)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>RE-cord</td><td>re-CORD</td></tr><tr><td>PRE-sent</td><td>pre-SENT</td></tr><tr><td>OB-ject</td><td>ob-JECT</td></tr><tr><td>IN-crease</td><td>in-CREASE</td></tr><tr><td>PER-mit</td><td>per-MIT</td></tr><tr><td>CON-tract</td><td>con-TRACT</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><strong>Step 4 — Sentence stress: the content words get the beat.</strong> Function words (articles, prepositions, auxiliaries, pronouns) are weak and often reduced. So:</p>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>I WANT to GO to the MAR-ket to BUY some FRUIT.\n (weak)  (strong)   (weak)          (strong)     (weak)</code></pre>\n<p>Change <em>which</em> word is stressed and you change the meaning, with no new words:</p>\n<div class=\"tbl-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Sentence</th><th>Stress on</th><th>Implied meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>I didn't say he stole it</td><td><strong>I</strong></td><td>someone else said it</td></tr><tr><td>I didn't say he stole it</td><td><strong>say</strong></td><td>maybe I implied it</td></tr><tr><td>I didn't say he stole it</td><td><strong>he</strong></td><td>someone else stole it</td></tr><tr><td>I didn't say he stole it</td><td><strong>stole</strong></td><td>maybe he borrowed it</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>This is why flat stress feels \"unclear\" to listeners even when every sound is correct.</p>\n<h4>Step 5 — Intonation: two directions, five jobs.</h4>\n<div class=\"tbl-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Contour</th><th>Signals</th><th>Example</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Falling</strong> ↘</td><td>completeness, certainty, statement, wh-question</td><td>\"My name is Arjun.\" / \"Where do you live?\"</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Rising</strong> ↗</td><td>yes/no question, uncertainty, politeness, request for confirmation</td><td>\"Are you coming?\" / \"You're from Kerala?\"</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Fall–rise</strong> ↘↗</td><td>reservation, contrast, \"but…\"</td><td>\"It's <em>possible</em>…\" (implying: not likely)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Rise on each item, fall on the last</strong></td><td>list</td><td>\"We need paper ↗, pens ↗, and folders ↘.\"</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Flat / low</strong></td><td>boredom, annoyance, or (in some contexts) rudeness</td><td>\"Fine.\"</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>Two things that go wrong constantly in Indian classrooms: (i) wh-questions given a rising tone, which sounds uncertain — \"What is your NAME ↗?\" sounds like the speaker is unsure of the question; (ii) statements given a rise, which makes them sound like questions and undermines confidence. Fixing only these two makes a student sound noticeably more assured.</p>\n<p><strong>Step 6 — Rhythm: squeeze the weak syllables.</strong> English rhythm is usually described as stress-timed: stressed syllables land at roughly equal intervals and the unstressed ones get compressed. So <em>vegetable</em> is commonly three syllables in speech (VEJ-tuh-bul), <em>comfortable</em> is often KUMF-tuh-bul, and <em>interesting</em> is often IN-truh-sting. You do not have to imitate these; you do have to <strong>not</strong> give every syllable equal weight, or your English sounds machine-like and slow.</p>\n<p><strong>Step 7 — Drill, don't read.</strong> Ten minutes daily: minimal pairs aloud, one word-stress shift table, and one sentence said with stress on each different word.</p>\n<div class=\"card\"><h4>Example question</h4><p>(a) A student says: <em>\"I have to present the present to my teacher.\"</em> Explain why a listener may be confused, and how stress fixes it. (b) Mark the stress in <em>photography</em>, <em>economic</em>, and <em>permit</em> (verb). (c) Give the intonation contour for: \"You've finished the report ↗?\" and say what it signals.</p>\n<h4>Solution</h4>\n<ul><li>(a) The two words are spelled identically but differ in stress and word class. <strong>PRE-sent</strong> (noun: a gift) vs <strong>pre-SENT</strong> (verb: to show/give). If both are said with the same stress, the listener has to infer from grammar, which costs time and can fail. Correct production: <em>\"I have to preSENT the PREsent to my teacher.\"</em></li><li>(b) <em>photography</em> = pho-TOG-ra-phy → stress on the <strong>2nd</strong> syllable (the \"-tog-\" syllable). <em>economic</em> = e-co-NOM-ic → stress on the <strong>3rd</strong> (the \"-nom-\" syllable). <em>permit</em> (verb) = per-MIT → stress on the <strong>2nd</strong>.</li><li>(c) Rise ↗ on the final word, i.e. a <strong>yes/no question contour</strong>. It signals a genuine question expecting confirmation — and note the present perfect (\"you've finished\") makes it about a completed action with present relevance. If the speaker used a falling contour ↘, it would sound like a statement being asserted, or a mildly accusing remark.</li></ul>\n<div class=\"res\">(a) stress distinguishes noun PRE-sent from verb pre-SENT; (b) pho-TOG-ra-phy, e-co-NOM-ic, per-MIT; (c) rising, signalling a yes/no confirmation question.</div>\n<p><strong>Why this works:</strong> it links every sound-level fact to a <em>communicative consequence</em>. In an exam, \"because the listener may misunderstand which word class it is\" is a much stronger reason than \"because the stress is different\".</p>\n<div class=\"note note--trap\"><span class=\"tag\">Common trap</span><p>marking stress on the wrong syllable because you counted letters rather than syllables — <em>photography</em> has four syllables, so stress \"on the second\" is \"-tog-\", not \"-to-\". Second trap: writing that a rising tone is used for wh-questions. It is not; wh-questions normally fall. Third: describing Indian English retroflex /t/ as \"incorrect\" — it is a variety feature.</p></div></div>"}, {"kind": "concept", "num": "2.3", "title": "Structured and extended speaking", "html": "<h4>Intuition</h4>\n<p>An unstructured answer is like a cupboard emptied on the floor: everything is there, nothing is findable. Structure is what lets a listener <em>hold</em> your answer while you finish it. And the good news is that you do not need a new structure for each question — one pattern covers almost everything.</p>\n<p>The pattern is <strong>point → example → point</strong> (PEP): say what you think, show one concrete thing, then say what it means. It is the same shape as an essay paragraph, and it works in interviews, vivas, GDs and IELTS.</p>\n<h4>The idea, step by step</h4>\n<h4>Step 1 — Learn the three-line skeleton.</h4>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>POINT:    Answer the question in one sentence. (Topic sentence)\nEXAMPLE:  One specific instance, number, or story. (Evidence)\nPOINT:    So what? Link back, or draw the conclusion. (Landing)</code></pre>\n<p><strong>Step 2 — Add signposting so the listener can hear the structure.</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tbl-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Function</th><th>Phrases</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Announce structure</td><td>\"I'll answer in two parts…\" / \"There are two reasons — the first is…\"</td></tr><tr><td>Move on</td><td>\"That brings me to…\" / \"The second point is…\"</td></tr><tr><td>Give an example</td><td>\"For instance…\" / \"A quick example from last semester…\"</td></tr><tr><td>Contrast</td><td>\"Having said that…\" / \"The flip side is…\"</td></tr><tr><td>Buy time (legitimately)</td><td>\"That's a good question — let me think about the order.\"</td></tr><tr><td>Close</td><td>\"So overall…\" / \"Which is why I'd say…\"</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><strong>Step 3 — The 1-minute talk: three chunks of about 20 seconds.</strong> 20s point, 20s example, 20s landing. Do not attempt more than one idea.</p>\n<p><strong>Step 4 — The 2-minute talk: five chunks of about 24 seconds.</strong> Structure: <em>what → why it matters → one detailed example → a limitation or contrast → conclusion</em>. The fourth chunk is what separates a 7 from a 6 in IELTS: it shows you can see more than one side.</p>\n<p><strong>Step 5 — Learn a reusable frame for the cue card.</strong> The cue card gives bullet points; convert them into a route, not a checklist you must tick. For \"Describe a skill you want to learn\", the frame is: what it is → why you want it → what you have done so far → what is difficult about it → how you plan to learn it.</p>\n<p><strong>Step 6 — Never memorise a full script; memorise the frame and five chunks.</strong> A memorised script collapses the moment you forget one sentence, and examiners recognise recitation instantly. A frame survives.</p>\n<div class=\"card\"><h4>Example question</h4><p>Write the full script of a 60-second answer to the interview question: <em>\"Tell me about a time you had to learn something quickly.\"</em> Use PEP and signposting, and keep it to roughly 150–180 spoken words.</p>\n<h4>Solution</h4>\n<div class=\"note note--why\"><span class=\"tag\">Note</span><p><em>(POINT — 1 sentence, direct answer)</em> \"Yes — the clearest example was last semester, when I had to learn enough SQL to build a working database for our mini-project in about ten days.\"  <em>(SIGNPOST + EXAMPLE — the specific story, with detail)</em> \"I'd never written a query before. So I split it into three steps. First, two hours a day on the basics — select, where, joins — from one source only, because switching between five tutorials was my usual mistake. Second, I rebuilt the project tables myself instead of copying the schema our senior gave us, because I wanted the errors. Third, I made a list of every error message I hit, and by day six that list was my revision sheet.\"  <em>(POINT — the landing, what it means)</em> \"We submitted on time, and more importantly I can now debug a query instead of guessing. So what I actually learned was a method: shrink the scope, practise on the real problem, and keep an error log — not just SQL.\"</p></div>\n<div class=\"res\">roughly 170 words, three named steps, one number (\"ten days\", \"two hours a day\", \"day six\"), and a landing that generalises to a method rather than restating the story.</div>\n<p><strong>Why this works:</strong> the structure is <em>audible</em> — the listener hears \"the clearest example was\", \"first, second, third\", \"so what I actually learned\". Specific numbers make it credible without boasting. And the landing turns a small student project into evidence of a transferable habit, which is exactly what the interviewer is scoring.</p>\n<div class=\"note note--trap\"><span class=\"tag\">Common trap</span><p>answering with a <em>list of adjectives</em> (\"I am a fast learner, hardworking, sincere\") instead of one story. Adjectives are unfalsifiable and score nothing. Second trap: an example with no numbers or names — \"I learned it quickly and it went well\" tells the listener nothing. Third: running out of time mid-example because you spent 45 seconds on the point.</p></div></div>"}, {"kind": "concept", "num": "2.4", "title": "Fluency development", "html": "<h4>Intuition</h4>\n<p>Fluency is not a talent, and it is not \"thinking faster\". It is mostly <strong>retrieval speed</strong>: how quickly your brain can pull a ready-made phrase out of storage. A fluent speaker is not composing grammar in real time — they are assembling pre-built blocks.</p>\n<p>That is why the drills here are mechanical and slightly boring. You are not learning new English; you are moving English you already know from \"recognise\" to \"can say instantly\".</p>\n<h4>The idea, step by step</h4>\n<p><strong>Step 1 — Understand what a filler is doing.</strong> Fillers fall into two groups:</p>\n<div class=\"tbl-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Kind</th><th>Examples</th><th>Effect</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Harmless / natural</strong></td><td>\"well\", \"you know\", \"I mean\", short \"um\" at a thought boundary</td><td>Native-like; signals thinking, not ignorance</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Damaging</strong></td><td>\"like… like…\", repeated \"actually\", \"basically\" in every sentence, \"what I am saying is\" three times</td><td>Signals panic; the listener stops tracking content</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>Target: keep the first kind, delete the second. Total silence is not the goal — a 1-second pause at a boundary is better than a filler in the middle of a word.</p>\n<p><strong>Step 2 — Learn three pause strategies that are better than fillers.</strong></p>\n<ol><li><strong>The stall phrase:</strong> \"That's a good question — let me take that in two parts.\" (buys 3 seconds, sounds confident)</li><li><strong>The honest pause:</strong> stop, breathe, then speak. One second of silence reads as thoughtfulness; five fillers read as confusion.</li><li><strong>The rephrase-instead-of-restart:</strong> if you get stuck mid-sentence, finish it roughly and then add \"Let me put that more clearly —\". Restarting from zero is what destroys fluency scores.</li></ol>\n<p><strong>Step 3 — Use the 4/3/2 technique (Paul Nation's drill).</strong> This is the single most efficient fluency exercise known, and it is free.</p>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>1. Pick a topic you can talk about (a project, your hometown, a hobby).\n2. Talk for 4 minutes to a partner/listener/recorder.\n3. Immediately talk on the SAME topic again for 3 minutes.\n4. Immediately again for 2 minutes.\n5. Compare the three recordings.</code></pre>\n<p>Why it works: the content stays the same, so the second and third attempts have nothing to invent and everything to <em>retrieve</em>. You naturally drop fillers, tighten sentences and speed up. Typical result after four weeks of daily 4/3/2: the 2-minute version contains the same content as the original 4-minute one, in half the time.</p>\n<p><strong>Step 4 — Build chunk banks, not word lists.</strong> Words are stored singly and assembled slowly; chunks come out whole. Learn families:</p>\n<div class=\"tbl-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Function</th><th>Chunks</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Giving an opinion</td><td>\"As far as I'm concerned…\", \"My take on this is…\", \"I'd argue that…\"</td></tr><tr><td>Hedging</td><td>\"It depends on…\", \"Broadly speaking…\", \"I'm not fully sure, but…\"</td></tr><tr><td>Explaining cause</td><td>\"The reason for that is…\", \"That's largely because…\"</td></tr><tr><td>Disagreeing safely</td><td>\"I see it slightly differently…\", \"That's true up to a point, but…\"</td></tr><tr><td>Buying time</td><td>\"Let me think about how to put this…\", \"Off the top of my head…\"</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>Twelve well-drilled chunks will do more for your fluency than two hundred new words.</p>\n<p><strong>Step 5 — Practise thinking in English, in low-stakes moments.</strong> Narrate ordinary actions silently: <em>\"I'm boiling water, so I need a mug — the blue one, since the other is cracked.\"</em> The point is to remove the translation step. Translation is the main cause of the 2-second mid-sentence pause: you are waiting for your first language to hand over a word.</p>\n<p><strong>Step 6 — Fluency has a ceiling you should respect.</strong> Do not chase speed at the cost of clarity. A comfortable 130–150 words per minute with clear pauses scores better than a rushed 200 with mangled endings.</p>\n<div class=\"card\"><h4>Example question</h4><p>A student's 4/3/2 recordings show: attempt 1 = 4 min with 14 fillers and 6 restarts; attempt 3 = 2 min with 9 fillers and 2 restarts, but the same content. Diagnose what improved, what did not, and give the next drill.</p>\n<h4>Solution</h4>\n<ul><li><strong>What improved:</strong> content compression — the same material in half the time. That means retrieval got faster: the student stopped searching for what to say and started searching only for how to say it. Restarts fell from 6 to 2, so the \"abandon and restart\" habit is weakening.</li><li><strong>What did not improve:</strong> fillers fell only from 14 to 9, and per minute that is roughly 3.5 → 4.5, i.e. <strong>the filler <em>rate</em> actually got slightly worse</strong>. Reason: under time pressure, the student's filler habit became the default pause. This is a very common and easily missed pattern — always compute fillers per minute, not total.</li><li><strong>Diagnosis:</strong> fluency of <em>content</em> is improving; the <em>pause behaviour</em> is not yet trained.</li><li><strong>Next drill:</strong> a \"pause substitution\" drill — repeat the same 2-minute talk, but every time a filler is about to appear, replace it with a full stop and one breath. Record and count. Target: under 4 fillers in 2 minutes, then move to a new topic.</li></ul>\n<div class=\"res\">content retrieval and restart-avoidance improved; the filler rate per minute worsened slightly (3.5 → 4.5/min); next drill = pause substitution on the same topic.</div>\n<p><strong>Why this works:</strong> it refuses to accept \"fewer fillers overall\" as progress, because the talk was shorter. Counting per minute is the honest measure, and this is exactly the kind of quantitative check an examiner's ear is doing unconsciously.</p>\n<div class=\"note note--trap\"><span class=\"tag\">Common trap</span><p>measuring fillers by total count when the duration changed. Second trap: drilling 4/3/2 on a <em>new</em> topic each time — the whole mechanism depends on repeating the same content. Third: trying to eliminate all pauses. Native speakers pause constantly; the goal is well-placed pauses, not zero.</p></div></div>"}, {"kind": "concept", "num": "2.5", "title": "Group discussions", "html": "<h4>Intuition</h4>\n<p>A GD is not a debate and it is not a queue. It is a <strong>group of strangers trying to build one shared answer in about fifteen minutes, while being watched.</strong> The panel is not scoring who is right; they are scoring who is <em>useful to a group</em>.</p>\n<p>So the mental shift that matters most: stop asking \"how do I win?\" and start asking \"how do I move this group forward?\" The person who summarises the disagreement and names the two options is usually the one who gets selected — not the loudest one.</p>\n<h4>The idea, step by step</h4>\n<p><strong>Step 1 — Know the format.</strong> Typically 8–12 candidates, a topic given 1–3 minutes to think, 10–20 minutes of discussion, then a short summary from one or two members. Topics are usually current affairs, abstract statements (\"Is technology making us less social?\"), or case-based (\"Your company must cut costs by 20% — how?\").</p>\n<p><strong>Step 2 — Know the roles that exist, whether or not they are assigned.</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tbl-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Role</th><th>What they do</th><th>Selection value</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Initiator</strong></td><td>Opens with a definition or frame</td><td>High, if brief and accurate</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Builder</strong></td><td>Extends someone else's point with a new angle</td><td>Highest and safest</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Evidence-giver</strong></td><td>Adds a number, example, or case</td><td>High — most candidates do not</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Balancer</strong></td><td>Brings in the missing side</td><td>High — reads as maturity</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Summariser</strong></td><td>Names the options and the emerging agreement</td><td>High, especially late in the GD</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Dominator</strong></td><td>Speaks 40% of the time, interrupts</td><td>Negative — gets cut</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Silent passenger</strong></td><td>Says one sentence in fifteen minutes</td><td>Negative — no evidence to score</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><strong>Step 3 — Prepare three entry lines and keep them ready.</strong> Entering is the hardest 10 seconds; a prepared line removes the fear.</p>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>Opening (if you get the first chance):\n\"Let me start with a quick definition, so we're all arguing about the same thing — ...\"\n\nEntering mid-discussion (polite interrupt):\n\"Sorry to jump in — I'd like to add one angle to what Ravi said.\"\n\nEntering after a pause:\n\"Can I take that forward? The point about cost seems to have two parts.\"\n\nEntering when two people are talking:\n\"I think there are two views here — could we take them one at a time?\"</code></pre>\n<p><strong>Step 4 — Build on others, using their name.</strong> This is the single highest-scoring behaviour. Formula: <strong>name + their point + your addition.</strong></p>\n<div class=\"note note--why\"><span class=\"tag\">Note</span><p>\"Building on what Priya said about rural connectivity — the part I'd add is that the bottleneck isn't cables, it's electricity.\"</p></div>\n<p>Compare with the low-scoring version: \"I think rural connectivity is bad.\" That is a new monologue, not a contribution.</p>\n<p><strong>Step 5 — Handle a dominant speaker, without a fight.</strong> Three escalating moves:</p>\n<ol><li><strong>The bridge:</strong> \"That's a strong point — let me add to it.\" (you take the turn by accepting theirs)</li><li><strong>The redirect:</strong> \"Before we go further, what does the group think about the cost side?\" (you hand the floor to others; the dominator is now outnumbered politely)</li><li><strong>The explicit, polite appeal:</strong> \"We've heard a lot from this side — I'd really like to hear from those who haven't spoken.\" (this is <em>group-protective</em>, and panels notice it)</li></ol>\n<p>Never do: raising your voice, \"let me finish!\" with a hard tone, or personal remarks. All three read as a temperament problem, which is the one thing a panel will reject you for.</p>\n<p><strong>Step 6 — Avoid the monologue trap.</strong> A monologue is any turn over about 45 seconds. Even excellent content loses points after that, because you have stopped being a group member. Discipline: <strong>two sentences, then hand over.</strong> \"…so that's my view — what do you think about the implementation cost?\"</p>\n<p><strong>Step 7 — Keep a mental scoreboard.</strong> Track three things while speaking: (i) which points have been made, (ii) which side is under-represented, (iii) how long since you last spoke. Item (iii) is the one candidates forget — a good contribution at minute 2 and silence till minute 15 reads as a one-off.</p>\n<p><strong>Step 8 — Prepare a closing summary shape.</strong> \"We seem to have three threads: X, Y, Z. On X there's broad agreement; on Y we split, mainly over cost. The one thing nobody has covered is implementation time.\" Naming an un-covered angle at the end is a strong finish.</p>\n<div class=\"card\"><h4>Example question</h4><p>In a GD on <em>\"Should coding be taught in schools from class 6?\"</em> the discussion is going badly: one candidate, Sameer, has spoken four times in six minutes, two others have not spoken at all, and the group keeps repeating \"it will help in future jobs\". Write (a) your entry line, (b) a contribution that builds on someone, and (c) a line that both handles Sameer and invites the silent members.</p>\n<h4>Solution</h4>\n<ul><li>(a) Entry line (after Sameer's fourth turn, using the bridge): <em>\"Building on Sameer's point about future jobs — I'd add a distinction that might help us. There's coding as a </em>skill<em> and coding as a </em>way of thinking<em>. Those are different arguments, and we're mixing them.\"</em> Reason: it enters without confrontation, and it introduces a frame the group can use — which is what \"initiator\" value looks like even mid-discussion.</li><li>(b) Building contribution: <em>\"To take Priya's point about teacher shortage one step further — if we can't staff it, the alternative isn't no coding, it's a shorter module taught by existing maths teachers, using block-based tools. That's a cost we can actually estimate.\"</em> Reason: name + their point + a new, concrete angle with a feasibility element.</li><li>(c) Handling Sameer and inviting the silent ones: <em>\"Sameer, you've made a strong case for the jobs angle — could we hold that and hear the other side for a minute? We haven't heard from Anjali or Farhan at all, and I'd genuinely like the counter-argument before we conclude.\"</em> Reason: it credits Sameer (so he does not feel attacked), asks for a specific behaviour change (\"hold that\"), and names the silent members directly — direct naming is much more effective than a general \"anyone else?\".</li></ul>\n<div class=\"res\">enter by bridging and reframing (skill vs way of thinking), build on a named speaker with a concrete feasibility angle, and redirect with credit plus direct naming of the silent members.</div>\n<p><strong>Why this works:</strong> all three moves increase the group's output rather than your airtime. That is the actual selection criterion, and it is why a quieter, well-structured candidate often beats a dominant one.</p>\n<div class=\"note note--trap\"><span class=\"tag\">Common trap</span><p>treating the GD as a speech contest and speaking 40% of the time. Second trap: \"I agree with him\" with no addition — agreement without extension is scored as nothing. Third: a personal remark about a dominant speaker (\"you're not letting anyone speak\") which turns a content issue into a character issue.</p></div></div>"}, {"kind": "concept", "num": "2.6", "title": "Proficiency-test speaking tasks", "html": "<h4>Intuition</h4>\n<p>Test speaking is <strong>performance under a known format</strong>. You are not being asked to be interesting; you are being asked to produce enough language, of sufficient variety, coherently, for a fixed number of seconds. Once you know the format, the fear drops sharply, because there is nothing to be surprised by.</p>\n<h4>The idea, step by step</h4>\n<h4>Step 1 — Know the four task types.</h4>\n<div class=\"tbl-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Task</th><th>Format</th><th>What it tests</th><th>The trap</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Cue-card long turn</strong> (IELTS Part 2 style)</td><td>1 min prep, then 1–2 min talk on a card with 4 bullets</td><td>Extended monologue: structure, range, fluency</td><td>Treating bullets as a checklist; finishing in 40 seconds; freezing on one bullet</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Follow-up questions</strong></td><td>Short questions after the long turn</td><td>Spontaneous response, opinion, justification</td><td>One-word answers; no reason given</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Describing a chart / graph</strong></td><td>Look at a visual, describe and compare</td><td>Data language, comparison, trends</td><td>Reading every number; no overall trend statement</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Interactive discussion</strong> (Part 3 style)</td><td>Abstract questions, examiner pushes back</td><td>Argument, hedging, range under pressure</td><td>Repeating the same point louder</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><strong>Step 2 — Cue card: use the 1-minute prep properly.</strong> Do not write sentences. Write <strong>five keywords</strong> in a column, one per chunk of your talk, and a number beside each. Prep order:</p>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>what it is          -&gt; 1 keyword\nwhen/where          -&gt; 1 keyword\nwhy it matters      -&gt; 1 keyword\none detail/story    -&gt; 1 keyword + 1 number\nhow you feel / next -&gt; 1 keyword</code></pre>\n<p><strong>Step 3 — Cue card: the first sentence decides your confidence.</strong> Use a frame, not a memorised script:</p>\n<div class=\"note note--why\"><span class=\"tag\">Note</span><p>\"I'd like to talk about ___, which is something I've ___ for about ___.\"</p></div>\n<p>That single sentence gets you 8 seconds in, and momentum does the rest.</p>\n<p><strong>Step 4 — Cue card: handle a bullet you cannot answer.</strong> Say so briefly and move: <em>\"I don't have a strong memory of the exact date, but what I do remember is…\"</em> Examiners do not penalise gaps in content; they penalise silence.</p>\n<p><strong>Step 5 — Follow-ups: use the 2-line answer.</strong> Line 1 = direct answer. Line 2 = reason or example. That is enough for most follow-ups, and it stops you from rambling.</p>\n<div class=\"note note--why\"><span class=\"tag\">Note</span><p>Q: \"Do you think people read less than before?\" A: \"Probably less, yes — though not less <em>text</em>. Most people I know read more words per day than ever, just in messages and feeds rather than books.\"</p></div>\n<h4>Step 6 — Describing a chart: use the four-move structure.</h4>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>1. What the chart shows (one sentence, no numbers yet)\n2. The overall trend (the single most important sentence)\n3. Two or three specific figures as support\n4. A comparison or a caveat</code></pre>\n<p>Trend language to have ready: <em>rose steadily, climbed sharply, plateaued, dipped slightly, fell by half, doubled, remained flat, peaked at, bottomed out at</em>. Approximation language: <em>roughly, just under, around, nearly, a little over</em>.</p>\n<p><strong>Step 7 — Do not read the chart aloud.</strong> \"In 2019 it was 20, in 2020 it was 25, in 2021 it was 31\" scores poorly. The examiner wants the <em>story</em> the numbers tell.</p>\n<div class=\"card\"><h4>Example question</h4><p>Cue card: <em>\"Describe a piece of technology you find useful. You should say: what it is; how long you have had it; how you use it; and explain why you find it useful.\"</em> Write the full 2-minute talk (about 300 words), then write a 2-line answer to the follow-up: <em>\"Do you think people depend too much on technology?\"</em></p>\n<h4>Solution</h4>\n<div class=\"note note--why\"><span class=\"tag\">Note</span><p><em>(Opening frame)</em> \"I'd like to talk about my noise-cancelling headphones, which I've had for about eighteen months now — they were a birthday gift from my brother, and honestly they've changed how I study.\"  <em>(What and how long, briefly — do not linger)</em> \"They're a fairly ordinary pair, nothing expensive by today's standards, but the noise cancellation is good enough to make a hostel room feel like a library.\"  <em>(How I use it — the detail chunk, with a number)</em> \"I use them in two specific ways. First, for deep work: I block out ninety minutes in the evening, headphones on, phone in another room. Second, for what I'd call 'learning by listening' — I run lecture recordings at 1.25 speed while walking to the mess, so about forty minutes a day of revision happens without me scheduling it.\"  <em>(Why useful — the landing, generalising beyond the object)</em> \"What makes them useful isn't the sound quality, it's that they remove a decision. I don't have to find a quiet place or wait for the room to empty; the quiet is portable. So the real benefit is that my study time is no longer hostage to my environment. That's a small thing, but it adds up to maybe six or seven hours a week.\"  <em>(Close)</em> \"I'd recommend them to any student in a shared room, though I'd say the cheaper models are fine — the difference is smaller than the price suggests.\"</p></div>\n<p>Follow-up answer: <em>\"In some ways, yes — but I'd separate dependence from convenience. Depending on a tool that makes you more effective is fine; the problem is when the tool decides what you do next. The test I use is simple: can I work for an hour without it? If the answer is no, that's dependence.\"</em></p>\n<div class=\"res\">roughly 300 words; a five-chunk structure (frame → brief facts → two specific uses → generalised value → close with a recommendation); follow-up answered in two lines with a distinction and a test.</div>\n<p><strong>Why this works:</strong> it covers all four bullets without sounding like a checklist, it has one number per chunk (\"ninety minutes\", \"1.25 speed\", \"forty minutes a day\", \"six or seven hours\"), and the landing moves from the <em>object</em> to a <em>principle</em>. Examiners score range and coherence; both come from exactly this shape.</p>\n<div class=\"note note--trap\"><span class=\"tag\">Common trap</span><p>talking for only 45 seconds and stopping. In a 2-minute task, running out of content is a bigger loss than a small grammar error. Second trap: \"In 2019 it was 20, in 2020 it was 25…\" for charts. Third: answering follow-ups with \"Yes, I think so\" and nothing else.</p></div></div>"}, {"kind": "concept", "num": "2.7", "title": "Public speaking", "html": "<h4>Intuition</h4>\n<p>Nerves are not a sign that something is wrong. They are the same physical response that makes a singer's voice carry: raised heart rate, sharpened attention, extra adrenaline. The problem is not the adrenaline; it is that we interpret it as \"I am not ready\".</p>\n<p>The most useful reframe: <strong>you are not performing for the audience, you are giving them something.</strong> A talk is a gift with a shape — here is the thing, here is why it matters to you, here is what you can do with it. Audiences forgive a shaky voice; they do not forgive a talk with no point.</p>\n<h4>The idea, step by step</h4>\n<h4>Step 1 — Build the opening in three moves (first 30 seconds).</h4>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>Hook:     a question, a number, or a small story   (5-8 seconds)\nStake:    why it matters to THIS audience          (8-10 seconds)\nMap:      \"I'll cover three things: A, B, C\"        (8-10 seconds)</code></pre>\n<p>Never open with \"Good morning everyone, myself is…, today I am going to talk about…\". It spends your most valuable 20 seconds on nothing.</p>\n<h4>Step 2 — Use one of four reliable structures.</h4>\n<div class=\"tbl-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Structure</th><th>Shape</th><th>Best for</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Problem–Solution–Benefit</strong></td><td>Here is the pain → here is the fix → here is the gain</td><td>Project demos, pitches</td></tr><tr><td><strong>What–So What–Now What</strong></td><td>The fact → why it matters → what to do</td><td>Persuasive talks, seminars</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Past–Present–Future</strong></td><td>Where we were → where we are → where we are going</td><td>Reviews, annual updates</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Three points</strong></td><td>\"There are three reasons…\"</td><td>Almost anything short</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><strong>Step 3 — Practise audience awareness in three questions.</strong> (i) What do they already know? (skip it) (ii) What do they care about? (lead with that) (iii) What do they fear or resist? (address it early). A talk that answers (iii) early is trusted; one that hides it is resisted.</p>\n<p><strong>Step 4 — Manage nerves with preparation, not willpower.</strong> Four things that actually work:</p>\n<ol><li><strong>Rehearse the first 60 seconds and the last 30 seconds to automaticity.</strong> Nerves peak at the start; a rehearsed opening carries you through the peak.</li><li><strong>Slow down deliberately at the start.</strong> Adrenaline tends to speed speech up, and most speakers do not notice it; consciously slowing the first two sentences fixes most of the rush.</li><li><strong>Breathe low, once, before you begin.</strong> One slow exhale lowers the pitch and steadies the voice.</li><li><strong>Look at one friendly face per sentence, not the whole room.</strong> Sweeping the room with your eyes makes you feel exposed; landing on one person feels like conversation.</li></ol>\n<p><strong>Step 5 — Body language: three rules only.</strong> (i) <strong>Feet still, hands visible</strong> — no pockets, no pen-clicking. (ii) <strong>One gesture per key point</strong>, made slightly bigger than feels natural. (iii) <strong>Pause instead of filling</strong> — a two-second pause after an important sentence signals confidence and gives the point room.</p>\n<p><strong>Step 6 — Handle questions with a three-part reply.</strong> Acknowledge → answer → check. <em>\"Good question. The short answer is X, because Y. Does that cover it?\"</em> If you do not know: <em>\"I don't know — I'll check and get back to you by tomorrow.\"</em> This is a strong answer; inventing one is the only fatal answer.</p>\n<p><strong>Step 7 — Close by returning to the opening.</strong> If you opened with a question or a story, answer it in the last line. This makes a talk feel designed rather than assembled.</p>\n<div class=\"card\"><h4>Example question</h4><p>Write the opening 30 seconds of a 5-minute talk to first-year students titled <em>\"Why your first project will fail — and why that's fine\"</em>, then the closing 20 seconds. Mark the hook, stake and map.</p>\n<h4>Solution</h4>\n<div class=\"note note--why\"><span class=\"tag\">Note</span><p><em>(HOOK — question, 6 seconds)</em> \"How many of you have already started planning your first-year project? Keep your hand up if you're sure it will work.\"  <em>(beat — pause while hands come down)</em>  <em>(STAKE — why it matters to them, 10 seconds)</em> \"I'm asking because mine didn't work. Not 'it was late' — it didn't work at all. And the useful part of that experience wasn't the failure; it was discovering that the failure was predictable, in advance, from three specific mistakes.\"  <em>(MAP — 10 seconds)</em> \"So in the next five minutes I'll show you the three mistakes, in the order they'll hit you — scope, tools, and teammates — and one habit that fixes all three.\"  <em>(…body of the talk…)</em>  <em>(CLOSING — 20 seconds, returns to the opening)</em> \"Remember the hands that stayed up at the start? Almost none of them will still be up in March, and that's not because those people were wrong about themselves. It's because they hadn't yet made the three mistakes. So I'll leave you with this: your first project is not a test of whether you're good at engineering. It's a rehearsal for the version of you that will be. Fail cheaply, early, and on purpose — that's the whole skill.\"</p></div>\n<div class=\"res\">hook = a show-of-hands question with a pause; stake = a personal failure plus the promise of a predictable pattern; map = three named mistakes plus one fix; close = returns to the raised hands and reframes failure as rehearsal.</div>\n<p><strong>Why this works:</strong> the opening earns attention in six seconds without introducing yourself, the map gives the audience a place to put each point, and the close pays off the hook. Every one of those is a technique, not a personality trait.</p>\n<div class=\"note note--trap\"><span class=\"tag\">Common trap</span><p>\"Myself is Arjun\" — wrong grammar (\"My name is Arjun\" or \"I'm Arjun\") and a wasted opening. Second: reading slides aloud, which tells the audience the slides are the speaker. Third: apologising (\"sorry, I'm a bit nervous, I didn't prepare much\") — the audience did not notice until you mentioned it.</p></div></div>"}, {"kind": "concept", "num": "2.8", "title": "AI-assisted practice and self-assessment", "html": "<h4>Intuition</h4>\n<p>You cannot hear your own speech the way others hear it, because while you speak you are busy producing it. A recording solves that instantly: the gap between what you <em>thought</em> you said and what you actually said is usually large, and seeing it is what makes improvement fast.</p>\n<p>AI tools add two things to a plain recorder: a <strong>transcript</strong> (so you can count fillers and errors objectively) and <strong>unlimited patience</strong> (so you can rehearse the same answer ten times without a human getting bored). What they cannot give you is a judgement about your content or a real listener's reaction — so keep a human in the loop for anything that matters.</p>\n<h4>The idea, step by step</h4>\n<p><strong>Step 1 — Record with a fixed protocol.</strong> Same task, same duration, once a week. Otherwise you cannot compare.</p>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>Weekly speaking protocol (15 minutes)\n1. One 2-minute monologue on a set topic (recorded).\n2. Transcribe it (AI or by hand).\n3. Count: fillers, restarts, long pauses, different adjectives.\n4. Re-record the SAME topic once, applying one fix.\n5. Save both files with the date.</code></pre>\n<p><strong>Step 2 — Use the transcript for counting, not for judging.</strong> The transcript gives you hard numbers: fillers per minute, sentences started and abandoned, repeated words. Numbers reveal patterns that memory hides. But a transcript cannot tell you about stress, intonation or eye contact — do not pretend it can.</p>\n<p><strong>Step 3 — Ask AI for specific, constrained feedback.</strong> Weak prompt: \"how is my English?\" Strong prompt: <em>\"Here is a 2-minute transcript of my speech. Do three things only: (1) list every filler and its count, (2) mark grammar errors and give the corrected sentence, (3) suggest three more natural phrases for the parts I repeated. Do not rewrite my whole answer.\"</em> The constraint matters — unconstrained AI rewrites your answer into a different, more formal register you cannot reproduce live.</p>\n<p><strong>Step 4 — Use shadowing and reading aloud for pronunciation.</strong> Pick a 60-second clip of a clear speaker. Listen, then speak <em>simultaneously</em> with them, matching rhythm and stress rather than individual sounds. Then record yourself alone on the same text and compare. This is the fastest fix for flat stress.</p>\n<p><strong>Step 5 — Self-rate with a rubric, monthly.</strong> Score 1–5 on each row, and write one piece of evidence per row. Evidence is what stops the scores drifting upward for free.</p>\n<div class=\"tbl-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Criterion</th><th>1–2 (needs work)</th><th>3 (adequate)</th><th>4–5 (strong)</th><th>My score + evidence</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Fluency</strong></td><td>Frequent long pauses, restarts</td><td>Some hesitation, keeps going</td><td>Smooth, natural pauses</td><td></td></tr><tr><td><strong>Accuracy</strong></td><td>Errors block meaning</td><td>Errors noticeable but clear</td><td>Few errors, self-corrects</td><td></td></tr><tr><td><strong>Range</strong></td><td>Same 20 words repeated</td><td>Adequate vocabulary</td><td>Precise, varied, idiomatic</td><td></td></tr><tr><td><strong>Pronunciation</strong></td><td>Listener must ask for repeats</td><td>Understood with effort</td><td>Clear stress and intonation</td><td></td></tr><tr><td><strong>Coherence</strong></td><td>No visible structure</td><td>Some signposting</td><td>Clear point–example–point</td><td></td></tr><tr><td><strong>Interaction</strong></td><td>Monologue or silence</td><td>Responds when asked</td><td>Builds, checks, invites</td><td></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><strong>Step 6 — Pick exactly one target per fortnight.</strong> Six criteria and six simultaneous goals equals zero progress. Choose the lowest-scoring row with the highest impact (usually coherence or fluency), drill only that, and re-rate after two weeks.</p>\n<p><strong>Step 7 — Keep a human checkpoint.</strong> Once a month, speak to a person — a senior, a teacher, a friend who will be honest — and ask one question: <em>\"What was the one thing that made me hard to follow?\"</em> AI will not tell you that.</p>\n<div class=\"card\"><h4>Example question</h4><p>A student's rubric reads: Fluency 3, Accuracy 3, Range 2, Pronunciation 2, Coherence 2, Interaction 3. Their transcript shows 22 uses of \"good\", 9 uses of \"very\", and every answer built as one long sentence. Choose the single fortnight target, justify the choice, and write the exact practice plan and AI prompt.</p>\n<h4>Solution</h4>\n<ul><li><strong>Choose Range (2) and Coherence (2) together — but pick Coherence as the primary target.</strong> Reason: the evidence shows one long sentence per answer, so there is no structure for the listener to hold. Range is a close second (22 \"good\", 9 \"very\"), and it is easy to improve, but fixing range without structure produces a talk full of better words with no shape.</li><li>Why not Pronunciation first, despite the 2? Because pronunciation work needs a human or careful audio comparison and gives slower visible returns in two weeks; it is the target for the <em>next</em> fortnight, run in parallel as a 5-minute daily shadowing habit.</li><li><strong>Practice plan (fortnight):</strong></li><li>Daily 10 min: answer one question using forced PEP — one sentence point, one example with a number, one landing sentence. Record. Count sentences per answer; target ≥ 3.</li><li>Daily 5 min: shadowing, one clip.</li><li>Twice weekly: 4/3/2 on the same topic.</li><li><strong>AI prompt:</strong> <em>\"I'll send a transcript of a 90-second answer. Check only two things: (1) does it have a clear point, one example, and a concluding sentence — quote the sentences that do or don't; (2) list every repeated adjective and give me two more precise alternatives for each. Do not rewrite the answer. Then give me three follow-up questions on the same topic.\"</em></li></ul>\n<div class=\"res\">primary target = coherence (structure), secondary = range; pronunciation deferred to a parallel daily shadowing habit; plan = daily PEP answers with a sentence count, plus twice-weekly 4/3/2; prompt constrained to structure-checking and adjective alternatives only.</div>\n<p><strong>Why this works:</strong> it chooses based on <em>evidence</em> (\"one long sentence\"), it sequences rather than stacking goals, and it constrains the AI so the feedback is actionable by the student rather than a replacement for them.</p>\n<div class=\"note note--trap\"><span class=\"tag\">Common trap</span><p>asking AI to \"improve my answer\" and then memorising the polished version — it will not survive a live conversation, and it hides your real errors. Second: rating yourself 4s with no evidence. Third: changing six things at once and being unable to tell what worked.</p></div></div>"}, {"kind": "formula", "num": "", "title": "Formulas, rules and properties", "html": "<p>No formulas here either — this is the \"which technique when\" guide for speaking.</p>\n<div class=\"tbl-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Situation</th><th>Use this</th><th>One-line rule</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Diagnosing your speaking</td><td>Six components: fluency, accuracy, range, pronunciation, coherence, interaction</td><td>Name the weak one with evidence; do not say \"my English is bad\"</td></tr><tr><td>Confusing word pairs</td><td>Minimal-pair drill (v/w, th, s/sh)</td><td>One pair, ten repetitions, daily</td></tr><tr><td>Long words mis-stressed</td><td>Stress-shift families (PHOtograph → phoTOgraphy → photoGRAPHic)</td><td>-ic, -tion, -ity pull stress left by one</td></tr><tr><td>Statement sounding unsure</td><td>Falling intonation ↘</td><td>Statements and wh-questions fall</td></tr><tr><td>Yes/no question sounding flat</td><td>Rising intonation ↗</td><td>Rise for confirmation, not for wh-questions</td></tr><tr><td>Any interview answer</td><td>Point → Example → Point</td><td>One story with one number beats five adjectives</td></tr><tr><td>1-minute talk</td><td>3 chunks × 20 seconds</td><td>One idea only</td></tr><tr><td>2-minute talk</td><td>5 chunks × 24 seconds</td><td>Include a limitation before the close</td></tr><tr><td>Building fluency</td><td>4/3/2 on the <em>same</em> topic</td><td>Repetition is the mechanism, not the shortcut</td></tr><tr><td>Too many fillers</td><td>Pause substitution + stall phrases</td><td>Silence at a boundary beats a filler mid-phrase</td></tr><tr><td>Entering a GD</td><td>\"Building on what X said…\"</td><td>Name + their point + your addition</td></tr><tr><td>Dominant speaker in a GD</td><td>Bridge → redirect → polite appeal to the group</td><td>Never make it personal</td></tr><tr><td>Cue card</td><td>5 keywords in 1 minute of prep</td><td>Keywords, never sentences</td></tr><tr><td>Describing a chart</td><td>What it shows → overall trend → 2–3 figures → caveat</td><td>The trend sentence carries the marks</td></tr><tr><td>Nerves before a talk</td><td>Rehearse first 60s and last 30s; slow the opening</td><td>Adrenaline speeds you up; compensate on purpose</td></tr><tr><td>Improving alone</td><td>Record → transcribe → count → re-record one fix</td><td>One target per fortnight</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"}, {"kind": "example", "num": "", "title": "Solved examples", "html": "<p><strong>Example 1 (easy) — stress changes word class.</strong> <em>Task:</em> Mark the stress and say the word class: (a) \"The committee will <em>object</em> to the <em>object</em>.\" (b) \"We need to <em>increase</em> the <em>increase</em>.\" <em>Step 1:</em> English has noun–verb stress pairs where the noun takes first-syllable stress and the verb second. <em>Step 2:</em> (a) verb = ob-JECT, noun = OB-ject. Sentence: \"The committee will obJECT to the OBject.\" <em>Step 3:</em> (b) verb = in-CREASE, noun = IN-crease. Sentence: \"We need to inCREASE the INcrease.\" <em>Step 4:</em> Sanity check: same pattern as REcord/reCORD, PERmit/perMIT — consistent. <strong>Final answer:</strong> obJECT (v) / OBject (n); inCREASE (v) / INcrease (n).</p>\n<p><strong>Example 2 (easy–medium) — rewrite a weak answer as PEP.</strong> <em>Weak:</em> \"I am a hardworking person and I like to learn new things and I am very passionate about coding.\" <em>Step 1:</em> Diagnose — three adjectives, no evidence, one long sentence, no landing. Range is thin (\"very passionate\"). <em>Step 2:</em> Build the point: \"I'd describe myself as someone who finishes things.\" <em>Step 3:</em> Add an example with a number: \"Last semester four of us started a attendance-tracker project; three dropped out by week five, and I rebuilt the backend alone in nine days to keep the deadline.\" <em>Step 4:</em> Land it: \"So the pattern I'd point to isn't talent — it's that I'd rather ship a smaller version than abandon a bigger one.\" <em>Step 5:</em> Check length: about 55 words, one story, two numbers, one claim. Better than 30 words of adjectives. <strong>Final answer:</strong> point (finisher) → example (attendance tracker, nine days, three dropouts) → landing (ship small rather than abandon big).</p>\n<p><strong>Example 3 (medium) — GD contribution under pressure.</strong> <em>Situation:</em> GD topic: \"Work from home is better than working from office.\" You have not spoken for five minutes; two people are arguing loudly; the group has ignored cost and team culture entirely. <em>Step 1:</em> Enter with the bridge, not a new topic: \"Can I bring in the angle we've skipped?\" <em>Step 2:</em> Name the gap: \"We've argued productivity both ways, but nobody has mentioned cost — for the employee, the office is a subsidised space.\" <em>Step 3:</em> Add one concrete point: \"A Mumbai commuter saves roughly two hours and a few thousand rupees a month; a company saves rent but pays for laptops and security.\" <em>Step 4:</em> Hand over: \"So it may be that WFH is better for the employee and worse for the junior who needs to learn by overhearing. Does that split hold for the rest of you?\" <em>Step 5:</em> Why this scores: it is a builder + evidence-giver + summariser move in 40 seconds, it credits the existing debate, and it ends with a question that pulls others in. <strong>Final answer:</strong> bridge → name the missing angle (cost) → one concrete figure → a testable distinction (employee vs junior) → hand over.</p>\n<p><strong>Example 4 (exam level) — full chart description.</strong> <em>Task:</em> A line graph shows average daily study hours for three groups of students (hostellers, day scholars, working students) over four years. Hostellers: 5, 5.5, 6, 6.2. Day scholars: 4, 4.2, 4, 3.8. Working students: 2, 2.5, 3, 3.5. Write a 90-second description. <em>Step 1:</em> What it shows (no numbers): \"The graph compares average daily study hours across three groups of students over four years.\" <em>Step 2:</em> Overall trend in one sentence: \"The clearest pattern is convergence — hostellers rose steadily while working students more than doubled, and the gap between them narrowed from three hours to under three.\" (Careful: 5 − 2 = 3 at the start; 6.2 − 3.5 = 2.7 at the end — so \"narrowed from about three hours to under three\" is accurate.) <em>Step 3:</em> Specific figures: \"Hostellers climbed from 5 to 6.2 hours. Working students went from 2 to 3.5 — the steepest relative rise, a 75% increase. Day scholars are the only group that ended lower than they started, dipping from 4 to 3.8 after a small rise in year two.\" <em>Step 4:</em> Caveat: \"The one figure I'd treat cautiously is the final hosteller number, because a 0.2-hour change is within normal weekly variation — the trend matters more than the exact value.\" <em>Step 5:</em> Check all arithmetic: 3.5/2 = 1.75 → 75% increase . 4 → 3.8 = a 0.2 fall . 5 − 2 = 3 , 6.2 − 3.5 = 2.7 . <strong>Final answer:</strong> what-it-shows → convergence as the overall trend → three supporting figures with one percentage → a caveat about precision. Note that the model never reads the twelve numbers in order.</p>"}, {"kind": "trap", "num": "", "title": "Common traps and mistakes", "html": "<ul><li><strong>Saying \"my English is weak\" instead of naming a component.</strong> Why it costs marks: no diagnosis means no fix, and in an exam the components <em>are</em> the answer.</li><li><strong>Judging pronunciation from a written transcript.</strong> You cannot hear sounds in text. Saying so is worth more than inventing a judgement.</li><li><strong>Chasing an accent instead of intelligibility.</strong> The target is zero \"sorry, what?\" requests, not a British accent.</li><li><strong>Calling Indian English retroflex /t/ and /d/ \"wrong\".</strong> They are variety features; you adjust for international clarity, not because they are errors.</li><li><strong>Marking stress by counting letters.</strong> Count syllables: <em>photography</em> = pho-to-graph-y (4), stress on syllable 2.</li><li><strong>Rising intonation on wh-questions.</strong> \"What is your NAME ↗\" sounds uncertain. Wh-questions fall.</li><li><strong>Answering with adjectives instead of evidence.</strong> \"Hardworking, sincere, fast learner\" is unfalsifiable; one story with one number is not.</li><li><strong>A monologue in a GD (over ~45 seconds).</strong> You stop being a group member and the panel stops scoring you.</li><li><strong>\"I agree with him\" with no addition.</strong> Agreement without extension scores as nothing.</li><li><strong>Making a dominant speaker's behaviour personal.</strong> It converts a content problem into a temperament problem, which panels reject.</li><li><strong>Memorising a full script for a cue card.</strong> One forgotten sentence collapses the whole thing, and recitation is obvious.</li><li><strong>Reading a chart aloud number by number.</strong> The trend sentence is where the marks are.</li><li><strong>Opening a talk with \"Myself is…\" or an apology.</strong> Both waste the 20 seconds that decide the audience's attention.</li><li><strong>Counting fillers by total instead of per minute.</strong> If the talk got shorter, the total can fall while the rate rises.</li><li><strong>Running 4/3/2 on a new topic each time.</strong> The drill works because the content repeats; changing topics removes the mechanism.</li><li><strong>Asking AI to \"improve my answer\" and memorising it.</strong> The polished version does not survive a live conversation, and it hides your real errors.</li><li><strong>Rating yourself 4/5 with no evidence.</strong> The numbers drift upward and stop measuring anything.</li></ul>"}, {"kind": "revision", "num": "", "title": "Quick revision box", "html": "<ul><li>Six components: <strong>fluency, accuracy, range, pronunciation, coherence, interaction</strong>. Diagnose with evidence; fix <strong>coherence first</strong>, accent last.</li><li>Sound drills that matter for Indian speakers: <strong>v/w, th (/θ ð/), s/sh, aspiration of initial p/t/k</strong>; retroflex /t d/ is a variety feature, not an error.</li><li><strong>Word stress</strong> moves with word form: PHOtograph → phoTOgraphy → photoGRAPHic. <strong>-ic, -tion, -ity</strong> pull stress to the syllable before. Noun/verb pairs: OBject/obJECT.</li><li><strong>Content words carry sentence stress</strong>; shifting the stress shifts the meaning. <strong>Falling ↘</strong> = statement, wh-question, certainty. <strong>Rising ↗</strong> = yes/no question, uncertainty. Fall–rise = reservation.</li><li><strong>PEP</strong>: point → example (with a number) → point. 1-minute talk = 3 chunks × 20s; 2-minute talk = 5 chunks × 24s including a limitation.</li><li><strong>4/3/2 on the same topic</strong> is the highest-return fluency drill; replace fillers with a <strong>pause at a boundary</strong>, and use stall phrases rather than restarts.</li><li><strong>GD</strong>: enter by building (\"Building on what X said…\"), keep turns under ~45 seconds, handle dominators with <strong>bridge → redirect → polite group appeal</strong>, and summarise the disagreement.</li><li><strong>Tests</strong>: cue card = 5 keywords in prep, frame sentence first, never stop early; charts = what-it-shows → trend → 2–3 figures → caveat.</li><li><strong>Public speaking</strong>: hook (6s) → stake (10s) → map (10s); rehearse first 60s and last 30s; slow the opening; close by returning to the hook.</li><li><strong>Practice loop</strong>: record → transcribe → count → re-record one fix. <strong>One target per fortnight</strong>, and a human checkpoint monthly.</li></ul>"}], "practice": [{"q": "Name the component of speaking proficiency that is weakest in each: (a) \"He don't know the answer.\" (b) \"It was good, the class was good, the teacher was good.\" (c) A listener asks \"sorry, was that west or vest ?\" (d) The student speaks for four minutes with no visible order and no conclusion.", "a": "<p>(a) <strong>Accuracy</strong> — subject–verb agreement error. (b) <strong>Range</strong> — the same adjective three times; nothing is described precisely. (c) <strong>Pronunciation</strong> — the /v/–/w/ contrast is not being produced distinctly. (d) <strong>Coherence</strong> — no structure, no signposting, no landing (though the length itself suggests fluency is adequate, so the diagnosis must not blame fluency).</p>", "tag": "easy", "qHtml": "Name the component of speaking proficiency that is weakest in each: (a) \"He don't know the answer.\" (b) \"It was good, the class was good, the teacher was good.\" (c) A listener asks \"sorry, was that <em>west</em> or <em>vest</em>?\" (d) The student speaks for four minutes with no visible order and no conclusion."}, {"q": "Mark the stressed syllable and state the word class: (a) record in \"keep a record\", (b) record in \"we will record it\", (c) permit as a noun, (d) photography . Then say which intonation contour you would use for \"You've submitted it ↗?\" and why.", "a": "<p>Key step: count syllables, then apply the noun-first / verb-second pattern. (a) <strong>RE-cord</strong>, noun. (b) re-<strong>CORD</strong>, verb. (c) <strong>PER-mit</strong>, noun. (d) pho-<strong>TOG</strong>-ra-phy, stress on the second syllable — the \"-tog-\" syllable, not \"-to-\". Intonation: rising ↗ — a yes/no question asking for confirmation. A falling contour would make it a flat statement of fact (or sound like an accusation of lateness).</p>", "tag": "medium", "qHtml": "Mark the stressed syllable and state the word class: (a) <em>record</em> in \"keep a record\", (b) <em>record</em> in \"we will record it\", (c) <em>permit</em> as a noun, (d) <em>photography</em>. Then say which intonation contour you would use for \"You've submitted it ↗?\" and why."}, {"q": "Rewrite this answer to an interview question using point → example → point, in under 70 words: \"I am very passionate about teamwork and I always help my teammates and I am also good at coding.\"", "a": "<p>Model answer (66 words):</p>\n<div class=\"note note--why\"><span class=\"tag\">Note</span><p>\"I'd point to one project rather than describe myself. Four of us built an attendance tracker; I wrote the backend and the database schema, and when two members left in week five I also took over the testing. We shipped two days early. So what I'd claim isn't 'good at teamwork' — it's that I take the unglamorous part of a team task without being asked.\"</p></div>\n<p>Key step: replace all three adjectives with one story, one number (\"two days early\") and one claim you could be challenged on.</p>", "tag": "medium", "qHtml": "Rewrite this answer to an interview question using point → example → point, in under 70 words: <em>\"I am very passionate about teamwork and I always help my teammates and I am also good at coding.\"</em>"}, {"q": "In a GD, a candidate says: \"Nobody here understands economics, so let me explain it.\" Write your two-part response: one line that keeps the discussion civil, and one line that brings the group back to the topic.", "a": "<p>Model answer:</p>\n<ul><li>Civil line: <em>\"That's a strong area to bring in — and I think several of us have different pieces of it, so let's pool them rather than have one explanation.\"</em> (Credits the person, removes the put-down, avoids a fight.)</li><li>Back-to-topic line: <em>\"Coming back to the question — should the subsidy be universal or means-tested? That's the decision we actually have to reach.\"</em> (Replaces the personal exchange with a decision the group must make.)</li></ul>\n<p>Why it works: neither line argues about the insult, so the group's time is not spent on it, and both lines hand the floor back to the topic.</p>", "tag": "medium", "qHtml": "In a GD, a candidate says: <em>\"Nobody here understands economics, so let me explain it.\"</em> Write your two-part response: one line that keeps the discussion civil, and one line that brings the group back to the topic."}, {"q": "Write the full 90-second opening of a talk to your department titled \"Why we should document our code\" , marking hook, stake and map, and name the structure you would use for the body.", "a": "<p>Model opening:</p>\n<div class=\"note note--why\"><span class=\"tag\">Note</span><p><em>(HOOK)</em> \"Last month a teammate left our project. His code still runs in production — and nobody, including me, can explain two of the functions in it. So we're paying a small tax every week for a decision we made in a hurry.\"  <em>(STAKE)</em> \"I'm not going to argue that documentation is good. You know that. I want to argue something narrower: that undocumented code costs the <em>team</em>, not the writer — which is why it never feels urgent to the person who could fix it.\"  <em>(MAP)</em> \"Three things, in the order they'll bite you: what documentation actually is, when it pays for itself, and the one rule that gets 80% of the benefit for 10% of the effort.\"  <em>(BODY STRUCTURE)</em> Problem–Solution–Benefit. Problem = undocumented code transfers cost to the team. Solution = comment the <em>why</em>, not the <em>what</em>, plus a five-line README. Benefit = faster onboarding and fewer repeated bugs. Close by returning to the teammate's two unexplained functions.</p></div>", "tag": "exam", "qHtml": "Write the full 90-second opening of a talk to your department titled <em>\"Why we should document our code\"</em>, marking hook, stake and map, and name the structure you would use for the body."}, {"q": "Your self-rating is Fluency 4, Accuracy 3, Range 2, Pronunciation 3, Coherence 4, Interaction 2. Choose one target for the next fortnight, justify it with evidence, and write a five-line practice plan plus the AI prompt you would use.", "a": "<p>Model answer:</p>\n<ul><li><strong>Target: Interaction (2).</strong> Evidence: in the rubric, interaction 2 means \"monologue or silence\" — and this is the lowest score with the highest interview cost, because a candidate who does not build on others or check understanding reads as a poor collaborator regardless of fluency. Range (2) is the second priority and can be folded into the same drill by forcing one precise word per turn.</li><li>Justification against the alternative: Pronunciation (3) is not blocking communication; fluency (4) and coherence (4) are already adequate, so drilling them yields little.</li><li><strong>Practice plan:</strong></li><li>Daily 10 min: answer one question, then add one <em>building</em> sentence (\"that connects to…\") and one <em>checking</em> question (\"does that answer it?\").</li><li>Twice weekly: 4/3/2 on the same topic, with a rule of ending each turn by handing over.</li><li>Weekly: one 15-minute conversation with a senior; ask them to note every time you interrupted or went silent.</li><li>Record and count: turns per conversation, and questions asked per conversation.</li><li>Re-rate in 14 days with written evidence per row.</li><li><strong>AI prompt:</strong> <em>\"Act as a conversation partner on 'my final-year project'. Ask me one question at a time and wait. After each of my answers, do not praise me — instead note whether I (a) built on my previous point, (b) asked you anything. At the end, give me the count of my turns and questions, and three phrases I could have used to hand the turn back.\"</em></li></ul>", "tag": "exam", "qHtml": "Your self-rating is Fluency 4, Accuracy 3, Range 2, Pronunciation 3, Coherence 4, Interaction 2. Choose one target for the next fortnight, justify it with evidence, and write a five-line practice plan plus the AI prompt you would use."}], "file": "Semester_2/CSFE-CommunicationSkills/CSFE-M02.md"}, {"n": 3, "title": "CO3 – Reading", "tagline": "Reading is the skill you will use most in four years of engineering, and almost nobody teaches it properly.", "minutes": 41, "sections": [{"kind": "big", "num": "", "title": "Big picture, 30 seconds", "html": "<p>Reading is the skill you will use most in four years of engineering, and almost nobody teaches it properly. In school you read one way: start at the top, read every word, reach the bottom. In college that method quietly fails, because the load is not just bigger — it is different in kind. In one week you might read a 12-page textbook section, a 2-page lab manual, a 6-page research paper, a 40-page datasheet, a question paper and thirty emails. Reading all of those the same way is like using a screwdriver for everything, including hammering nails.</p>\n<p>Think of reading as a bicycle with three gears, plus a torch:</p>\n<ul><li><strong>Top gear (skimming)</strong> — fast, you see the shape of the road, you will miss small stones. Fine, because you only wanted to know where the road goes.</li><li><strong>Radar (scanning)</strong> — you are not travelling at all; you are hunting one specific thing: a date, a number, a name, an error code.</li><li><strong>Low gear with a torch (intensive reading)</strong> — slow, deliberate, every sentence examined, because each line matters: a proof, a clause, a method you must reproduce.</li><li><strong>The torch also turns on your own understanding</strong> — that is critical reading: \"is this true, and who gains if I believe it?\"</li></ul>\n<p>Why it matters for marks: most \"I studied but scored less\" cases in a communication paper are not knowledge problems, they are reading problems — misreading what the question asked, missing the word <em>not</em>, answering from general knowledge instead of from the passage, or not noticing that the passage never actually said the thing.</p>"}, {"kind": "basics", "num": "", "title": "Basics you must know first", "html": "<ul><li><strong>Main idea</strong> — the one sentence the paragraph exists to support; usually first or second sentence,<p>sometimes the last.</p></li><li><strong>Topic sentence</strong> — the sentence that states a paragraph's main idea.</li><li><strong>Inference</strong> — a conclusion the text supports but never states (\"the bus was 40 minutes late\" implies irritation without anyone writing \"I was irritated\").</li><li><strong>Register</strong> — level of formality (a WhatsApp message vs a project report).</li><li><strong>Genre</strong> — type of text (textbook, paper, datasheet, blog, manual). Genre tells you where the<p>useful parts usually sit.</p></li><li><strong>Signpost words</strong> — <code>however</code>, <code>therefore</code>, <code>for example</code>, <code>in contrast</code>: the road signs of a<p>paragraph, telling you the writer is turning, proving or concluding.</p></li><li><strong>Schema</strong> — your background knowledge of the topic. More schema, faster reading, because you<p>predict correctly instead of decoding every word.</p></li></ul>\n<div class=\"divider\"></div>"}, {"kind": "concept", "num": "3.1", "title": "Skimming, scanning and intensive reading", "html": "<h4>Intuition</h4>\n<p>You walk into a large library to find material on drones. Three jobs, three behaviours. (1) You read only the shelf labels down each aisle — 30 seconds, and you know which two aisles matter. That is <strong>skimming</strong>: fast, wide, structural. (2) You open one book's index and hunt the single word \"payload\" — you are not reading the book, you are hunting a target. That is <strong>scanning</strong>. (3) You sit with the one chapter that matters and read every line with a pen, because you must explain it in a seminar tomorrow. That is <strong>intensive reading</strong>.</p>\n<p>Same library, same student, three modes. Nobody tells you to switch; you just do. The skill is switching <em>on purpose</em>, and knowing which mode the task needs.</p>\n<h4>The idea, step by step</h4>\n<p><strong>Step 1 — Skim the skeleton, not the flesh.</strong> Read the title, headings, first sentence of each paragraph, the last paragraph, and any bold or boxed text. Ignore examples, numbers and repeated explanations. In 3 minutes you should be able to say what the text is about, how it is organised, and whether it deserves a proper read. <em>Tiny example:</em> headings read <em>Introduction / Causes of packet loss / Measuring loss / Mitigation / Conclusion</em> tell you the page runs problem → measurement → solution, so you can jump straight to \"Mitigation\" later.</p>\n<p><strong>Step 2 — Scan only with a named target.</strong> Say the target to yourself first (\"operating temperature range\"), then move your eyes down the page looking only for the <em>shape</em> of the answer — digits with a degree symbol, a capitalised name, a date. Do not read sentences. <em>Tiny example:</em> scanning for \"3.3 V\" you catch digit patterns and fly past \"intended for low-voltage applications\", which is correct behaviour, because it contains no digits.</p>\n<p><strong>Step 3 — Read intensively with a question in hand.</strong> For each paragraph ask: what is the claim, what supports it, how does it link to the last one? Underline the claim, box the support, write a 5-word margin note. A page should end with 4–5 margin notes; none means you were looking at words, not reading. <em>Tiny example:</em> \"Accuracy improved by 4%, but only for inputs shorter than 50 tokens\" — the second half is where the marks live, so this sentence gets intensive treatment.</p>\n<p><strong>Step 4 — Choose the mode from the task, not from the text.</strong> The same paper is skimmed to decide whether to cite it, scanned to find its sample size, and read intensively to reproduce its method.</p>\n<div class=\"tbl-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Mode</th><th>Typical speed</th><th>Depth</th><th>Eyes do</th><th>Use when</th><th>Typical task</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Skimming</td><td>~400–800 wpm</td><td>40–60%, the shape</td><td>headings, first lines, last para</td><td>deciding relevance, previewing</td><td>\"does this support my claim?\"</td></tr><tr><td>Scanning</td><td>fastest, not measured in wpm</td><td>near zero for sentences</td><td>hunt one target string</td><td>you know the exact fact you want</td><td>\"find the 2022 figure\"</td></tr><tr><td>Intensive</td><td>~150–250 wpm</td><td>80–100%</td><td>every sentence, with a pen</td><td>each line's meaning matters</td><td>\"explain this method\"</td></tr><tr><td>Extensive (bonus)</td><td>comfortable</td><td>enough to enjoy</td><td>flowing, no pen</td><td>building vocabulary over months</td><td>a novel, popular science</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>Those speeds are typical adult ranges, not rules. What matters is the <em>ratio</em>: skimming should feel at least twice as fast as intensive reading.</p>\n<div class=\"card\"><h4>Example question</h4><p>You are handed a 6-page project report on solar-powered water pumps. You have 8 minutes to decide whether it is useful for your project, then you must find the exact efficiency figure reported for the pump used in the field trial. Which mode for which step, and in what order?</p>\n<h4>Solution</h4>\n<ol><li><strong>Minutes 0–5: skim.</strong> Title, abstract, all headings, first sentence per section, conclusion.<p><em>Reason: the skeleton tells you the scope (design? field trial? costing?) and whether it matches your project.</em></p></li><li><strong>Minute 5–6: write the decision.</strong> One line: \"Field trial of a 1 hp solar pump in Rajasthan;<p>reports efficiency and cost; relevant to my costing section.\" <em>Reason: an unwritten decision means you re-read the same report next week.</em></p></li><li><strong>Minutes 6–8: scan.</strong> Go to Results and hunt only a percentage pattern near the word<p>\"efficiency\"; do not read the surrounding prose. <em>Reason: relevance is settled, so you need one number, and scanning is the only mode that finds one number fast.</em></p></li><li><strong>Minute 8+: intensive, but only 3–4 sentences around that number</strong> — the conditions (irradiance,<p>head, flow rate). <em>Reason: an efficiency figure without its conditions is unusable and is exactly what gets marked wrong in a lab report.</em></p></li></ol>\n<div class=\"res\">skim (relevance) → scan (locate the figure) → intensive on 3–4 sentences around it (capture the conditions). Order matters: skimming before scanning stops you hunting a number inside an irrelevant report.</div>\n<p><strong>Why this works:</strong> each mode answers a different <em>type</em> of question — \"what is this?\" (skim), \"where is X?\" (scan), \"what exactly does X mean here?\" (intensive). Mixing them is the classic time-waster: students scan a report they never skimmed, so they do not know which section to scan.</p>\n<div class=\"note note--trap\"><span class=\"tag\">Common trap</span><p>\"Skimming\" is not \"reading fast\". Reading every sentence quickly is just bad intensive reading, and you remember nothing. Skimming means deliberately <em>skipping</em>. And scanning is not skimming slower — if you catch yourself understanding whole sentences while scanning, you have lost the target.</p></div>\n<div class=\"divider\"></div></div>"}, {"kind": "concept", "num": "3.2", "title": "Reading speed and comprehension", "html": "<h4>Intuition</h4>\n<p>Two students read the same 3-page handout. One finishes in 12 minutes and remembers nothing; the other finishes in 6 minutes and can explain it. The difference is not intelligence or eye speed — it is that the second student has stopped three habits that silently eat time. Think of reading as a pipeline with three leaks:</p>\n<ul><li><strong>Leak 1, your mouth.</strong> You silently \"say\" each word. Your eyes can take in 4–5 words at a glance,<p>but your inner voice manages about one word per quarter-second, so your eyes wait for your mouth. That is <strong>sub-vocalisation</strong>.</p></li><li><strong>Leak 2, your rewinding.</strong> You finish a line, doubt yourself, jump back two lines. That is<p><strong>regression</strong>, and it usually means you were not attending, not that you missed something.</p></li><li><strong>Leak 3, word-by-word reading.</strong> \"the / quick / brown / fox\" instead of \"the quick brown fox\" as one chunk — the absence of <strong>chunking</strong>.</li></ul>\n<p>Fix the leaks and speed rises with no loss of understanding. That last part is the whole point: speed without comprehension is not reading, it is page-turning.</p>\n<h4>The idea, step by step</h4>\n<p><strong>Step 1 — Measure a baseline honestly.</strong> Pick a text at your level, get its word count (a word processor shows one), read once at normal pace for understanding, time it.</p>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>WPM = words read / minutes taken\nExample: 900 words in 4 min 30 s -&gt; 4.5 min -&gt; 900 / 4.5 = 200 wpm\n\nComprehension % = (correct answers / total questions) x 100\nExample: 6 correct out of 8 -&gt; (6 / 8) x 100 = 75%</code></pre>\n<p>Use questions you did not write yourself. Typical adult silent reading of academic English is roughly 200–250 wpm at 70–80% comprehension — landmarks, not targets.</p>\n<h4>Step 2 — Combine both numbers into one honest score.</h4>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>Effective reading rate = WPM x comprehension fraction\nExample: 200 wpm at 75% -&gt; 200 x 0.75 = 150 effective wpm</code></pre>\n<p>This single number is what you actually try to improve, because it cannot be gamed.</p>\n<p><strong>Step 3 — Kill sub-vocalisation mechanically, not by willpower.</strong> Two tricks that work by occupying the inner voice: hold a pencil between your teeth, or silently count \"one, two, three\" while reading. Both feel strange for a week. The goal is not zero inner voice — for hard technical text you want some — it is to stop it being the bottleneck on easy text.</p>\n<h4>Step 4 — Chunk: three or four eye stops per line, not ten.</h4>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>Word by word : The / compiler / reports / an / error / when / the / semicolon / is / missing.\nChunked      : The compiler reports / an error when / the semicolon is missing / at the line end.</code></pre>\n<p>Four stops instead of ten, identical meaning. Practise by slash-marking a paragraph, then re-reading it once per group.</p>\n<p><strong>Step 5 — Cut regressions with a purpose.</strong> Regression is mostly a symptom of passive reading. Give yourself one question before you start (\"what does this section claim about latency?\") and your eyes stop wandering. If you truly must re-read, do it after the paragraph, not mid-sentence.</p>\n<p><strong>Step 6 — Respect the tradeoff.</strong> Push 20–30% above your comfort band and comprehension holds; push 100% above it and comprehension collapses, you re-read, and total time goes <em>up</em>. Find the fastest speed that keeps comprehension above roughly 70% <em>for that genre</em>. A datasheet tolerates a very fast scan because you only want numbers; a proof tolerates no speed at all.</p>\n<div class=\"tbl-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Leak</th><th>Looks like</th><th>Why it costs you</th><th>Fix</th><th>Time to effect</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Sub-vocalisation</td><td>lips or throat move, you \"hear\" words</td><td>caps speed near speaking rate</td><td>pencil trick, silent counting</td><td>1–2 weeks</td></tr><tr><td>Regression</td><td>eyes jump back mid-sentence</td><td>same words read 2–3 times</td><td>purpose per paragraph</td><td>1 week</td></tr><tr><td>No chunking</td><td>one fixation per word</td><td>3–4x too many eye stops</td><td>slash-marked phrases</td><td>2–3 weeks</td></tr><tr><td>Passive reading</td><td>eyes move, mind drifts</td><td>comprehension under 50%, so speed is wasted</td><td>margin notes, a question first</td><td>immediate</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"card\"><h4>Example question</h4><p>You read a 600-word passage in 3 minutes and answer 6 of 8 comprehension questions correctly. On a similar passage you push harder: 600 words in 2 minutes, but only 5 of 8 correct. Which read was actually more efficient, and what should you change?</p>\n<h4>Solution</h4>\n<ol><li><strong>Passage 1 speed:</strong> <code>600 / 3 = 200 wpm</code>. <em>Reason: WPM is words divided by minutes.</em></li><li><strong>Passage 1 comprehension:</strong> <code>(6 / 8) x 100 = 75%</code>. <em>Reason: correct over total, as a percentage.</em></li><li><strong>Passage 1 effective rate:</strong> <code>200 x 0.75 = 150 effective wpm</code>. <em>Reason: multiplying stops a fast-but-wrong read from looking good.</em></li><li><strong>Passage 2 speed:</strong> <code>600 / 2 = 300 wpm</code>; <strong>comprehension</strong> <code>(5 / 8) x 100 = 62.5%</code>;<p><strong>effective rate</strong> <code>300 x 0.625 = 187.5 effective wpm</code>. <em>Reason: same formula, so the two reads are comparable.</em></p></li><li><strong>Compare:</strong> 187.5 &gt; 150, so the faster read was genuinely more efficient — but 62.5% is below the ~70% floor, which means comprehension is now the risk, not speed.</li><li><strong>What to change:</strong> hold around 250–270 wpm on this genre and recover the lost marks by scanning<p>back for the two guessed answers. <em>Reason: you want the highest effective rate that still clears 70%.</em></p></li></ol>\n<div class=\"res\">Passage 1 = 200 wpm at 75% = <strong>150 effective wpm</strong>; Passage 2 = 300 wpm at 62.5% = <strong>187.5 effective wpm</strong>. Passage 2 wins on efficiency, but its comprehension has dropped below the safe band, so settle near 250–270 wpm and verify guessed answers by scanning rather than pushing speed further.</div>\n<p><strong>Why this works:</strong> it replaces the vague feeling \"I read faster now\" with numbers you can track weekly, and it blocks the common self-deception where speed gains are really comprehension losses.</p>\n<div class=\"note note--trap\"><span class=\"tag\">Common trap</span><p>Treating wpm as a score. A student at 400 wpm and 50% comprehension is <em>slower in real terms</em> than one at 200 wpm and 85%, because the first must read everything twice. Never quote a speed without its comprehension figure.</p></div>\n<div class=\"divider\"></div></div>"}, {"kind": "concept", "num": "3.3", "title": "Technical and academic texts", "html": "<h4>Intuition</h4>\n<p>A research paper is not written in the order you should read it. The author writes Introduction → Method → Results → Discussion, because that is the logic of an argument; a reader with 20 minutes should read almost backwards. It is like arriving at a railway station: you look for the platform board and the exit signs first, not the architect's drawings. A <strong>datasheet</strong> is a reference table with prose sprinkled on it, so you scan it like a dictionary. A <strong>lab manual</strong> is a recipe: read it before you start, never during.</p>\n<h4>The idea, step by step</h4>\n<p><strong>Step 1 — Know the five rooms of a paper.</strong> Abstract = what was done and found (80% of your citation decision). Introduction = why it matters and what is missing (the gap is in its <em>last</em> paragraph). Method = how it was done. Results = what the numbers show. Discussion/Conclusion = what it means, plus limits and future work.</p>\n<p><strong>Step 2 — Read a paper in 20 minutes in three passes.</strong> A widely used approach, and the timings below are a workable version of it:</p>\n<div class=\"tbl-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Minutes</th><th>Pass</th><th>You do</th><th>Output</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>0–2</td><td>reconnaissance</td><td>title, abstract, keywords, all headings, last para of conclusion</td><td>one line: what is this about?</td></tr><tr><td>2–6</td><td>skeleton</td><td>every figure and table caption, last para of introduction</td><td>one line: claimed contribution?</td></tr><tr><td>6–14</td><td>evidence</td><td>results figures properly; method only where you doubt something</td><td>three notes: numbers, method, limitation</td></tr><tr><td>14–17</td><td>trust check</td><td>sample size, baselines, assumptions, who funded it</td><td>one line: do I believe it?</td></tr><tr><td>17–20</td><td>write and decide</td><td>4-line summary in your own words</td><td>note card filed: keep / discard</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>Most papers do not survive this, and that is the point — 20 minutes, not three hours. If one survives and matters, <em>then</em> read it intensively.</p>\n<p><strong>Step 3 — Read a datasheet by columns.</strong> Everything sits under <strong>Min / Typ / Max</strong>. <code>Typ</code> means \"typically measured\" — it is <em>not</em> a guarantee; only <code>Min</code> and <code>Max</code> are guaranteed, and only over the stated test conditions. So: read <strong>Absolute Maximum Ratings</strong> first (what destroys the part), then <strong>Electrical Characteristics</strong> (what you may design with) with its conditions row, then the pin description.</p>\n<p><strong>Step 4 — Read a lab manual as a procedure with variables.</strong> Before the lab, mark four things: the aim, the independent variable (what you change), the dependent variable (what you measure), and the precautions (a list that exists because a previous batch broke something). In the lab you only glance at the observation table.</p>\n<div class=\"card\"><h4>Example question</h4><p>You have 20 minutes to decide whether this paper is worth a full read for your mini-project on reducing mobile-app battery drain. Give the pass-by-pass outputs and the decision.</p>\n<div class=\"note note--why\"><span class=\"tag\">Note</span><p><em>Abstract (sample text):</em> \"Background: background location tracking is a major source of battery drain in Android apps, yet developers rarely measure its cost. Method: we instrumented 40 popular apps and logged location-request frequency over 14 days on 12 devices. Results: apps requesting location every 30 seconds consumed 11–17% more battery per hour than apps batching requests every 5 minutes, with no measurable loss in delivered accuracy. We propose a batching guideline and a linter that flags high-frequency requesters. Conclusion: batching is a cheap, general fix, though our sample covers only foreground location requests.\"</p></div>\n<h4>Solution</h4>\n<ol><li><strong>Pass 1 (0–2 min):</strong> \"Battery cost of frequent location requests in Android apps, measured on 40<p>apps and 12 devices.\" <em>Reason: the abstract's first two sentences carry topic and scale.</em></p></li><li><strong>Pass 2 (2–6 min):</strong> two contributions — an empirical measurement (11–17% extra drain) and a tool<p>plus guideline (batch every 5 minutes). <em>Reason: \"we propose\" marks the contribution, \"we instrumented\" marks the evidence.</em></p></li><li><strong>Pass 3 (6–14 min):</strong> check the baseline behind 11–17% (same apps with batching on?), then skim<p>the method for one thing only — was battery measured by software estimate or hardware meter? <em>Reason: the number transfers to your project only if the measurement method is comparable.</em></p></li><li><strong>Trust check (14–17 min):</strong> 40 apps, 12 devices, 14 days is reasonable but narrow, and the<p>authors themselves flag the foreground-only limit. <em>Reason: stated limits are the cheapest honest critique you will ever get.</em></p></li><li><strong>Decide (17–20 min):</strong> keep, read Method and Results fully later. Card: \"11–17% drain, 30 s polling vs 5 min batching; 40 apps / 12 devices / 14 days; limit: foreground only.\"</li></ol>\n<div class=\"res\"><strong>keep, read fully later.</strong> The 20 minutes already produced the two things you need — a number to compare against and a method to borrow — plus the authors' own limitation.</div>\n<p><strong>Why this works:</strong> it never reads the paper in the order it was written, and it ends in a <em>decision plus a note</em>, the only output that survives the week.</p>\n<div class=\"note note--trap\"><span class=\"tag\">Common trap</span><p>Quoting <code>Typ</code> as a guaranteed value, or quoting a headline percentage without its test conditions — both give numbers that are correct on the page and wrong in your report. Second trap: reading Method first because it \"feels like the real content\"; you cannot judge which details matter until you know the claim.</p></div>\n<div class=\"divider\"></div></div>"}, {"kind": "concept", "num": "3.4", "title": "Vocabulary in context", "html": "<h4>Intuition</h4>\n<p>Someone at a wedding says \"he is my mother's brother's son\". You have never heard his name, yet you know exactly who he is — from the <em>structure</em> of the description. Words work the same way. You rarely need a dictionary; you need to look at what surrounds the word. Two sources of clues: <strong>inside the word</strong> (morphology: <code>un-</code> + <code>predict</code> + <code>-able</code> = not able to be predicted) and <strong>around the word</strong> (context: the sentence often defines, contrasts, exemplifies or causes the word, if you know where to look).</p>\n<h4>The idea, step by step</h4>\n<h4>Step 1 — Split prefix + root + suffix.</h4>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>in + comprehens + ible  -&gt; not able to be comprehended = impossible to understand\nhyper + tension         -&gt; tension above normal\nmono + lith + ic        -&gt; \"one stone\" -&gt; cut from a single block</code></pre>\n<h4>Step 2 — Learn the four context clue types.</h4>\n<div class=\"tbl-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Clue</th><th>Signal words</th><th>Example</th><th>Inference</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Definition</td><td><code>is</code>, <code>means</code>, <code>that is</code>, <code>:</code>, <code>—</code></td><td>\"The cache is <em>volatile</em>: it loses its contents when power is cut.\"</td><td>loses data without power</td></tr><tr><td>Contrast</td><td><code>but</code>, <code>unlike</code>, <code>however</code>, <code>rather than</code></td><td>\"Unlike the earlier <em>brittle</em> design, the new one tolerates a node failure.\"</td><td>brittle = fails completely under stress</td></tr><tr><td>Example</td><td><code>such as</code>, <code>for example</code>, <code>including</code></td><td>\"Ephemeral data, such as a session token, is discarded at logout.\"</td><td>ephemeral = short-lived</td></tr><tr><td>Cause–effect</td><td><code>because</code>, <code>therefore</code>, <code>so</code>, <code>as a result</code></td><td>\"The system is <em>stateless</em>; therefore any server can serve the next request.\"</td><td>keeps no memory of past requests</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h4>Step 3 — Keep a prefix/suffix toolbox in your head.</h4>\n<div class=\"tbl-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Prefix</th><th>Meaning</th><th>Example</th><th>Suffix</th><th>Meaning</th><th>Example</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>un-, in-, non-</td><td>not</td><td>unsafe</td><td>-able, -ible</td><td>can be</td><td>portable</td></tr><tr><td>re-</td><td>again</td><td>rebuild</td><td>-less</td><td>without</td><td>stateless</td></tr><tr><td>pre-, post-</td><td>before, after</td><td>preprocess</td><td>-ous, -ive</td><td>having the quality</td><td>porous, adaptive</td></tr><tr><td>sub-, super-</td><td>under, above</td><td>subset</td><td>-fy, -ise/-ize</td><td>to make</td><td>simplify</td></tr><tr><td>inter-, intra-</td><td>between, within</td><td>interconnect</td><td>-tion, -ment</td><td>act or result of</td><td>allocation</td></tr><tr><td>mono-, multi-</td><td>one, many</td><td>multithreaded</td><td>-ity</td><td>state of being</td><td>scalability</td></tr><tr><td>micro-, macro-</td><td>small, large</td><td>microservice</td><td>-logy, -graphy</td><td>study, writing</td><td>topology</td></tr><tr><td>anti-, co-</td><td>against, together</td><td>co-processor</td><td>-meter, -scope</td><td>measure, view</td><td>thermometer</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><strong>Step 4 — Watch false friends and Indian-English habits.</strong> A <strong>false friend</strong> looks familiar but means something else in standard English. <code>actually</code> = \"in fact\", <strong>not</strong> \"currently\" (\"Actually I am in second year\" should be \"I am currently in second year\"). <code>eventually</code> = \"in the end, after a long time\", not \"possibly\". <code>doubt</code> = uncertainty, not a question — \"I have a doubt\" is understood in India, but write \"I have a question\" for an international reader. <code>revert</code> (to return to a previous state) is used for \"reply\" only in Indian English; <code>prepone</code> is an Indian coinage (standard: \"bring forward\"); <code>out of station</code> → \"out of town\"; <code>kindly do the needful</code> → \"please take the necessary action\". None of these are crimes — they mark a variety of English — but in formal academic writing and international exams the standard form is safer.</p>\n<div class=\"card\"><h4>Example question</h4><p>Using only the sentences, work out what <em>idempotent</em> and <em>amortised</em> mean and name the clue type used for each.</p>\n<div class=\"note note--why\"><span class=\"tag\">Note</span><p>\"A good retry mechanism needs an <em>idempotent</em> operation: if the request is sent twice, the effect on the database must be the same as sending it once. This is why the team rejected the naive 'increment counter' endpoint, whose cost was <em>amortised</em> over thousands of retries because each retry silently doubled the count.\"</p></div>\n<h4>Solution</h4>\n<ol><li><strong>Find the definition of <em>idempotent</em>.</strong> The colon introduces the explanation, and \"must be the<p>same as sending it once\" <em>is</em> the definition. Clue: <strong>definition (punctuation)</strong>. <em>Reason: a colon after a term almost always announces \"here is what it means\".</em></p></li><li><strong>Paraphrase immediately:</strong> doing it twice has the same result as doing it once. <em>Reason: a word left unparaphrased stays a label with nothing behind it.</em></li><li><strong>Attack <em>amortised</em> by morphology:</strong> <code>a-</code> (to) + <code>mort</code> (death, Latin) + <code>-ise</code> (to make) — to<p>bring to death, i.e. to pay off or spread out until gone. <em>Reason: the root gives a direction.</em></p></li><li><strong>Confirm with context:</strong> \"cost was amortised over thousands of retries because each retry doubled<p>the count\" — cost spread across many retries. Clue: <strong>cause–effect (<code>because</code>)</strong>. <em>Reason: two independent clues agreeing is a strong inference.</em></p></li><li><strong>Substitute back:</strong> \"the cost was spread out over thousands of retries\" fits without contradiction. <em>Reason: substitution is the only real test of a guess.</em></li></ol>\n<div class=\"res\"><em>idempotent</em> = an operation whose repeated application gives the same result as one application (definition clue, colon). <em>amortised</em> = a cost spread out or paid off gradually across many repetitions (morphology <code>a- + mort + -ise</code>, confirmed by <code>because</code>).</div>\n<p><strong>Why this works:</strong> it requires two independent evidence sources before committing to a meaning, then tests the guess by substitution — so the answer is checkable, not a feeling.</p>\n<div class=\"note note--trap\"><span class=\"tag\">Common trap</span><p>Guessing from the <em>sound</em> of a word rather than its parts — <code>inflammable</code> means \"easily set on fire\", not \"not flammable\". And one clue is not proof: if the definition clue and the contrast clue disagree, re-read the whole paragraph before writing a meaning down.</p></div>\n<div class=\"divider\"></div></div>"}, {"kind": "concept", "num": "3.5", "title": "Critical reading", "html": "<h4>Intuition</h4>\n<p>A shopkeeper says \"this phone has the best camera in the market\". You do not write that in your project report. You ask: best according to whom, tested how, and does he earn more if I buy this one? That reflex — <em>who says so, on what evidence, and what do they gain</em> — is critical reading. It is not being negative; it is being an engineer instead of a photocopier. Critical reading separates four things ordinary reading blends: <strong>claim</strong> (what the author wants you to believe), <strong>evidence</strong> (the data or citations offered), <strong>assumption</strong> (the unstated belief that must hold), and <strong>bias</strong> (the interest that shaped which evidence was chosen).</p>\n<h4>The idea, step by step</h4>\n<p><strong>Step 1 — State the claim in one sentence:</strong> \"The author claims that ___.\" If you cannot fill the blank, you have not found it yet.</p>\n<p><strong>Step 2 — Separate claim from evidence.</strong> Evidence looks like numbers, sample sizes, controlled comparisons, named studies, sourced quotations. Non-evidence: \"obviously\", \"everyone knows\", \"studies show\" (which studies?), anecdotes, authority without data.</p>\n<p><strong>Step 3 — Name the assumption.</strong> Test: if it were false, would the claim collapse? Claim \"our app cut study time by 30%\", evidence \"users reported studying less\" — the assumption is that self-reported time is accurate <em>and</em> that less time means the same learning. Both are shaky, and that is where a good critique lives.</p>\n<p><strong>Step 4 — Check bias and framing.</strong> Who paid? What is measured, and what is conveniently <em>not</em> measured? Which comparison was chosen (\"compared to our older version\" is a much easier baseline than \"compared to the market leader\")? Watch loaded words: <em>revolutionary</em>, <em>seamless</em>, <em>industry-leading</em> are marketing, not evidence.</p>\n<h4>Step 5 — Run the five-point source check.</h4>\n<div class=\"tbl-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Check</th><th>Question</th><th>Red flag</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Authority</td><td>who wrote it, with what credentials?</td><td>no author, no affiliation</td></tr><tr><td>Currency</td><td>when was it published?</td><td>undated page, or old for a fast field</td></tr><tr><td>Accuracy</td><td>are claims backed by checkable data?</td><td>round numbers, no method, no sample size</td></tr><tr><td>Purpose</td><td>informing, selling or persuading?</td><td>a company blog praising its own product</td></tr><tr><td>Audience</td><td>who is it written for?</td><td>simplified for the public, quoted as research</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"card\"><h4>Example question</h4><p>A company blog says: \"AI will replace 40% of software engineering jobs by 2030 — our survey of 500 developers proves it.\" Write a four-part critical evaluation (claim, evidence, assumption, verdict).</p>\n<h4>Solution</h4>\n<ol><li><strong>Claim:</strong> \"40% of software engineering jobs will disappear by 2030 because of AI.\" <em>Reason: strip the persuasive wording before judging anything.</em></li><li><strong>Evidence:</strong> a survey of 500 developers — but a survey of developers' <em>opinions</em> is not evidence<p>about <em>job numbers</em>; nothing described counts jobs. <em>Reason: match the evidence type to the claim type; opinions cannot support a headcount forecast.</em></p></li><li><strong>Assumption:</strong> that developers' predictions are accurate forecasts, and that 500 self-selected<p>respondents represent the global profession. <em>Reason: the claim collapses if either fails.</em></p></li><li><strong>Bias and purpose:</strong> the author sells AI tooling, so a dramatic forecast serves the business, and<p>\"proves it\" pre-empts doubt. <em>Reason: purpose explains the choice of evidence and the certainty of tone.</em></p></li><li><strong>Verdict:</strong> unreliable as evidence; usable at most as an illustration of industry anxiety, and<p>only if labelled as opinion. <em>Reason: a critique must end in a decision — cite it or do not.</em></p></li></ol>\n<div class=\"res\">claim = 40% job loss by 2030; evidence = a 500-developer opinion survey, which cannot support a headcount forecast; assumption = developer predictions are accurate and the sample is representative; verdict = <strong>not citable as evidence of job loss</strong>.</div>\n<p><strong>Why this works:</strong> it keeps the three levels apart — what is said, what is shown, what is smuggled in — and converts criticism into a decision instead of a complaint.</p>\n<div class=\"note note--trap\"><span class=\"tag\">Common trap</span><p>Confusing \"I disagree with this\" with \"this is badly evidenced\". A paper whose conclusion you dislike can be methodologically excellent, and one you agree with can be junk. Attack the link between evidence and claim, not the claim's politics.</p></div>\n<div class=\"divider\"></div></div>"}, {"kind": "concept", "num": "3.6", "title": "Proficiency-test reading tasks", "html": "<h4>Intuition</h4>\n<p>IELTS, TOEFL and PTE do not test whether you understood the passage; they test whether you can <em>prove</em> a specific claim about it, in a fixed format, under time pressure. That is a different game, and it has known tricks: every task type has one signature trap, and once you know it you stop losing the same two marks every attempt. Formats change over the years — IELTS Academic Reading is currently 3 passages / 40 questions / 60 minutes, and the shorter TOEFL iBT format uses 2 passages of 10 questions in about 35 minutes — so confirm the current pattern on the official site; treat any pattern printed in a coaching book as a claim to check.</p>\n<h4>The idea, step by step</h4>\n<p><strong>Step 1 — Read the questions before the passage</strong> (for most task types). Knowing that question 4 is about the year 2019 turns the passage into a scanning exercise.</p>\n<h4>Step 2 — Learn the signature trap of each type.</h4>\n<div class=\"tbl-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Task type</th><th>Asks</th><th>Signature trap</th><th>Rule that saves you</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>True / False / Not Given</td><td>agree, disagree, or silent?</td><td>treating Not Given as False</td><td>FALSE needs the passage to <em>contradict</em>; no contradicting words means NOT GIVEN</td></tr><tr><td>Yes / No / Not Given</td><td>same, about the writer's <em>views</em></td><td>confusing fact with opinion</td><td>Y/N/NG is opinion, T/F/NG is fact</td></tr><tr><td>Matching headings</td><td>one heading per paragraph</td><td>matching a heading to a word that appears there</td><td>the heading must cover the main idea, not a detail</td></tr><tr><td>Summary completion</td><td>fill gaps from the passage</td><td>copying words when the gap needs a grammar change</td><td>the finished sentence must be grammatical <em>and</em> meaning-identical</td></tr><tr><td>Matching information</td><td>which paragraph mentions X</td><td>answering from memory</td><td>mark the exact line before answering</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><strong>Step 3 — Use the pointer test for Not Given.</strong> Put a finger on the sentence that decides the answer. If your finger has nothing to rest on, it is NOT GIVEN. No exceptions, no \"it is implied somewhere\".</p>\n<p><strong>Step 4 — Budget per passage, not per question.</strong> For 60 minutes and 40 questions that is roughly 20 minutes a passage including answer transfer. If a question has eaten 90 seconds, guess, mark it, move.</p>\n<div class=\"card\"><h4>Example question</h4><p>Read the passage and answer (a) three True/False/Not Given statements, (b) the best heading, (c) the summary gaps.</p>\n<div class=\"note note--why\"><span class=\"tag\">Note</span><p>\"Recycling of lithium-ion batteries has lagged far behind their production. Although collection schemes exist in the European Union, only about a fifth of spent batteries are currently recycled there. The main obstacle is economic rather than technical: hydrometallurgical processes can recover over 90% of lithium, cobalt and nickel, but the recovered material often costs more than newly mined metal. Several start-ups are therefore testing direct recycling, which preserves the cathode structure instead of dissolving it. Whether this approach can scale remains unclear.\"</p></div>\n<div class=\"note note--why\"><span class=\"tag\">Note</span><p>(a) i. Battery recycling has not kept pace with battery manufacturing. ii. Technical limits are the biggest barrier to recycling. iii. Direct recycling has been shown to work at industrial scale. (b) Headings: (i) Why recycling is uneconomic (ii) The chemistry of lithium extraction (iii) Battery demand in Europe (c) \"Although over 90% of key metals can be recovered by ______ processes, the recovered material often costs more than ______ metal.\"</p></div>\n<h4>Solution</h4>\n<ol><li><strong>(a) i = TRUE.</strong> \"has lagged far behind their production\" says the same thing as \"has not kept pace\". <em>Reason: same meaning, different words — that is what TRUE looks like.</em></li><li><strong>(a) ii = FALSE.</strong> The passage says the obstacle is \"economic rather than technical\". *Reason:<p>FALSE needs an actual contradiction, and \"rather than technical\" contradicts the statement.*</p></li><li><strong>(a) iii = NOT GIVEN.</strong> The passage says scaling \"remains unclear\" — neither confirmed nor denied.<p><em>Reason: the pointer test fails, and nothing contradicts it, so it cannot be FALSE either.</em></p></li><li><strong>(b) = (i).</strong> The spine is: recycling is rare → because recovered material costs more than mined metal. <em>Reason: (ii) covers one clause only and (iii) is never mentioned.</em></li><li><strong>(c) gap 1 = \"hydrometallurgical\"</strong> (the named process recovering over 90%); **gap 2 = \"newly<p>mined\"** (copy the passage's phrase so the contrast survives). <em>Reason: grammatical fit plus identical meaning.</em></p></li></ol>\n<div class=\"res\">(a) TRUE, FALSE, NOT GIVEN; (b) heading (i); (c) hydrometallurgical; newly mined.</div>\n<p><strong>Why this works:</strong> every answer is anchored to a phrase you could underline, and the \"no evidence\" case was settled by the pointer test rather than by intuition.</p>\n<div class=\"note note--trap\"><span class=\"tag\">Common trap</span><p>Over-thinking T/F/NG. Students find a sentence that merely <em>hints</em> at the statement and mark TRUE, or mark FALSE because \"the passage doesn't say that\" — which is the definition of NOT GIVEN. Two checks: can I underline support? → TRUE. Can I underline a contradiction? → FALSE. Neither? → NOT GIVEN. Write answers exactly as instructed (<code>TRUE</code>, not <code>T</code> and not <code>True, because…</code>).</p></div>\n<div class=\"divider\"></div></div>"}, {"kind": "concept", "num": "3.7", "title": "Research-based reading", "html": "<h4>Intuition</h4>\n<p>You would not build a project by copying one repository blindly; you would compare several and see where they agree. Literature reading is the same — the unit of work is not \"the paper\", it is <strong>the claim</strong>, and your job is to find out what several independent sources say about that one claim. Three habits make it fast: a <strong>search strategy</strong> (so you find the right ten papers, not the first ten), <strong>note cards</strong> (so you never re-read a paper), and <strong>synthesis</strong> (so your paragraph shows a conversation, not a list).</p>\n<h4>The idea, step by step</h4>\n<p><strong>Step 1 — Search with a strategy.</strong> Turn the topic into 2–4 keyword groups and combine with Boolean operators: <code>AND</code> narrows, <code>OR</code> widens with synonyms, <code>NOT</code> excludes.</p>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>(\"flipped classroom\" OR \"inverted classroom\") AND (engineering OR \"technical education\")\nAND (India OR \"Indian colleges\")</code></pre>\n<p>Then filter by year and type, and <strong>snowball</strong>: open the best paper's reference list and go backwards; open \"cited by\" and go forwards. Engineering databases commonly used: IEEE Xplore, ACM Digital Library, Scopus, Web of Science, Google Scholar.</p>\n<h4>Step 2 — Fill a note card immediately, five fields.</h4>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>SOURCE     : Menon &amp; Rao (2022), journal, vol(issue), pages\nCLAIM      : flipped classroom raised end-semester marks ~6% vs lecture-only\nEVIDENCE   : 210 second-year students, one circuits course, one institution\nLIMIT      : authors admit single-institution sample\nMY COMMENT : good for the \"marks\" angle; too small for general claims\nPAGE       : p. 214 (so the exact number can be cited later)</code></pre>\n<p>If you cannot fill CLAIM and EVIDENCE, you have not read it yet — do not file the card.</p>\n<p><strong>Step 3 — Synthesise with three moves.</strong> A synthesis paragraph <strong>agrees and adds</strong> (\"both report a small gain, and Y adds that it persists\"), <strong>extends</strong> (\"Z shows it holds in large classes\"), or <strong>qualifies</strong> (\"however, Y warns that most studies measure marks only\"). Never write \"Author A says… Author B says… Author C says…\" — that is a list, not a synthesis.</p>\n<p><strong>Step 4 — Avoid plagiarism <em>while</em> reading.</strong> Two rules: <strong>close the source before you paraphrase</strong> (if the original is on screen your eyes copy its sentence structure even when you change the words), and keep the page number, because you cannot cite a number you cannot locate. Quote only when exact wording matters, keep quotes under roughly a tenth of your text, and mark them with quotation marks plus a citation.</p>\n<div class=\"card\"><h4>Example question</h4><p>Using these two note cards, write a three-sentence synthesis paragraph for a report on flipped classrooms in Indian engineering colleges, with correct attribution.</p>\n<div class=\"note note--why\"><span class=\"tag\">Note</span><p>Card A — Menon &amp; Rao (2022): 210 second-year students, one circuits course, one institution; flipped classroom raised end-semester marks by about 6% over lecture-only; authors caution that the sample is single-institution. Card B — Iyer (2023): review of 34 studies in Indian engineering colleges; effects positive but small (2–8%); most studies measure marks only, not lab or design skills.</p></div>\n<h4>Solution</h4>\n<ol><li><strong>Sentence 1, agreement:</strong> \"Both Menon and Rao (2022) and Iyer (2023) report that flipping an<p>engineering classroom produces a measurable but modest improvement in student marks.\" <em>Reason: start with what the sources share; that is the finding you can state confidently.</em></p></li><li><strong>Sentence 2, attributed figures:</strong> \"Menon and Rao found a gain of about 6% in a single circuits<p>course, while Iyer's review of 34 studies places the range at 2–8%.\" <em>Reason: attach each figure to its source so the reader knows which one generalises.</em></p></li><li><strong>Sentence 3, qualification that opens your contribution:</strong> \"Iyer notes, however, that almost all<p>of this evidence rests on marks rather than lab or design skills, a gap this study addresses by measuring lab performance.\" <em>Reason: the limitation is where your own work enters; without it the paragraph is a summary, not a synthesis.</em></p></li></ol>\n<div class=\"res\">the three-sentence paragraph above — agreement, attributed figures, then a qualification that opens your own contribution. (Author names and years are placeholders showing the format; use your own real sources.)</div>\n<p><strong>Why this works:</strong> every number carries its source, the sources talk to each other instead of queuing, and the last sentence turns reading into an argument for your project.</p>\n<div class=\"note note--trap\"><span class=\"tag\">Common trap</span><p>\"Patchwriting\" — keeping the original sentence and swapping synonyms (\"the main obstacle is economic rather than technical\" → \"the primary barrier is financial instead of technical\"). The structure and idea-order are still copied, so it is still plagiarism. Close the tab, write from your note card, then check the facts against the source.</p></div>\n<div class=\"divider\"></div></div>"}, {"kind": "concept", "num": "3.8", "title": "AI-assisted comprehension and self-assessment", "html": "<h4>Intuition</h4>\n<p>A chatbot's summary is a <em>hypothesis about the text</em>, not a fact about it. It is fast, fluent, and confidently wrong exactly where the text was subtle — numbers, negations, who did what to whom. Used well, AI is a tireless study partner: it summarises, quizzes, simplifies a hard sentence, gives a second opinion. Used badly, it replaces the reading and you walk into the exam holding a summary of a summary. The safe pattern never changes: <strong>read first, summarise yourself, then compare.</strong></p>\n<h4>The idea, step by step</h4>\n<p><strong>Step 1 — Read and write your own 3-sentence summary before opening any tool.</strong> That summary is your control; without it you cannot tell a good AI summary from a plausible one.</p>\n<p><strong>Step 2 — Ask for a fixed shape, then diff.</strong> \"Three sentences, no opinions, keep every number.\" Then compare line by line with four checks, in this order: <strong>numbers</strong> (did a digit change?), <strong>negations</strong> (did \"under-rated\" become \"over-rated\"?), <strong>who/what</strong> (did actor and object swap?), <strong>certainty</strong> (did \"may help\" become \"helps\"?).</p>\n<p><strong>Step 3 — Use it as an examiner, not an author.</strong> Good prompts: \"Give me 8 comprehension questions on this passage, hardest last, answers hidden.\" \"Explain this sentence in simpler English without changing its meaning.\" \"Which claim here has the weakest evidence, and why?\" Then check the answers against the passage yourself — a wrong quiz teaches you wrong things.</p>\n<p><strong>Step 4 — Never let it write your citation.</strong> An AI can invent a reference that reads perfectly and does not exist. Every source you cite must be one you have opened yourself.</p>\n<h4>Step 5 — Score yourself on a rubric, not a feeling.</h4>\n<div class=\"tbl-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Criterion</th><th>Beginner</th><th>Competent</th><th>Proficient</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Main idea</td><td>gets the topic, not the claim</td><td>states the claim in one sentence</td><td>states claim plus scope and limits</td></tr><tr><td>Detail accuracy</td><td>confuses similar facts</td><td>recalls key numbers</td><td>recalls numbers with their conditions</td></tr><tr><td>Inference</td><td>guesses beyond the text</td><td>separates stated from implied</td><td>justifies each inference with a line</td></tr><tr><td>Vocabulary</td><td>needs a dictionary for each unknown</td><td>uses context clues</td><td>uses morphology and context together</td></tr><tr><td>Critical stance</td><td>accepts everything</td><td>spots unsupported claims</td><td>names the assumption and the bias</td></tr><tr><td>Speed</td><td>under 150 wpm</td><td>200–250 wpm</td><td>250+ wpm at 70%+ comprehension</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"card\"><h4>Example question</h4><p>A student had an AI summarise the passage below and then compared it with the original. Find every error and say which of the four checks caught it.</p>\n<div class=\"note note--why\"><span class=\"tag\">Note</span><p><em>Original:</em> \"In a 2023 trial at a Bengaluru hospital, a triage chatbot was tested on 1,200 patient descriptions. It matched the nurse's urgency rating in 84% of cases, but under-rated urgency in 9% of cases — the errors concentrated in descriptions written in Kannada. The authors concluded that the tool was usable only as a second opinion, and recommended further work on multilingual input.\"  <em>AI summary:</em> \"A 2023 Bengaluru hospital trial tested a triage chatbot on 1,200 patient descriptions. It matched nurses' urgency ratings in 84% of cases and over-rated urgency in 9% of cases, mostly for English descriptions. The authors concluded the tool could replace first-line triage and recommended more work on multilingual input.\"</p></div>\n<h4>Solution</h4>\n<ol><li><strong>Numbers first:</strong> 2023, 1,200 and 84% all survive unchanged. <em>Reason: numbers are the cheapest thing to verify and the most damaging when wrong.</em></li><li><strong>Negation check:</strong> original \"under-rated urgency\", summary \"over-rated urgency\" — <strong>error 1, direction flipped.</strong> <em>Reason: a flipped direction reverses the safety implication.</em></li><li><strong>Who/what check:</strong> original errors \"concentrated in descriptions written in Kannada\", summary<p>\"mostly for English descriptions\" — <strong>error 2, a substituted specific.</strong> <em>Reason: substituted details are the commonest hallucination.</em></p></li><li><strong>Certainty check:</strong> original \"usable only as a second opinion\", summary \"could replace first-line<p>triage\" — <strong>error 3, a hedge became a promotion.</strong> <em>Reason: this reverses the paper's actual recommendation.</em></p></li><li><strong>Judge the artefact:</strong> two of three sentences carry errors, one of which inverts the conclusion, so<p>the summary is unusable — it would have produced a wrong literature note. <em>Reason: judge, then decide to fix or discard.</em></p></li></ol>\n<div class=\"res\">three errors — (1) \"over-rated\" should be \"under-rated\" (negation check), (2) \"English\" should be \"Kannada\" (who/what check), (3) \"could replace first-line triage\" should be \"usable only as a second opinion\" (certainty check). The numbers were all correct, which is exactly why a careless reader would have trusted it.</div>\n<p><strong>Why this works:</strong> it turns \"is this summary good?\" into four mechanical checks that need no expertise, and it exposes the real danger — summaries fail in the direction of sounding <em>more</em> confident than the source.</p>\n<div class=\"note note--trap\"><span class=\"tag\">Common trap</span><p>Answering exam questions from the AI summary instead of the passage. It reads better than the original, so it feels more reliable; it is not. Summaries also flatten hedges (\"may\", \"suggests\", \"in this sample\"), and exams test exactly those words.</p></div>\n<div class=\"divider\"></div></div>"}, {"kind": "concept", "num": "3.1", "title": "Summary table — which reading approach when?", "html": "<div class=\"tbl-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Your task</th><th>Mode</th><th>Time guide</th><th>Output you should have</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>\"Is this source relevant?\"</td><td>skim</td><td>3–5 min</td><td>one-line relevance verdict</td></tr><tr><td>\"Find the 2022 efficiency figure\"</td><td>scan</td><td>1–2 min</td><td>the number plus its conditions</td></tr><tr><td>\"Reproduce or explain this method\"</td><td>intensive</td><td>as long as it takes</td><td>margin notes, then a paraphrase</td></tr><tr><td>\"Improve my reading speed\"</td><td>timed easy text</td><td>10 min/day</td><td>wpm + comprehension % + effective rate</td></tr><tr><td>\"Review a paper\"</td><td>three passes</td><td>20 min</td><td>4-line note card</td></tr><tr><td>\"Unknown word in a technical text\"</td><td>morphology + context</td><td>30 s</td><td>guessed meaning, substituted back</td></tr><tr><td>\"Should I trust this blog/paper?\"</td><td>critical read</td><td>10 min</td><td>claim, evidence, assumption, verdict</td></tr><tr><td>\"IELTS/TOEFL question set\"</td><td>questions first, then targeted scan</td><td>~20 min per passage</td><td>answer written exactly as instructed</td></tr><tr><td>\"Build a literature base\"</td><td>keyword search + snowball + cards</td><td>45 min per 5 papers</td><td>5 filled cards</td></tr><tr><td>\"Check my understanding\"</td><td>own summary → AI summary → diff</td><td>10 min</td><td>list of discrepancies</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"}, {"kind": "example", "num": "", "title": "Solved examples", "html": "<p><strong>Example 1 (easy) — vocabulary in context.</strong> In \"The protocol is <em>lossy</em>: frames may be dropped when the buffer overflows, so video plays smoothly but fine detail disappears\", what does <em>lossy</em> mean and which clue told you?</p>\n<p>Solution: (1) The colon after <em>lossy</em> announces a definition — <strong>definition clue</strong>. (2) The colon's explanation is \"frames may be dropped… fine detail disappears\", so <em>lossy</em> = some data is deliberately discarded. (3) The contrast with \"plays smoothly\" plus <code>so</code> confirms the trade: smoothness bought by losing detail. <strong>Final answer:</strong> lossy = data is irreversibly discarded to gain speed or smoothness; clue = definition (colon) with cause–effect (<code>so</code>) support.</p>\n<p><strong>Example 2 (medium) — reading a paper's headline number.</strong> A paper reports \"a 32% reduction in latency on 5G testbeds\". What four questions do you ask before quoting that number?</p>\n<p>Solution: (1) <strong>Compared to what?</strong> the authors' own earlier version is not the market leader — <em>the baseline decides how impressive 32% is</em>. (2) <strong>Under what conditions?</strong> load, payload size, distance, number of users — <em>a latency gain often vanishes at high load</em>. (3) <strong>Measured how?</strong> simulation or real testbed, mean or 99th percentile — <em>mean latency hides tail latency, which is what users feel</em>. (4) <strong>Sample and repeats?</strong> — <em>one run's 32% can be noise</em>. <strong>Final answer:</strong> baseline, conditions, measurement method, variance; if any is missing, quote the number with its condition attached (\"32% lower mean latency in the authors' simulation\").</p>\n<p><strong>Example 3 (exam level) — critical paragraph.</strong> Write a 120-word critical paragraph on: \"Our campus Wi-Fi survey of 60 students shows 92% satisfaction, so the network needs no upgrade.\"</p>\n<p>Model answer: \"The claim rests on a satisfaction survey of 60 students, and three problems limit what it can support. First, satisfaction is self-reported and unanchored: without a definition of 'acceptable speed', a student who only checks email and one who uploads lab videos answer the same question differently. Second, the sample is small and its selection method is undescribed, so we cannot tell whether dissatisfied users replied at all — non-response bias usually inflates satisfaction figures. Third, satisfaction measures perception, not capacity: a network can satisfy today's users and still fail a lab of 40 machines running simultaneous updates. The evidence therefore supports 'most respondents are content with current use', not 'no upgrade is needed'. A utilisation log over one semester would test the stronger claim.\"</p>\n<p>Why it works: it names the claim, gives three independent reasons (measurement, sampling, construct), each with a mechanism, and ends with the evidence that would settle it — four marking-scheme boxes in one paragraph.</p>"}, {"kind": "trap", "num": "", "title": "Common traps and mistakes", "html": "<ul><li><strong>Reading every text the same way.</strong> One speed for a datasheet and a proof wastes hours and still misses detail. <em>Cost: you run out of time in exams and in project work.</em></li><li><strong>Confusing \"not stated\" with \"false\".</strong> The biggest single scorer of T/F/NG marks. <em>Cost: 2–4 marks per passage, every attempt.</em></li><li><strong>Answering from general knowledge instead of the passage.</strong> Your own knowledge of batteries is irrelevant if the passage says the opposite. <em>Cost: confident wrong answers.</em></li><li><strong>Chasing wpm without comprehension.</strong> Speed with no comprehension figure is a vanity metric. <em>Cost: re-reading everything, so net time rises.</em></li><li><strong>Reading Method first.</strong> You cannot judge which details matter before you know the claim. <em>Cost: 40 minutes on a paper that deserved five.</em></li><li><strong>Quoting <code>Typ</code> as a guarantee.</strong> Datasheet typicals are not limits. <em>Cost: a circuit that fails in the lab and a report that is simply wrong.</em></li><li><strong>Patchwriting with the source open.</strong> Synonym-swapping is still plagiarism. <em>Cost: an academic-integrity case, far more expensive than a bad mark.</em></li><li><strong>Trusting an AI summary as the text.</strong> Flipped negations read perfectly. <em>Cost: wrong citations and wrong claims.</em></li><li><strong>Skipping the limitations section.</strong> It is the cheapest critique available and the authors wrote it for you. <em>Cost: a review that reads as a summary.</em></li></ul>"}, {"kind": "revision", "num": "", "title": "Quick revision box", "html": "<ul><li><strong>Skim = shape</strong> (headings, first lines, last para); <strong>scan = one target</strong>; <strong>intensive = every sentence with a pen</strong>. Choose by task, not by text.</li><li><strong>WPM = words ÷ minutes; comprehension % = correct ÷ total × 100; effective rate = WPM × comprehension fraction.</strong> Track the third number, not the first.</li><li>Three leaks: <strong>sub-vocalisation, regression, no chunking</strong> — fix with the pencil trick, a purpose per paragraph, and slash-marked phrases.</li><li>Paper in 20 min: <strong>abstract → headings/figures → results → method (only what you doubt) → write a 4-line card.</strong> Most papers get discarded, correctly.</li><li>Datasheets: <strong>Min / Typ / Max</strong>; <code>Typ</code> is not guaranteed and a number without test conditions is unusable.</li><li>Unknown word: <strong>split prefix + root + suffix</strong>, check definition / contrast / example / cause–effect clues, then substitute the guess back into the sentence.</li><li>Prove before you believe: critical reading separates <strong>claim, evidence, assumption, bias</strong> and ends in a verdict; in T/F/NG the pointer test decides — <strong>support → TRUE, contradiction → FALSE, neither → NOT GIVEN.</strong></li><li>AI loop: <strong>read → own summary → AI summary → diff numbers, negations, who/what, certainty.</strong> Never cite a source you have not opened.</li></ul>"}], "practice": [{"q": "(easy) A 12-page chapter is due tomorrow and you need only the definition of \"deadlock\" and the four Coffman conditions. Which modes, in what order, and roughly how long?", "a": "<p>Answer not given in the notes file.</p>", "tag": "easy", "qHtml": "<em>(easy)</em> A 12-page chapter is due tomorrow and you need only the definition of \"deadlock\" and the four Coffman conditions. Which modes, in what order, and roughly how long?"}, {"q": "(easy) You read 750 words in 2 minutes 30 seconds and get 7 of 10 questions right. Compute wpm, comprehension %, and effective reading rate.", "a": "<p>Answer not given in the notes file.</p>", "tag": "easy", "qHtml": "<em>(easy)</em> You read 750 words in 2 minutes 30 seconds and get 7 of 10 questions right. Compute wpm, comprehension %, and effective reading rate."}, {"q": "(medium) In \"The scheduler is work-conserving : it never leaves a core idle while a runnable task is waiting\", state the meaning and the clue type, then give one reason a scheduler might deliberately not be work-conserving.", "a": "<p>Answer not given in the notes file.</p>", "tag": "medium", "qHtml": "<em>(medium)</em> In \"The scheduler is <em>work-conserving</em>: it never leaves a core idle while a runnable task is waiting\", state the meaning and the clue type, then give one reason a scheduler might deliberately <em>not</em> be work-conserving."}, {"q": "(medium) Write the four note-card fields (claim, evidence, limit, my comment) for: \"Kulkarni (2021) tested a peer-review intervention in two first-year writing sections (n = 78); drafts improved on organisation but not on grammar; the author notes both sections were taught by the same instructor.\"", "a": "<p>Answer not given in the notes file.</p>", "tag": "medium", "qHtml": "<em>(medium)</em> Write the four note-card fields (claim, evidence, limit, my comment) for: \"Kulkarni (2021) tested a peer-review intervention in two first-year writing sections (n = 78); drafts improved on organisation but not on grammar; the author notes both sections were taught by the same instructor.\""}, {"q": "(medium-hard) A post says: \"Since our app launched, average screen time has risen 40%, proving users love the new design.\" Write a 60-word critical evaluation naming the assumption.", "a": "<p>Answer not given in the notes file.</p>", "tag": "hard", "qHtml": "<em>(medium-hard)</em> A post says: \"Since our app launched, average screen time has risen 40%, proving users love the new design.\" Write a 60-word critical evaluation naming the assumption."}, {"q": "(exam level) Correct this AI summary and list its errors. Original: \"The prototype reduced false positives by 22% but increased false negatives, and the authors warn it is unsuitable for screening without human review.\" AI summary: \"The prototype reduced false positives and false negatives by 22%, and the authors recommend it for unsupervised screening.\"", "a": "<p>Answer not given in the notes file.</p>", "tag": "exam", "qHtml": "<em>(exam level)</em> Correct this AI summary and list its errors. <em>Original:</em> \"The prototype reduced false positives by 22% but increased false negatives, and the authors warn it is unsuitable for screening without human review.\" <em>AI summary:</em> \"The prototype reduced false positives and false negatives by 22%, and the authors recommend it for unsupervised screening.\""}], "file": "Semester_2/CSFE-CommunicationSkills/CSFE-M03.md"}, {"n": 4, "title": "CO4 – Writing", "tagline": "Writing is the skill that gets judged the most and taught the least.", "minutes": 44, "sections": [{"kind": "big", "num": "", "title": "Big picture, 30 seconds", "html": "<p>Writing is the skill that gets <em>judged</em> the most and taught the least. In college you are marked on lab reports, assignments, seminar abstracts and answer scripts; in a job you will be judged on emails, design documents, incident reports and proposals. Nobody will grade your accent, but everybody will read your writing — and unlike speech, writing does not let you correct yourself with a smile.</p>\n<p>Here is the mental model that fixes 80% of weak writing: <strong>writing is not talking, typed out.</strong> Speech is repaired on the fly by tone, gesture and \"sorry, I meant…\". Writing gets exactly one shot, read by someone tired, in a hurry, possibly not a native speaker of your context. So writing has to do the repair work in advance: a clear structure the reader can skim, sentences whose meaning cannot be misread, and a tone that does not accidentally sound rude or childish.</p>\n<p>Think of a document as a building, not a sentence-factory:</p>\n<ul><li><strong>Structure</strong> (walls and floors) — sections, paragraphs, topic sentences. This is <em>coherence</em>: the reader always knows where they are.</li><li><strong>Connections</strong> (doors and staircases) — linking words, pronouns, repeated key terms. This is <em>cohesion</em>: the reader can move from one idea to the next without falling.</li><li><strong>Finishing</strong> (paint and plaster) — grammar, spelling, punctuation, formatting. This is <em>correctness</em>, and it is what a reader notices first even though it is the least important.</li></ul>\n<p>Most students polish the paint and forget the walls. That is why a grammatically perfect paragraph can still score badly: it never told the reader what it was about.</p>"}, {"kind": "basics", "num": "", "title": "Basics you must know first", "html": "<ul><li><strong>Sentence</strong> — one complete thought with a subject and a finite verb. A <em>fragment</em> (\"Because the server crashed.\") is not a sentence; a <em>run-on</em> (\"The server crashed we lost data.\") is two sentences fused without punctuation or a conjunction.</li><li><strong>Paragraph</strong> — one idea, developed. If a paragraph has two ideas, it is two paragraphs.</li><li><strong>Register</strong> — the formality level: chat message → email → report → research paper. Wrong register is the commonest \"it feels off\" problem.</li><li><strong>Claim</strong> — a statement you assert to be true. <strong>Evidence</strong> — what backs it. <strong>Warrant</strong> — the reasoning that connects the two.</li><li><strong>Citation</strong> — telling the reader where a fact or idea came from.</li><li><strong>Draft</strong> — a version written to be improved, not defended. Every good document is a second draft.</li></ul>\n<div class=\"divider\"></div>"}, {"kind": "concept", "num": "4.1", "title": "Extended writing", "html": "<h4>Intuition</h4>\n<p>A paragraph is a thought; an essay is an argument made of thoughts. The difference is not length — it is that an essay must <em>go somewhere</em>. A student who writes six paragraphs, each a separate observation about social media, has written six paragraphs, not an essay.</p>\n<p>The one-sentence test: <strong>can you state what your essay argues, in one sentence, before you write it?</strong> If yes, every paragraph has a job (support, qualify, illustrate that sentence). If no, you are hoping the argument appears while typing, and it usually does not.</p>\n<h4>The idea, step by step</h4>\n<p><strong>Step 1 — Write the thesis statement first, and make it arguable.</strong> A thesis is the claim the whole text defends. It must be specific enough that someone could disagree with it.</p>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>Weak (a topic, not a thesis): \"This essay is about online examinations.\"\nWeak (unarguable):            \"Online examinations have advantages and disadvantages.\"\nStrong (arguable, specific):  \"Online examinations are fairer than written ones for large\n                               first-year courses, provided the question paper is redesigned\n                               for open-book conditions.\"</code></pre>\n<p>The strong version tells you what to prove and hints at the counter-argument (invigilation, cheating) that you must answer.</p>\n<p><strong>Step 2 — Plan in a skeleton, not in prose.</strong> Five minutes of planning saves twenty minutes of rewriting. A workable skeleton for a 700-word essay:</p>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>Thesis: &lt;one sentence&gt;\nP1  Background + why the question matters (2-3 sentences, no argument yet)\nP2  Claim 1 + evidence + explanation + link to thesis\nP3  Claim 2 + evidence + explanation + link to thesis\nP4  Counter-claim + rebuttal (this is what separates a 6/10 from an 8/10)\nP5  Conclusion: restate thesis in new words + what follows from it (no new claims)</code></pre>\n<p><strong>Step 3 — Build every body paragraph with PEEL.</strong> <strong>P</strong>oint (topic sentence stating the claim of <em>this</em> paragraph), <strong>E</strong>vidence (data, example, quotation, your own observation), <strong>E</strong>xplanation (why the evidence supports the point — this is the part students skip and it is where marks live), <strong>L</strong>ink (back to the thesis, or forward to the next paragraph).</p>\n<p>Model PEEL paragraph:</p>\n<div class=\"note note--why\"><span class=\"tag\">Note</span><p><strong>Point:</strong> Redesigned open-book papers test understanding rather than recall. <strong>Evidence:</strong> In a 2022 trial across three engineering colleges, students given open-book papers scored 14% lower on recall questions but 22% higher on questions requiring the application of a formula to an unfamiliar case. <strong>Explanation:</strong> This shift matters because recall is the skill employers automate and application is the skill they hire for; a closed-book paper rewards memorising a formula's statement, while an open-book paper rewards knowing when to use it. <strong>Link:</strong> If the assessment is changed to match the skill we claim to teach, the fairness objection to online exams loses most of its force.</p></div>\n<p><strong>Step 4 — Control paragraph length and shape.</strong> 100–180 words per paragraph in academic writing; one idea per paragraph; the first sentence carries the claim, the last sentence either closes it or hands over to the next.</p>\n<p><strong>Step 5 — Write the introduction and conclusion last.</strong> They are summaries of an argument you have not built yet. Write them after the body exists, and the introduction will actually match the essay.</p>\n<p><strong>Step 6 — Revise at three levels, in this order.</strong> (1) <em>Argument</em>: is the thesis answered, is any paragraph doing no work? (2) <em>Structure</em>: does each paragraph open with a claim, do the links exist? (3) <em>Language</em>: grammar, word choice, spelling. Fixing language first is the most common waste of time — you may delete the whole paragraph anyway.</p>\n<div class=\"card\"><h4>Example question</h4><p>Write one PEEL paragraph (about 105 words) answering: \"Should first-year students be allowed to use AI assistants for programming assignments?\"</p>\n<h4>Solution</h4>\n<ol><li><strong>Choose a position you can defend in one paragraph</strong> — not the whole debate. <em>Reason: one paragraph carries one claim; a paragraph that argues both sides argues neither.</em></li><li><strong>P (Point):</strong> \"Allowing AI assistants in first-year programming assignments is defensible only if the assessment is changed to test debugging and verification rather than code production.\"</li><li><strong>E (Evidence):</strong> \"Students who copy a working solution from an assistant typically cannot explain why it works: in a small classroom study, 8 of 12 students who submitted AI-generated code could not locate the bug when the same code was deliberately broken.\" <em>Reason: a concrete observation beats a general claim.</em></li><li><strong>E (Explanation):</strong> \"The value of the assignment was never the typing — it was the mental model built while debugging. If the deliverable is code, the assistant removes the learning; if the deliverable is a verified, tested, explained solution, the assistant becomes a fast compiler that still requires a programmer.\" <em>Reason: this is the step students skip, and it is where the marks sit.</em></li><li><strong>L (Link):</strong> \"The question is therefore not whether to ban the tool, but what the assignment is actually measuring.\"</li><li><strong>Check length and shape.</strong> The paragraph is about 105 words, opens with a claim, contains one piece of evidence, explains it, and ends by re-framing the question. <em>Reason: PEEL is a shape, and shape is checkable.</em></li></ol>\n<div class=\"res\">the paragraph above — Point (assessment must change), Evidence (8 of 12 could not debug), Explanation (the learning was in the debugging), Link (re-frame the question).</div>\n<p><strong>Why this works:</strong> it makes one claim instead of surveying a debate, it separates evidence from interpretation, and the link sentence makes the paragraph do work for a larger argument rather than standing alone.</p>\n<div class=\"note note--trap\"><span class=\"tag\">Common trap</span><p>Writing an \"essay\" that is really a list — one paragraph per example, no thesis, no links. It feels organised because the paragraphs are tidy, but there is no argument, so it scores in the middle no matter how good the grammar is. Second trap: a thesis so vague (\"technology has changed education\") that no evidence could ever contradict it.</p></div>\n<div class=\"divider\"></div></div>"}, {"kind": "concept", "num": "4.2", "title": "Technical reports, proposals and professional documents", "html": "<h4>Intuition</h4>\n<p>A <strong>report</strong> looks backwards: here is what we did, what we found, and what it means. A <strong>proposal</strong> looks forwards: here is what we propose to do, why it is worth funding, and what it will cost. Same skeleton, opposite tense and opposite purpose — and mixing them is one of the most common reasons a project proposal gets rejected (\"you have written a report on work you have not done yet\").</p>\n<p>Professional documents are the same skill at smaller scale: an email is a one-page document with a subject line doing the work of a title. The reader's question is always \"what do you want from me, and by when?\" — answer it in the first two lines.</p>\n<h4>The idea, step by step</h4>\n<p><strong>Step 1 — Use the standard report structure.</strong> Markers look for these sections, and so do managers:</p>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>Title page        : title, author, roll number, date, course\nAbstract / Exec summary : 100-150 words, the whole report in miniature (problem, method, result)\n1. Introduction   : background, problem, aim, scope (what is NOT covered)\n2. Literature / Related work : what others found (short in a course report)\n3. Methodology    : how you did it, enough for someone to repeat it\n4. Results        : what you measured, in tables and figures, no interpretation yet\n5. Discussion     : what the results mean, limitations, sources of error\n6. Conclusion     : answer the aim in 3-5 sentences\n7. Recommendations: what should be done next (optional in a lab report, expected in a survey)\nReferences        : every source you actually used\nAppendices        : raw data, code listings, questionnaires</code></pre>\n<p><strong>Step 2 — Separate results from discussion.</strong> Results report; discussion interprets. \"The average latency was 42 ms\" belongs in Results; \"this is acceptable for interactive use but not for real-time control\" belongs in Discussion. Mixing them makes the report look like an opinion piece.</p>\n<h4>Step 3 — Know the report-vs-proposal differences.</h4>\n<div class=\"tbl-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Feature</th><th>Report</th><th>Proposal</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Time direction</td><td>past (what was done)</td><td>future (what will be done)</td></tr><tr><td>Main verb tense</td><td>past (\"we measured\")</td><td>future/present (\"we will measure\")</td></tr><tr><td>Core question</td><td>what happened, and what does it mean?</td><td>why fund this, and can you deliver it?</td></tr><tr><td>Must contain</td><td>results, discussion, limitations</td><td>objectives, methodology, timeline, budget, outcomes</td></tr><tr><td>Reader's decision</td><td>accept the findings</td><td>release money, time or approval</td></tr><tr><td>Failure mode</td><td>results without interpretation</td><td>promises without a plan or a cost</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><strong>Step 4 — Write emails with the ask in the first two lines.</strong> Structure: subject line that names the topic and the action → one-line context → the specific ask → the deadline → what you have attached. Three samples:</p>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>REQUEST\nSubject: Request for lab slot change - CS lab, Thursday 2 pm\n\nRespected Sir/Madam,\nI am Ravi Kumar (22CS1043), Section B. I have a mid-semester exam on Thursday at 2 pm,\nwhich clashes with our DBMS lab slot.\nCould our batch's lab be moved to Friday 10 am, or may I attend the Friday batch instead?\nI have attached the exam timetable for reference.\nThank you for considering this.\nRavi Kumar, 22CS1043\n\nESCALATION (after a reasonable wait, with facts and no blame)\nSubject: Escalation: lab network down for 3 days - impact on Batch B submissions\n\nRespected Sir,\nThe CS lab network has been unavailable since Monday 9 am, so Batch B (32 students) cannot\naccess the DBMS server for the assignment due on Friday.\nI reported this to the lab assistant on Monday (ticket 1187) and again on Tuesday; the\nissue is still open.\nCould the deadline be extended to Monday, or could we be given access to the backup server?\nI can be reached on 98xxxxxx21 at any time today.\nRavi Kumar, 22CS1043, Batch B\n\nAPOLOGY (own it, say what you will do, no excuses first)\nSubject: Apology and correction: wrong file attached to yesterday's submission\n\nRespected Sir,\nI attached the wrong PDF to yesterday's assignment submission; the correct file is attached\nhere and is also uploaded to the portal as \"assignment2_final.pdf\".\nI have checked the remaining pages and the rest of the submission is unaffected.\nI am sorry for the extra work this causes you, and I will verify attachments before\nsubmitting from now on.\nRavi Kumar, 22CS1043</code></pre>\n<p><strong>Step 5 — Format a formal letter correctly.</strong> Sender's address, date, receiver's address, subject, salutation, body (3 paragraphs: purpose, detail, action), complimentary close, signature block. Two conventions worth knowing (British style): <code>Dear Sir/Madam</code> closes with <code>Yours faithfully</code>; <code>Dear Mr Sharma</code> closes with <code>Yours sincerely</code>. Do not mix them.</p>\n<p><strong>Step 6 — Use a memo for internal, no-salutation communication.</strong> Format: <code>TO / FROM / DATE / SUBJECT</code>, then a one-paragraph purpose statement, then numbered points, then the action required. No \"Dear\", no signature.</p>\n<div class=\"card\"><h4>Example question</h4><p>You are the team lead of a four-person mini-project group. A teammate has missed two review meetings and his module is three days late, which now threatens the submission. Write an escalation email to the course instructor (about 105 words) and state what you deliberately did <em>not</em> write.</p>\n<h4>Solution</h4>\n<ol><li><strong>Subject line names topic + impact + action.</strong> \"Escalation: Module 2 delayed by 3 days - risk to Friday submission (Team 7)\". <em>Reason: the instructor must be able to prioritise from the inbox list alone.</em></li><li><strong>Line 1–2: who, what, which team.</strong> \"I am Ananya Rao (22CS1071), lead of Team 7 in the Software Engineering mini-project.\" <em>Reason: context before complaint; the reader should not have to search.</em></li><li><strong>Facts and dates, no adjectives.</strong> \"Module 2 (database layer) was due on Tuesday. It is now three days late, and the teammate responsible has not attended the last two review meetings (5 and 12 November).\" <em>Reason: dates are checkable; \"he is careless\" is not, and it makes you look unprofessional.</em></li><li><strong>Impact, quantified.</strong> \"Because Module 3 depends on it, our integration and testing window has shrunk from five days to two, and the Friday submission is at risk.\" <em>Reason: escalation is justified by impact, not by frustration.</em></li><li><strong>Two concrete options, not an open complaint.</strong> \"Could you advise on (a) a short extension for the team, or (b) reassigning Module 2 to another member this week? I have kept a record of the review minutes in case they are useful.\" <em>Reason: giving options makes it easy to act, which is the only thing escalation is for.</em></li><li><strong>What I deliberately did not write.</strong> No blame adjectives (\"irresponsible\"), no group chat screenshots, no threat, no CC to five people, no \"as I already told you\". <em>Reason: escalation is a request for a decision; anything else invites a reply about your tone instead of the problem.</em></li></ol>\n<div class=\"res\">the email above — subject with impact, identity and context, dated facts, quantified risk, two actionable options, professional close — and deliberately no blame, no sarcasm, no mass CC.</div>\n<p><strong>Why this works:</strong> it converts a personal frustration into a decision someone can make in thirty seconds, and it keeps a record that protects the team rather than attacking a person.</p>\n<div class=\"note note--trap\"><span class=\"tag\">Common trap</span><p>Writing an escalation in the heat of the moment, with \"kindly look into the matter at the earliest\" and no dates or impact — it reads as a complaint and gets ignored. Another trap: the report that has Results and Conclusion but no Discussion, so the reader cannot tell what the numbers mean; and the proposal written in the past tense about work not yet done.</p></div>\n<div class=\"divider\"></div></div>"}, {"kind": "concept", "num": "4.3", "title": "Describing data and visuals", "html": "<h4>Intuition</h4>\n<p>A chart is an argument in pictures, and your paragraph is the caption that tells the reader what to notice. Two rules follow. First, <strong>never describe every number</strong> — a reader can see the bars; what they cannot see is which difference matters. Second, <strong>give the overview before the details</strong>: \"all four states improved, and the gap between them narrowed\" is more useful than any single percentage.</p>\n<p>Think of it as telling someone about a cricket match: you say who won and by how much first, then the innings that decided it — not ball-by-ball from over one.</p>\n<h4>The idea, step by step</h4>\n<p><strong>Step 1 — Use precise trend verbs and adverbs.</strong> Build a small vocabulary and vary it; repeating \"increased\" six times makes a description sound mechanical.</p>\n<div class=\"tbl-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Direction</th><th>Verbs</th><th>Adverbs / degree</th><th>Nouns</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Up</td><td>rose, climbed, increased, grew, surged, soared</td><td>slightly, steadily, sharply, dramatically, twofold</td><td>an increase, a rise, growth, an upturn</td></tr><tr><td>Down</td><td>fell, dropped, declined, decreased, dipped, plummeted</td><td>marginally, gradually, steeply, sharply</td><td>a fall, a decline, a drop, a downturn</td></tr><tr><td>Flat</td><td>plateaued, levelled off, remained stable, held steady</td><td>at around, roughly, approximately</td><td>a plateau, stability</td></tr><tr><td>Changeable</td><td>fluctuated, oscillated, varied</td><td>erratically, wildly</td><td>fluctuation</td></tr><tr><td>Multiples</td><td>doubled, tripled, quadrupled, halved</td><td>nearly, almost, exactly</td><td>a doubling, a halving</td></tr><tr><td>Comparison</td><td>exceeded, overtook, lagged behind, matched, was twice that of</td><td>considerably, marginally</td><td>the highest, the lowest, the gap</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><strong>Step 2 — Follow the four-part structure: introduce → overview → details → (optional) close.</strong> Introduction: what the chart shows (one sentence, no numbers). Overview: the two or three biggest patterns. Details: the numbers that support those patterns, grouped logically (by item, or by time). Close (only in longer texts): the implication.</p>\n<p><strong>Step 3 — Group, do not list.</strong> \"State A rose fastest, from 12% to 71%, while D rose slowest, from 25% to 52%\" is a comparison. \"A was 12, then 38, then 71; B was 20, then 45, then 66…\" is a list, and it wastes your word limit.</p>\n<p><strong>Step 4 — Handle time and units precisely.</strong> Use past tense for finished periods (\"fell in 2020\"), present perfect for periods continuing to now (\"has risen since 2019\"), and always attach the unit (% of households, ₹ crore, ms). Never write \"increased by 2 times\"; write \"doubled\", or \"increased twofold\".</p>\n<p><strong>Step 5 — Name what is <em>not</em> shown.</strong> A strong description adds one honest caveat (\"the chart covers households, not individual users\"), because it shows you understood the data rather than just reading labels.</p>\n<div class=\"card\"><h4>Example question</h4><p>Describe the chart below in about 150 words, using the overview-first structure. Data: percentage of households with internet access.</p>\n<div class=\"tbl-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>State</th><th>2015</th><th>2019</th><th>2023</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>A</td><td>12</td><td>38</td><td>71</td></tr><tr><td>B</td><td>20</td><td>45</td><td>66</td></tr><tr><td>C</td><td>8</td><td>22</td><td>58</td></tr><tr><td>D</td><td>25</td><td>40</td><td>52</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><strong>Solution (model description, 150 words):</strong></p>\n<div class=\"note note--why\"><span class=\"tag\">Note</span><p>The table shows the percentage of households with internet access in four states (A–D) at three points between 2015 and 2023.  Overall, access expanded in every state, but the gap between the best- and worst-covered states widened slightly, from 17 to 19 percentage points, because the leading state also grew fastest.  State A recorded the fastest growth, rising almost sixfold from 12% in 2015 to 71% in 2023, which lifted it from second-lowest to the highest position. State C followed a similar path, climbing from 8% to 58%, a more than sevenfold increase from the smallest base. By contrast, State D had the widest coverage in 2015 at 25% but grew most slowly, finishing last at 52%. State B stayed in the middle throughout, roughly tripling from 20% to 66%. The final period was the stronger one for A and C, which added 33 and 36 percentage points respectively after 2019.</p></div>\n<p>Step-by-step reasons (each one is checkable against the table):</p>\n<ol><li><strong>Sentence 1 fixes what is measured and when</strong> — no numbers yet. *Reason: the reader must know what<p>the unit is before any figure appears.*</p></li><li><strong>Sentence 2 is the overview</strong>, and it is arithmetic-checked: 2015 spread = 25 − 8 = 17 points;<p>2023 spread = 71 − 52 = 19 points. <em>Reason: an overview claim that the numbers do not support is the costliest error in this task, so compute the gap before writing about it.</em></p></li><li><strong>Details are grouped, not listed</strong>: fastest (A), similar (C), slowest (D), middle (B). *Reason: a<p>grouped description answers \"what should I notice?\"; a row-by-row list only repeats the table.*</p></li><li><strong>Multiples are computed, not guessed</strong>: 71 / 12 ≈ 5.9 (almost sixfold), 58 / 8 ≈ 7.25 (more than<p>sevenfold), 66 / 20 = 3.3 (roughly tripled). <em>Reason: markers check these.</em></p></li><li><strong>One closing observation</strong> (\"the final period was stronger for A and C\": 71 − 38 = 33 and<p>58 − 22 = 36) <em>Reason: it adds something the reader cannot see at a glance, which is the only justification for a fifth sentence.</em></p></li></ol>\n<div class=\"res\">the 150-word description above. Its spine is: what the chart shows → overview (everything rose, gap widened from 17 to 19 points) → grouped details with computed multiples → one closing observation.</div>\n<p><strong>Why this works:</strong> the overview carries the mark, the details are grouped so each sentence compares rather than lists, and every figure in it can be verified against the table in under a minute.</p>\n<div class=\"note note--trap\"><span class=\"tag\">Common trap</span><p>Describing data you did not check. Percentage <em>points</em> are not per cent: a rise from 12% to 71% is 59 percentage points, and it is also roughly a sixfold increase — mixing the two produces sentences that are confidently wrong. Second trap: starting with \"The chart given below shows…\" and spending 60 of your 150 words on the introduction.</p></div>\n<div class=\"divider\"></div></div>"}, {"kind": "concept", "num": "4.4", "title": "Coherence and cohesion", "html": "<h4>Intuition</h4>\n<p>Two words that sound alike and are graded separately. <strong>Coherence</strong> is whether the ideas hold together — the reader can follow the argument from paragraph to paragraph. <strong>Cohesion</strong> is whether the sentences are stitched together — the reader can follow from sentence to sentence without stumbling.</p>\n<p>Think of a train: coherence is the route (does this train go somewhere I want to go?), cohesion is the couplings between coaches (will the coaches stay attached?). A document with perfect couplings but no route is a beautifully linked list of unrelated points; a document with a great route but no couplings feels like jumping between coaches.</p>\n<h4>The idea, step by step</h4>\n<p><strong>Step 1 — Make every paragraph start with its claim.</strong> A topic sentence is not decoration; it is the reader's map. If you delete the first sentence of each paragraph and the argument still works, your topic sentences were decorative.</p>\n<p><strong>Step 2 — Use linking words by <em>function</em>, not by habit.</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tbl-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Function</th><th>Linking words</th><th>Example use</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Addition</td><td>and, moreover, furthermore, in addition, also</td><td>\"Moreover, the sample was small.\"</td></tr><tr><td>Contrast</td><td>but, however, yet, although, whereas, in contrast, nevertheless</td><td>\"The mean rose; however, the median fell.\"</td></tr><tr><td>Cause</td><td>because, since, as, due to, owing to</td><td>\"Because the buffer overflowed, frames were dropped.\"</td></tr><tr><td>Effect</td><td>therefore, thus, hence, consequently, as a result, so</td><td>\"The cache was cold; consequently, latency doubled.\"</td></tr><tr><td>Sequence</td><td>first, then, next, subsequently, finally, meanwhile</td><td>\"First the model is trained; then it is quantised.\"</td></tr><tr><td>Example</td><td>for example, for instance, such as, namely</td><td>\"Two metrics, namely precision and recall, were reported.\"</td></tr><tr><td>Concession</td><td>although, even though, while it is true that</td><td>\"Although costs fell, adoption stayed flat.\"</td></tr><tr><td>Conclusion</td><td>in conclusion, overall, to sum up, in short</td><td>\"Overall, the intervention helped only in large classes.\"</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><strong>Step 3 — Reference backwards instead of repeating.</strong> Use pronouns and summary nouns: <code>this</code>, <code>these</code>, <code>such</code>, <code>the former / the latter</code>, <code>this finding</code>, <code>that assumption</code>. The rule: a reference word must point to something unambiguous and recent.</p>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>Repeating : The compiler rejected the file. The compiler also logged a warning about the file.\nCohesive  : The compiler rejected the file and logged a warning about it.\nAmbiguous : The compiler rejected the parser's output because it was outdated.   &lt;- \"it\" = compiler or output?</code></pre>\n<p><strong>Step 4 — Repeat key terms on purpose.</strong> Exact repetition of a technical term is <em>good</em> cohesion; using three synonyms for the same thing (\"the model / the system / the framework\") makes the reader wonder whether you mean three things. This is the opposite of school advice about \"avoid repetition\" — that advice is for essays about holidays, not for technical writing.</p>\n<p><strong>Step 5 — Do not over-link.</strong> Every \"moreover\" and \"furthermore\" costs the reader attention. Two rules: do not use a linking word when the logical relation is already obvious from the sentence order, and never start more than two consecutive sentences with one.</p>\n<div class=\"card\"><h4>Example question</h4><p>Rewrite this paragraph so it is coherent and cohesive, then list what you changed.</p>\n<div class=\"note note--why\"><span class=\"tag\">Note</span><p>\"Solar panels are cheap now. Many houses have them. The government gives a subsidy. The subsidy is only for residential use. Commercial buildings do not get it. Commercial buildings use more electricity. The policy may be redesigned.\"</p></div>\n<h4>Solution</h4>\n<ol><li><strong>Find the through-line.</strong> The paragraph's real point is a mismatch: the subsidy targets the smaller consumer while the bigger consumer is excluded. <em>Reason: coherence starts by naming the claim, then ordering sentences to serve it.</em></li><li><strong>Write the topic sentence:</strong> \"India's rooftop solar subsidy is aimed at the wrong consumer.\" <em>Reason: the reader now knows what every following sentence is doing.</em></li><li><strong>Group the sentences into two moves: what the policy does, then why that is a problem.</strong> \"The subsidy covers residential rooftops only. Yet commercial buildings, which consume far more electricity per square metre, are excluded.\"</li><li><strong>Add only the links that carry logic</strong> — one contrast marker (<code>yet</code>), one consequence (<code>as a result</code>). <em>Reason: over-linking is as bad as no linking.</em></li><li><strong>Use reference words instead of repetition:</strong> \"the subsidy\" → \"it\"; \"commercial buildings\" repeated once is fine because it is the key term.</li></ol>\n<p>Model rewrite: \"India's rooftop solar subsidy is aimed at the wrong consumer. Panels are now cheap enough for most middle-class households, and the subsidy covers residential rooftops only. Yet commercial buildings, which consume far more electricity per square metre, are excluded. As a result, the policy subsidises the smaller load and leaves the larger one on the grid.\"</p>\n<div class=\"res\">the rewrite above. Changes made: (1) added a topic sentence stating the claim, (2) grouped six short sentences into two logical moves, (3) replaced repetition with <code>it</code> and <code>the policy</code>, (4) added exactly two connectives (<code>Yet</code>, <code>As a result</code>), (5) kept the technical key terms unreplaced.</div>\n<p><strong>Why this works:</strong> the paragraph now has a route (claim → what the policy does → why that fails) and couplings that only appear where the logic turns, so the reader spends attention on the argument instead of on decoding.</p>\n<div class=\"note note--trap\"><span class=\"tag\">Common trap</span><p>Sprinkling connectives as decoration — \"Moreover, furthermore, besides this, in addition to this\" in four consecutive sentences. It signals \"I know linking words exist\", not \"I have a logical relation here\". If you can delete the word and the logic survives, delete it.</p></div>\n<div class=\"divider\"></div></div>"}, {"kind": "concept", "num": "4.5", "title": "Formal and objective writing", "html": "<h4>Intuition</h4>\n<p>Formal writing is not \"fancy writing\". It is writing that removes the writer's personality from the foreground so the content can occupy it. Compare a lab notebook entry and a WhatsApp message about the same event: the facts are identical, but one is about the experiment and the other is about you.</p>\n<p>Objectivity has a practical purpose in engineering: a report must be usable by someone who does not trust you, does not know you, and will check your numbers. \"I think the motor overheated\" is a claim about your mental state; \"the motor winding reached 118 °C, above the 105 °C insulation rating\" is a claim about the world.</p>\n<h4>The idea, step by step</h4>\n<p><strong>Step 1 — Use the passive when the <em>action</em> matters more than the actor.</strong> Passive = <code>be</code> + past participle. It is right when the actor is unknown, irrelevant, obvious, or when you deliberately want to foreground the object.</p>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>Active  : We heated the samples to 60 °C for 20 minutes.\nPassive : The samples were heated to 60 °C for 20 minutes.        &lt;- better in a method section\nActive  : The intern deleted the database.\nPassive : The database was deleted.                               &lt;- hides who did it: use only if that is honest</code></pre>\n<p>Style note, not a rule: many journals and most Indian engineering reports prefer the passive in Methods. Some modern journals and most software documentation prefer active (\"we trained the model\"). Ask your department which it wants; both are defensible. What is never acceptable is using the passive to avoid responsibility.</p>\n<p><strong>Step 2 — Hedge claims to match your evidence.</strong> Hedging is not weakness; it is accuracy about how much you actually know.</p>\n<div class=\"tbl-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Overclaim</th><th>Hedged</th><th>Why the hedge is more accurate</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>\"This proves that X causes Y.\"</td><td>\"This suggests that X may contribute to Y.\"</td><td>one study cannot prove causation</td></tr><tr><td>\"The system is completely secure.\"</td><td>\"The system resists the attacks tested in this study.\"</td><td>absolute claims are untestable</td></tr><tr><td>\"All students struggle with grammar.\"</td><td>\"Most students in the sample struggled with articles.\"</td><td>scope and quantity are stated</td></tr><tr><td>\"Results will improve.\"</td><td>\"Results are likely to improve.\"</td><td>prediction, not fact</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>Hedges to keep handy: <code>may</code>, <code>appears to</code>, <code>suggests</code>, <code>indicates</code>, <code>tends to</code>, <code>is likely to</code>, <code>in this sample</code>, <code>under these conditions</code>.</p>\n<p><strong>Step 3 — Remove first person and contractions from reports (a style convention, not grammar).</strong> <code>I think</code>, <code>we feel</code>, <code>I guess</code> → delete or convert. <code>don't</code>, <code>can't</code>, <code>it's</code> → <code>do not</code>, <code>cannot</code>, <code>it is</code>. Note: \"we\" is normal in engineering papers when it means \"the authors\"; \"I think\" is not, because a report is not about your thinking.</p>\n<p><strong>Step 4 — Nominalise to make the text dense (and stop when it becomes unreadable).</strong> Turning verbs into nouns raises formality: \"We analysed the data and found that…\" → \"Analysis of the data showed that…\". Overdone, it produces \"the implementation of the optimisation of the allocation\", which is worse than the plain version. Density is a tool, not a target.</p>\n<p><strong>Step 5 — Cut conversational intensifiers and vague quantifiers.</strong> <code>very</code>, <code>really</code>, <code>a lot of</code>, <code>some</code>, <code>things</code>, <code>etc.</code> — replace with numbers or delete. \"There were a lot of errors in many files\" → \"Seventeen files contained 43 errors.\"</p>\n<p><strong>Before/after rewrites (the whole sub-topic in one table):</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tbl-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Weak (informal / subjective)</th><th>Strong (formal / objective)</th><th>What changed</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>\"I think our project is pretty good and it works most of the time.\"</td><td>\"The prototype met all five functional requirements; one non-functional requirement, cold-start latency, remains unmet.\"</td><td>opinion → measured claim; hedge removed by evidence</td></tr><tr><td>\"We didn't get good results because the lab was closed a lot.\"</td><td>\"Testing was limited by lab availability; only 6 of 12 planned trials were completed.\"</td><td>blame → factual constraint</td></tr><tr><td>\"The results are very bad for large inputs.\"</td><td>\"Accuracy fell from 94% to 71% for inputs above 10,000 records.\"</td><td>vague → quantified</td></tr><tr><td>\"You can see from the graph that sales went up a lot.\"</td><td>\"Sales rose 38% between Q2 and Q4 (Figure 3).\"</td><td>reader-directed → data-directed; figure cited</td></tr><tr><td>\"It is obvious that the algorithm is better.\"</td><td>\"The algorithm reduced runtime by 31% on the same hardware.\"</td><td>\"obvious\" → evidence</td></tr><tr><td>\"Mistakes were made during testing.\"</td><td>\"Two test cases were skipped when the test rig failed.\"</td><td>responsibility dodged → named honestly</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"card\"><h4>Example question</h4><p>Rewrite this paragraph as the Method section of a lab report (about 70 words): \"So we took 5 resistors and I measured them with the multimeter, and honestly the readings were all over the place, maybe because the multimeter is old. We didn't really do the temperature part because there was no time.\"</p>\n<h4>Solution</h4>\n<ol><li><strong>Delete the narrator.</strong> \"So we\", \"I measured\", \"honestly\" → remove. <em>Reason: a method section describes the procedure, not the person.</em></li><li><strong>Convert to passive where the actor is irrelevant:</strong> \"Five resistors were measured using a digital multimeter.\" <em>Reason: the reader needs to repeat the procedure, not meet you.</em></li><li><strong>Quantify the vague:</strong> \"all over the place\" → give the spread; \"maybe because the multimeter is old\" → state it as a possible source of error with a fact attached. \"The readings varied by up to 4%, which may reflect the multimeter's calibration status (last calibrated 2019).\" <em>Reason: \"maybe\" is a feeling; a calibration date is evidence.</em></li><li><strong>State the omission factually, in the limitations:</strong> \"The temperature-dependence test was not performed because the apparatus was unavailable within the lab session.\" <em>Reason: an honest limitation costs nothing; a hidden one destroys the report.</em></li><li><strong>Check tense and voice consistency.</strong> All past tense, all passive or impersonal. <em>Reason: mixing tenses inside a procedure reads as carelessness.</em></li></ol>\n<p>Model rewrite: \"Five carbon-film resistors (1 kΩ, 5%) were measured with a digital multimeter (model DT-830D, last calibrated 2019). Each resistor was measured three times and the mean recorded. Readings varied by up to 4% across repetitions, which may reflect the meter's calibration status rather than the components. The temperature-dependence test was not performed, as the heating apparatus was unavailable during the session; this limits the results to room-temperature behaviour.\"</p>\n<div class=\"res\">the rewrite above — impersonal, past tense, quantified, with the omission declared as a limitation instead of an excuse.</div>\n<p><strong>Why this works:</strong> every sentence is checkable by a stranger, and nothing in it depends on trusting the writer's mood or memory.</p>\n<div class=\"note note--trap\"><span class=\"tag\">Common trap</span><p>Thinking \"formal\" means long words. \"Utilise\" for \"use\", \"prior to\" for \"before\", \"in order to\" for \"to\" make writing longer without making it more formal — markers call this <em>pseudo-formal</em> writing. The other trap is the passive used to dodge responsibility (\"mistakes were made\"), which readers notice immediately.</p></div>\n<div class=\"divider\"></div></div>"}, {"kind": "concept", "num": "4.6", "title": "Argumentative and certification-style writing", "html": "<h4>Intuition</h4>\n<p>An argument is not a fight; it is a structure with three moving parts. You <strong>claim</strong> something, you acknowledge the strongest <strong>counter-claim</strong>, and you <strong>rebut</strong> it. Skip the counter-claim and your writing sounds naive; skip the rebuttal and you have conceded. The rebuttal is the part that earns the top band in IELTS/TOEFL essays, because it shows you can hold two ideas at once.</p>\n<p>Certification writing is the same skill under a clock: IELTS Writing Task 2 is 250 words in about 40 minutes, Task 1 (a chart description) 150 words in about 20 minutes; TOEFL's independent essay is about 300 words in 30 minutes. The examiner is not looking for brilliance; they are looking for a clear position, developed paragraphs, and range with accuracy.</p>\n<h4>The idea, step by step</h4>\n<h4>Step 1 — Build the claim–counter-claim–rebuttal unit.</h4>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>Claim        : Remote work raises productivity for focused engineering tasks.\nCounter-claim: But it weakens the informal mentoring that junior engineers need.\nRebuttal     : That loss is real but fixable by design - scheduled pairing sessions and code\n               reviews replace the corridor conversation; what cannot be replaced is the\n               accidental learning, which argues for hybrid rather than fully remote work.</code></pre>\n<p>Note the shape of a good rebuttal: it <em>concedes</em> what is true, <em>limits</em> the damage with a mechanism, and <em>narrows</em> the claim rather than denying the objection.</p>\n<h4>Step 2 — Use the certification essay structure.</h4>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>Introduction (2-3 sentences) : paraphrase the question, state your position clearly\nBody 1                       : strongest reason + example + explanation\nBody 2                       : second reason, OR the counter-claim + rebuttal\nConclusion (2 sentences)     : restate position, no new arguments, no new examples</code></pre>\n<p><strong>Step 3 — Take a position and keep it.</strong> \"While there are advantages and disadvantages, it depends on the situation\" is the classic band-6 answer: it is a refusal to answer. You may take a <em>qualified</em> position (\"beneficial, provided that…\") — that is different from refusing.</p>\n<p><strong>Step 4 — Write to the clock in fixed blocks.</strong> For a 40-minute, 250-word essay: 5 minutes planning (position + two reasons + one example each), 30 minutes writing, 5 minutes checking articles, plurals and verb endings. Never leave checking to chance — it is worth more marks per minute than writing.</p>\n<p><strong>Step 5 — Add range deliberately.</strong> One complex sentence, one conditional, one concessive (<code>although</code>), one impersonal construction. Do not attempt range you cannot control: an accurate simple sentence scores higher than a broken complex one.</p>\n<div class=\"card\"><h4>Example question</h4><p>Write a body paragraph (about 130 words) for: \"Universities should stop ranking students by marks and use continuous assessment instead.\" Include a counter-claim and a rebuttal.</p>\n<h4>Solution</h4>\n<ol><li><strong>Claim first, in the topic sentence:</strong> \"Continuous assessment is fairer than a single final examination because it measures learning rather than performance on one day.\" <em>Reason: the paragraph's job must be visible in sentence one.</em></li><li><strong>Evidence with a mechanism:</strong> \"A student who falls ill during exam week loses a whole semester's grade, whereas coursework spread across twelve weeks makes any single bad week recoverable.\" <em>Reason: mechanisms persuade; adjectives do not.</em></li><li><strong>Counter-claim, stated fairly:</strong> \"The obvious objection is that continuous assessment rewards diligence over understanding, and that marks awarded for weekly submissions can be inflated by effort rather than accuracy.\" <em>Reason: state the objection in its strongest form, or the rebuttal is worthless.</em></li><li><strong>Rebuttal that concedes and narrows:</strong> \"This is partly true, but it describes a design flaw rather than an inherent limit: assessment that uses unseen in-class tasks and clear rubrics measures understanding while keeping the spread of effort. What continuous assessment genuinely loses is the single high-pressure performance, which is precisely the skill universities claim not to be teaching.\" <em>Reason: concede, then relocate the problem.</em></li><li><strong>Link:</strong> \"The ranking debate therefore turns on which skill the degree is meant to certify.\" <em>Reason: closes the paragraph and hands over to the next.</em></li></ol>\n<div class=\"res\">the paragraph above — claim (fairer, measures learning), mechanism (recoverable weeks), counter-claim (rewards diligence), rebuttal (design flaw, not inherent limit), link (which skill is being certified).</div>\n<p><strong>Why this works:</strong> the counter-claim is stated at full strength, so the rebuttal does real work; the concession keeps the writer credible; and the claim is <em>narrowed</em> rather than defended absolutely, which is exactly what a top-band answer does.</p>\n<div class=\"note note--trap\"><span class=\"tag\">Common trap</span><p>The \"straw man\" counter-claim — inventing a weak objection (\"some people say it is too modern\") and knocking it down. Examiners and reviewers recognise it instantly. Second trap: a conclusion that introduces a brand-new argument, which cannot be developed and simply looks like an unfinished essay.</p></div>\n<div class=\"divider\"></div></div>"}, {"kind": "concept", "num": "4.7", "title": "Editing, proofreading, referencing and academic integrity", "html": "<h4>Intuition</h4>\n<p><strong>Editing</strong> is changing what the text says; <strong>proofreading</strong> is checking that it says it correctly. They are different passes and must be done separately — if you proofread while editing, you polish sentences you are about to delete.</p>\n<p>Think of a carpenter: editing is deciding the shape of the table, proofreading is sanding it. Nobody sands before cutting.</p>\n<h4>The idea, step by step</h4>\n<p><strong>Step 1 — Edit in three passes, from big to small.</strong> Pass 1: does each paragraph have one claim, and does the order work? Pass 2: are sentences clear, is anything repeated, is anything missing? Pass 3: grammar, spelling, punctuation, formatting. <em>Then</em> proofread aloud or backwards (read the last sentence first — it breaks the brain's auto-correction and exposes missing words).</p>\n<p><strong>Step 2 — Run a mechanical proofreading checklist.</strong> Use the same list every time; checklists catch what attention misses.</p>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>[ ] Subject-verb agreement            : \"the results shows\" -&gt; \"shows\"?\n[ ] Tense consistency                 : one tense per section, past for method\n[ ] Articles a / an / the             : countable singular nouns need one\n[ ] Plurals of uncountables           : information, research, equipment, software (no -s)\n[ ] Prepositions                      : discuss (no \"about\"), married to, depends on, consists of\n[ ] Sentence completeness             : no fragments, no run-ons\n[ ] Comma splices                     : \"The test failed, we fixed it.\" -&gt; full stop or semicolon\n[ ] Apostrophes                       : its / it's, students / student's / students'\n[ ] Number and unit spacing           : 5 V, 60 °C, 10 ms, ₹1,200 (consistent style)\n[ ] Figure and table references       : every figure mentioned in the text, in order\n[ ] Spell-check names and technical terms, then read them yourself (spell-check passes \"form\" for \"from\")</code></pre>\n<p><strong>Step 3 — Fix the errors Indian-English writing makes most often.</strong> These are the ones that cost marks in an English paper while being invisible to the writer.</p>\n<div class=\"tbl-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Error type</th><th>Wrong</th><th>Right</th><th>Why</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Article omitted</td><td>\"I am student of second year.\"</td><td>\"I am a second-year student.\"</td><td>singular countable nouns need an article</td></tr><tr><td>Article added</td><td>\"He gave me an advice.\"</td><td>\"He gave me some advice.\"</td><td>advice is uncountable</td></tr><tr><td>Preposition</td><td>\"We discussed about the results.\"</td><td>\"We discussed the results.\"</td><td><em>discuss</em> takes a direct object</td></tr><tr><td>Preposition</td><td>\"This is different than mine.\"</td><td>\"This is different from mine.\"</td><td>standard British/Indian academic usage</td></tr><tr><td>Countability</td><td>\"one of my friend\"</td><td>\"one of my friends\"</td><td><em>one of</em> + plural</td></tr><tr><td>Plural of uncountable</td><td>\"informations\", \"equipments\", \"softwares\"</td><td>information, equipment, software</td><td>uncountable in standard English</td></tr><tr><td>Tense shift</td><td>\"The sample was heated and then we observe the change.\"</td><td>\"…and then the change was observed.\"</td><td>keep the tense of the section</td></tr><tr><td>since / for</td><td>\"I am studying here since two years.\"</td><td>\"I have been studying here for two years.\"</td><td><em>since</em> + point in time, <em>for</em> + duration</td></tr><tr><td>less / fewer</td><td>\"less students\"</td><td>\"fewer students\"</td><td><em>fewer</em> with countable plurals</td></tr><tr><td>\"myself\" as subject</td><td>\"Myself Ravi Kumar.\"</td><td>\"I am Ravi Kumar.\"</td><td><em>myself</em> is reflexive, never a subject</td></tr><tr><td>Redundancy</td><td>\"return back\", \"repeat again\", \"discuss about it\"</td><td>return, repeat, discuss it</td><td>the second word adds nothing</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><strong>Step 4 — Cite in a consistent style.</strong> Two that you will meet constantly: <strong>author–date</strong> (APA/Harvard) — in text <code>(Sharma, 2021, p. 44)</code>, reference list alphabetical by author; and <strong>numbered</strong> (IEEE, standard in engineering) — in text <code>[1]</code>, reference list numbered in order of first appearance. Whichever you use, every in-text citation must have a reference entry and every entry must be cited. A reference list that includes sources you never cited is a marker's fastest signal that the list was copied.</p>\n<p><strong>Step 5 — Paraphrase properly, not decoratively.</strong> Real paraphrase changes the <em>structure</em> of the idea and keeps the <em>meaning</em>, with a citation. Copying with synonyms is not paraphrase.</p>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>Original : \"Because hydrometallurgical recovery costs more than newly mined metal, most spent\n            batteries are stockpiled rather than recycled.\"\nCopying  : \"Since hydrometallurgical recovery is more expensive than newly mined metal, the\n            majority of used batteries are stored instead of being recycled.\"   &lt;- still copying\nParaphrase: \"Economics, not technology, keeps used batteries in storage: recovery costs exceed\n            the price of mined metal (Sharma, 2021, p. 44).\"                    &lt;- restructured</code></pre>\n<p><strong>Step 6 — Treat academic integrity as a design constraint, not a risk.</strong> Keep your sources in a file as you read (not at the end), never paste a paragraph you have not paraphrased, and if you use an AI tool for anything beyond grammar, say so if your institution asks. The safe rule: <strong>if you could not explain the sentence to a friend without the source open, it is not yours yet.</strong></p>\n<div class=\"card\"><h4>Example question</h4><p>Proofread and correct this paragraph, listing each error type: \"Inspite of many research has been done on this topic, the results is not consistent. We discussed about the limitations in section 4. One of my friend helped me to collect the data, and we didn't faced any problem except the equipments was old.\"</p>\n<h4>Solution</h4>\n<ol><li><strong>\"Inspite\"</strong> → \"In spite\" (two words). <em>Error type: spelling.</em></li><li><strong>\"many research has been done\"</strong> → \"although much research has been done\" / \"despite the large body of research\". <em>Error type: countability (research is uncountable) plus a broken concessive structure — \"in spite of\" cannot be followed by a clause.</em></li><li><strong>\"the results is\"</strong> → \"the results are\". <em>Error type: subject–verb agreement.</em></li><li><strong>\"discussed about\"</strong> → \"discussed\". <em>Error type: preposition after a transitive verb.</em></li><li><strong>\"One of my friend\"</strong> → \"One of my friends\". <em>Error type: <code>one of</code> + plural noun.</em></li><li><strong>\"didn't faced\"</strong> → \"did not face\". <em>Error type: auxiliary + base form; also contraction removed for formal register.</em></li><li><strong>\"the equipments was old\"</strong> → \"the equipment was old\". <em>Error type: uncountable noun (no plural) — and with the correction, the singular verb is right.</em></li></ol>\n<p>Corrected version: \"Although much research has been done on this topic, the results are not consistent. The limitations are discussed in Section 4. One of my friends helped me collect the data, and we did not face any problem except that the equipment was old.\"</p>\n<div class=\"res\">seven corrections — spelling (<code>In spite</code>), countability (<code>much research</code>), agreement (<code>results are</code>), preposition (<code>discussed</code>), <code>one of my friends</code>, <code>did not face</code>, <code>the equipment was</code>. The corrected paragraph is above.</div>\n<p><strong>Why this works:</strong> every fix is named by <em>type</em>, which is the only way proofreading improves: fixing \"equipments\" teaches nothing, but knowing \"uncountable nouns take no plural\" catches <em>informations</em>, <em>softwares</em> and <em>advices</em> in the same session.</p>\n<div class=\"note note--trap\"><span class=\"tag\">Common trap</span><p>Relying on a spell-checker and a grammar tool as your proofreading. They miss real-word errors (\"form\" for \"from\", \"loose\" for \"lose\", \"then\" for \"than\") and they cannot see that your Section 4 reference points to a section you deleted. The other trap: a reference list full of sources you never opened — which is both a plagiarism risk and an obvious tell.</p></div>\n<div class=\"divider\"></div></div>"}, {"kind": "concept", "num": "4.1", "title": "Summary table — which writing tool when?", "html": "<div class=\"tbl-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>You are asked to…</th><th>Structure to use</th><th>Key language</th><th>The mark-deciding move</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Answer an essay question</td><td>thesis → PEEL paragraphs → counter-claim → conclusion</td><td>claim verbs, hedges, connectives</td><td>a thesis someone could disagree with</td></tr><tr><td>Write a lab report</td><td>aim → method → results → discussion → conclusion</td><td>past tense, passive in method</td><td>separating results from interpretation</td></tr><tr><td>Write a project proposal</td><td>objectives → method → timeline → budget → outcomes</td><td>future tense, commitments</td><td>a plan and a cost, not a promise</td></tr><tr><td>Write a request email</td><td>subject → context → ask → deadline → attachment</td><td>direct, courteous, specific</td><td>the ask inside the first two lines</td></tr><tr><td>Write an escalation</td><td>subject with impact → dated facts → quantified risk → options</td><td>neutral, factual, no blame</td><td>two options the reader can choose between</td></tr><tr><td>Write an apology</td><td>own it → correction → what changes</td><td>no excuses first</td><td>naming the consequence for the reader</td></tr><tr><td>Describe a chart</td><td>introduce → overview → grouped details</td><td>trend verbs, multiples, units</td><td>an arithmetic-checked overview</td></tr><tr><td>Make a paragraph flow</td><td>topic sentence → links only where logic turns</td><td>reference words, one connective per turn</td><td>deleting decorative connectives</td></tr><tr><td>Sound formal and objective</td><td>impersonal, quantified, hedged</td><td>passive where the actor is irrelevant</td><td>replacing opinion with a measurement</td></tr><tr><td>Argue a position under time</td><td>position → 2 reasons → counter + rebuttal → conclusion</td><td>concession + narrowing</td><td>a rebuttal that concedes before it answers</td></tr><tr><td>Submit without losing marks</td><td>edit (3 passes) → proofread (checklist) → cite consistently</td><td>articles, prepositions, plurals</td><td>naming each error by type</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"}, {"kind": "example", "num": "", "title": "Solved examples", "html": "<p><strong>Example 1 (easy) — thesis statement.</strong> Turn \"Write about electric vehicles in India\" into a thesis.</p>\n<p>Solution: (1) Pick a claim, not a topic: something must be arguable. (2) Add the scope (which vehicles, which market) and the condition that makes it true. (3) Check the test: could a reasonable person disagree? Model: \"Electric two-wheelers, not cars, are the realistic route to cutting urban transport emissions in India, because they need neither new highway charging infrastructure nor a change in household parking habits.\" <em>Why it works:</em> it names the object (two-wheelers), the claim (the realistic route), and the mechanism (no new infrastructure), so the essay has somewhere to go.</p>\n<p><strong>Example 2 (medium) — results paragraph.</strong> Write a Results paragraph (about 80 words) from this data: tensile tests on three filament samples, 5 trials each; mean strength 48.2 MPa, 51.7 MPa, 44.9 MPa; standard deviations 1.9, 2.4, 6.1; one sample failed at 33.4 MPa.</p>\n<p>Solution: (1) Report, do not interpret — save meaning for the discussion. (2) Lead with the comparison, then the spread, because the spread is the interesting part. (3) Name the outlier with its trial. Model: \"Mean tensile strength was 51.7 MPa for Sample B, 48.2 MPa for Sample A and 44.9 MPa for Sample C, averaged over five trials each (Table 2). Variability differed markedly: standard deviations were 2.4 MPa, 1.9 MPa and 6.1 MPa respectively. Sample C also produced the single lowest reading, 33.4 MPa in trial 4, which lies 1.9 standard deviations below its own mean. Samples A and B were therefore consistent within 5% of their means, while Sample C was not.\" <em>Why it works:</em> no sentence says \"this is because\" — the interpretation is left for the discussion, which is what a marker checks.</p>\n<p><strong>Example 3 (exam level) — argumentative conclusion plus rebuttal.</strong> Write the final two paragraphs (about 150 words) of an essay arguing that coding should be taught in every school.</p>\n<p>Solution: (1) State the strongest objection fairly. (2) Rebut by narrowing, not by denying. (3) Close by restating the position in new words with no new evidence. Model body paragraph: \"The strongest objection is one of opportunity cost: every hour spent on syntax is an hour not spent on mathematics or a second language, and most schoolchildren will not become programmers. This objection has force, but it assumes coding means vocational training in a specific language. Taught as computational thinking — decomposing a problem, testing an assumption, debugging an error — it is closer to logic than to typing, and the transfer to mathematics is measurable rather than theoretical. What the objection correctly rules out is teaching a framework that will be obsolete in five years; it does not rule out teaching the reasoning that outlives it.\" Model conclusion: \"Coding therefore belongs in the school curriculum not because every child will write software, but because every child will have to reason about systems that software controls. The curriculum should teach the thinking, and treat the language as replaceable.\" <em>Why it works:</em> the objection is stated at full strength, the rebuttal concedes a real limit (no frameworks), and the conclusion re-frames rather than repeats.</p>"}, {"kind": "trap", "num": "", "title": "Common traps and mistakes", "html": "<ul><li><strong>A topic instead of a thesis.</strong> \"This essay is about AI in education\" gives the reader nothing to agree or disagree with. <em>Cost: middle-band marks no matter how good the grammar is.</em></li><li><strong>Explanation skipped in PEEL.</strong> Point + Evidence + Link with no explanation is a quotation, not an argument. <em>Cost: the paragraph proves nothing, so it earns nothing.</em></li><li><strong>Results mixed with discussion.</strong> Interpretation inside the results table's paragraph makes the report look biased. <em>Cost: lost marks for structure, and your numbers become arguable.</em></li><li><strong>An overview that the data does not support.</strong> Claiming \"the gap narrowed\" without subtracting the two figures. <em>Cost: the single most visible error in a data-description task.</em></li><li><strong>Percent vs percentage points.</strong> \"Rose by 59%\" and \"rose by 59 percentage points\" are different claims about the same data. <em>Cost: a factually wrong sentence.</em></li><li><strong>Decorative connectives.</strong> \"Moreover, furthermore, in addition\" where nothing is being added. <em>Cost: cohesion marks drop for over-linking.</em></li><li><strong>Passive used to hide responsibility.</strong> \"Mistakes were made.\" <em>Cost: reads as evasion, which is worse than the mistake.</em></li><li><strong>Pseudo-formal vocabulary.</strong> \"Utilise\", \"prior to\", \"in order to\" for no reason. <em>Cost: longer text, no gain in formality, more errors.</em></li><li><strong>Straw-man counter-claims.</strong> Refuting an objection nobody makes. <em>Cost: examiners recognise it and discount the whole paragraph.</em></li><li><strong>Proofreading with a tool only.</strong> Spell-check passes \"form\" for \"from\" and cannot see a broken cross-reference. <em>Cost: careless-looking errors in an otherwise good document.</em></li><li><strong>Reference list padding.</strong> Sources cited nowhere in the text. <em>Cost: looks like copied formatting and invites an integrity question.</em></li></ul>"}, {"kind": "revision", "num": "", "title": "Quick revision box", "html": "<ul><li><strong>Thesis = arguable claim + scope + condition</strong> (if nobody could disagree, it is not a thesis), and every body paragraph follows <strong>PEEL: Point, Evidence, Explanation, Link</strong> — the Explanation is where the marks live.</li><li><strong>Report looks back, proposal looks forward.</strong> Report: results + discussion + limitations. Proposal: objectives + method + timeline + budget.</li><li><strong>Email: subject with the action, ask in the first two lines, deadline, attachment named.</strong></li><li><strong>Chart: introduce → overview → grouped details.</strong> Compute the gap and the multiples before you write them; use <em>percentage points</em> for differences.</li><li><strong>Coherence = route, cohesion = couplings.</strong> Add a connective only where the logic turns.</li><li><strong>Formal = impersonal + quantified + hedged</strong>, not long words. Passive when the actor is irrelevant, never to hide who acted.</li><li><strong>Argument = claim + counter-claim + rebuttal</strong>, where the rebuttal concedes, then narrows.</li><li><strong>Edit big to small, then proofread with a checklist</strong> (articles, prepositions, plurals, agreement, tense). <strong>Paraphrase restructures</strong> — copying with synonyms is still copying — and you cite only sources you actually opened.</li></ul>"}], "practice": [{"q": "(easy) Rewrite formally and objectively: \"I think our results are pretty good, but the lab was very noisy so maybe some readings are wrong.\"", "a": "<p>Answer not given in the notes file.</p>", "tag": "easy", "qHtml": "<em>(easy)</em> Rewrite formally and objectively: \"I think our results are pretty good, but the lab was very noisy so maybe some readings are wrong.\""}, {"q": "(easy) Write a subject line and the first two lines of an email asking your professor for a two-day extension on a report, giving one concrete reason.", "a": "<p>Answer not given in the notes file.</p>", "tag": "easy", "qHtml": "<em>(easy)</em> Write a subject line and the first two lines of an email asking your professor for a two-day extension on a report, giving one concrete reason."}, {"q": "(medium) Write a PEEL paragraph (about 110 words) on: \"Should attendance be compulsory in first-year engineering classes?\"", "a": "<p>Answer not given in the notes file.</p>", "tag": "medium", "qHtml": "<em>(medium)</em> Write a PEEL paragraph (about 110 words) on: \"Should attendance be compulsory in first-year engineering classes?\""}, {"q": "(medium) A chart shows average time spent on three activities (study, social media, sleep) for first-year students in 2019 and 2024: study 3.2 → 2.8 h, social media 2.1 → 3.4 h, sleep 7.1 → 6.4 h. Write a 90-word description with an overview first.", "a": "<p>Answer not given in the notes file.</p>", "tag": "medium", "qHtml": "<em>(medium)</em> A chart shows average time spent on three activities (study, social media, sleep) for first-year students in 2019 and 2024: study 3.2 → 2.8 h, social media 2.1 → 3.4 h, sleep 7.1 → 6.4 h. Write a 90-word description with an overview first."}, {"q": "(medium-hard) Write a memo (about 100 words) from a lab in-charge to all second-year students about a change in lab timings, with the action required stated once, clearly.", "a": "<p>Answer not given in the notes file.</p>", "tag": "hard", "qHtml": "<em>(medium-hard)</em> Write a memo (about 100 words) from a lab in-charge to all second-year students about a change in lab timings, with the action required stated once, clearly."}, {"q": "(exam level) Write the counter-claim and rebuttal paragraphs (about 150 words) for: \"Social media does more harm than good to teenagers.\"", "a": "<p>Answer not given in the notes file.</p>", "tag": "exam", "qHtml": "<em>(exam level)</em> Write the counter-claim and rebuttal paragraphs (about 150 words) for: \"Social media does more harm than good to teenagers.\""}], "file": "Semester_2/CSFE-CommunicationSkills/CSFE-M04.md"}, {"n": 5, "title": "Presentation Skills (CO5)", "tagline": "A presentation is not \"talking for ten minutes\". It is a guided journey you take an audience on: you say where you are going, you drive them there in…", "minutes": 57, "sections": [{"kind": "big", "num": "", "title": "Big picture, 30 seconds", "html": "<p>A presentation is not \"talking for ten minutes\". It is a <strong>guided journey</strong> you take an audience on: you say where you are going, you drive them there in small comfortable steps, and at the end you tell them where they have arrived. Everything else — slides, voice, eye contact, Q&amp;A — is either the road (structure), the vehicle (design), or the driving (delivery).</p>\n<p><strong>The analogy:</strong> an autorickshaw ride in a new city. A good driver says \"we'll go via MG Road, it's faster\" (roadmap), takes turns that feel smooth (transitions), and says \"here we are, that's the market\" (close). A bad driver starts the meter and drives in circles hoping you will work out the route. Most student presentations are the second driver.</p>\n<p><strong>Where you will use it:</strong> lab project reviews, seminar and viva, placement presentations, final-year demo, hackathon pitches, and the \"present your findings\" part of almost every job. CO5 is also where CO1–CO4 stop being separate subjects — you <em>listen</em> to questions, <em>speak</em> the answer, <em>read</em> your own slides critically, and <em>write</em> the outline first.</p>\n<p><strong>Where the marks are.</strong> In most rubrics, content and structure carry the most weight, delivery second, slide design usually the least. Students over-invest in pretty slides and under-invest in the outline. Do not be that student.</p>"}, {"kind": "basics", "num": "", "title": "Basics you must know first", "html": "<ul><li><strong>Audience</strong> – who is in the room, what they already know, what they will do after. Every design decision depends on this word.</li><li><strong>Purpose</strong> – the one change you want in the audience's head: \"approve our club\", \"believe our result\". One purpose, not four.</li><li><strong>Central message (thesis)</strong> – one sentence you could say in a lift. If you cannot write it in one line, you have notes, not a presentation.</li><li><strong>Signposting</strong> – spoken road signs: \"first\", \"that brings me to\", \"so what does this mean\".</li><li><strong>Visual aid</strong> – a slide, chart, demo or handout that supports your speech. It supports; it does not replace.</li><li><strong>Speaker notes</strong> – the words you will actually say, kept in the notes pane, not on the slide.</li><li><strong>Q&amp;A</strong> – the question-and-answer session after (or during) the talk.</li><li><strong>Rubric</strong> – the marks table your teacher uses. Ask for it. That is not cheating, it is target practice.</li></ul>"}, {"kind": "concept", "num": "5.1", "title": "Presentation structure and organisation", "html": "<h4>Intuition</h4>\n<p>Your friend calls: \"So anyway, the thing is, it depends, but basically I might come tomorrow, actually no, wait.\" You have no idea what the call was about. Now: \"Quick update — I'm coming tomorrow at 10, I'll bring the laptop, and I need your charger.\" Same information, but your brain relaxed instantly, because you knew the shape of what was coming.</p>\n<p>Audiences are that friend. When they know the shape, they stop spending energy guessing and start spending it listening. <strong>Structure is a kindness, not a formality.</strong></p>\n<h4>The idea, step by step</h4>\n<p><strong>Step 1 — Write the one-line message before anything else.</strong> Not \"I will talk about our project\" but \"our attendance app removes proxy attendance in forty seconds of class time\". Now every slide can be tested against that sentence: does it support the message or not?</p>\n<p><strong>Step 2 — Pick exactly three body points.</strong> Three is not magic, but it is the most most listeners hold without notes. Seven things to say means you have a document, not a talk — group them until three remain. <em>Mini example:</em> \"AI in healthcare\" becomes (1) diagnosis support, (2) hospital operations, (3) the risks; seven facts filed inside three buckets.</p>\n<h4>Step 3 — Build the opening in four beats.</h4>\n<div class=\"tbl-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Beat</th><th>Time</th><th>Job</th><th>Sample line</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Hook</td><td>15–20 s</td><td>Get attention: a number, a question, a short story</td><td>\"Last year, 41 of our 60 lab machines sat idle after 6 p.m.\"</td></tr><tr><td>Connect</td><td>10 s</td><td>Why it matters to <em>this</em> room</td><td>\"That's about 900 student-hours a month we cannot use.\"</td></tr><tr><td>Purpose</td><td>10 s</td><td>What you will do and what you want</td><td>\"In ten minutes I'll show three ways to fix that.\"</td></tr><tr><td>Roadmap</td><td>15 s</td><td>The three points, named</td><td>\"First the gap, then three options, then what I'm asking for.\"</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><strong>Step 4 — Follow the \"tell them what you'll tell them\" rule</strong> — say it three times, in three sizes: <em>before</em> (roadmap), <em>during</em> (signposts: \"that was A, now B\"), <em>after</em> (summary). This is a <strong>convention of good practice</strong>, often attributed to Dale Carnegie's advice on speeches, not a law of nature. Where it stops working: very short talks of 2–3 minutes, where the roadmap eats the content, and keynote storytelling, where revealing the ending kills suspense. For college academic and technical talks, always follow it.</p>\n<p><strong>Step 5 — Build the close in three beats:</strong> summary (the three points in one breath) → takeaway (the one sentence you want remembered) → ask (approve, fund, try, ask questions). Then a plain \"Thank you — happy to take questions.\" Never end on \"so, yeah, that's it.\"</p>\n<p><strong>Step 6 — Budget time before you write slides.</strong> For ten minutes: opening 1.0 min, body 6.5 min, close 1.5 min, buffer 1.0 min. At a comfortable presentation pace of <strong>130–150 words per minute</strong> that is roughly 1,300–1,500 words of speech. Conversation runs faster (160–190 wpm); presenting is slower because you pause. Reading a script at conversation speed is the number-one reason students overrun by three minutes.</p>\n<p><strong>Step 7 — Turn minutes into slides:</strong> one slide per 1–2 minutes of speaking, so 7–10 content slides plus a title for a ten-minute talk. Twenty-two slides means you exported a report, not designed a talk.</p>\n<h4>The sample outline (memorise this shape)</h4>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>TITLE SLIDE        project title / your name / guide / date\n1. HOOK            0:00-0:20   one number or one question, big type\n2. WHY IT MATTERS  0:20-1:00   purpose + three-word roadmap on one slide\n3. POINT 1         1:00-3:15   claim -&gt; evidence -&gt; so what\n4. POINT 2         3:15-5:30   claim -&gt; evidence -&gt; so what\n5. POINT 3         5:30-7:30   claim -&gt; evidence -&gt; so what\n6. WHAT IT MEANS   7:30-8:30   three points tied into one idea\n7. THE ASK         8:30-9:20   what you want the audience to do\n8. THANK YOU/Q&amp;A   9:20-10:00  name, contact, \"happy to take questions\"</code></pre>\n<p>Every body slide has the same inner shape: <strong>claim → evidence → so what</strong>. Claim is what you want them to believe; evidence is a number, a demo or a comparison; \"so what\" connects it back to the audience. Claim with no evidence is an assertion; evidence with no \"so what\" makes the audience do your thinking.</p>\n<div class=\"card\"><h4>Example question</h4><p>You have <strong>10 minutes</strong> before the principal and five faculty members to argue that your college needs a coding club. Write the full outline with time budget, slide count, and the exact words of your opening and closing.</p>\n<h4>Solution</h4>\n<p><em>Step 1 — Fix the message:</em> \"One hour a week, one lab, two faculty mentors — a coding club makes placement-relevant practice routine instead of optional.\"</p>\n<p><em>Step 2 — Three body points:</em> (1) the gap — what students do not currently get; (2) the proposal — what the club concretely is; (3) the proof — that it works elsewhere and costs almost nothing.</p>\n<p><em>Step 3 — Budget:</em> opening 1.0 min (2 slides) · gap 2.0 min (2) · proposal 2.5 min (2) · proof 2.0 min (2) · close 1.5 min (1) · buffer 1.0 min = <strong>10.0 min, 9 slides</strong>.</p>\n<p><em>Step 4 — Opening, verbatim (≈140 words ≈ 60 s):</em></p>\n<div class=\"note note--why\"><span class=\"tag\">Note</span><p>\"Sir, ma'am — one number. In our last two placement drives, 38% of shortlisted students cleared the coding round. The rest cleared the interview. They did not lack communication; they lacked practice at solving problems under time. That gap is fixable, and it needs no new building, no new lab, no new budget line. In the next ten minutes I'll make three points: what the gap actually is; exactly what a coding club would look like — day, time, room, mentors; and evidence that it works, at close to zero cost. At the end I'll ask for one hour, one lab, two mentors.\"</p></div>\n<p><em>Step 5 — Closing, verbatim (≈200 words ≈ 85 s):</em></p>\n<div class=\"note note--why\"><span class=\"tag\">Note</span><p>\"To bring it together. First the gap: a third of our shortlisted students lose offers at the coding round, and that is a practice problem, not a talent problem. Second the proposal: one hour a week, Saturday 4 to 5, Lab 3, run by two final-year students with two faculty mentors, on a simple ladder — basics, contests, interview sets. Third the proof: this already runs in colleges our size, and our own students have asked for it — 96 signatures in three days, which I have here. If you remember one sentence, let it be this: the cheapest placement improvement available to us is an hour a week we already own. I'm asking for three things: Lab 3 on Saturdays from 4 to 5, two faculty mentors, and a notice on the department board. Nothing else. Thank you — happy to take questions.\"</p></div>\n<p><em>Step 6 — Check:</em> 140 + 200 words ≈ 2.4 min of the 2.5 minutes budgeted for opening and close; the body gets 6.5 min ≈ 900 words, about 300 per point; nine slides. Fits with a minute spare.</p>\n<p><strong>Why this works:</strong> the hook is a number with a consequence, not \"today I will talk about\"; the roadmap names the points <em>and</em> previews the ask, so nobody wonders what you want; each point carries evidence rather than assertion; the ask is small and specific (small asks get approved, \"support our club\" gets a nod and nothing else); and the close repeats all three points so someone who dozed off at minute four still leaves with the message.</p>\n<div class=\"note note--trap\"><span class=\"tag\">Common trap</span><p>students plan the body in detail and improvise the opening and close, then overrun and end with \"so that's basically it\". The close is where the decision and the marks live — <strong>script the first and last 60 seconds word for word</strong> and rehearse only those; the middle survives on keyword cards.</p></div></div>"}, {"kind": "concept", "num": "5.2", "title": "Effective slide and visual design", "html": "<h4>Intuition</h4>\n<p>A slide is a <strong>billboard</strong>, not a page. You get about three seconds before someone decides whether to read it or ignore it — exactly like a hoarding on a highway, and nobody reads a billboard with eleven lines of 12-point text while driving. The test: if the slide were printed and stuck on the far wall, could you read the main line from here? If not, it is a document pretending to be a slide. Documents are fine; they belong in a handout, not on a projector.</p>\n<h4>The idea, step by step</h4>\n<p><strong>Step 1 — One idea per slide.</strong> Not one <em>topic</em>, one <em>idea</em>. If the title needs an \"and\" (\"Results and Limitations\"), it is two slides. <em>Mini example:</em> \"Method and Results\" becomes \"How we trained the model\" and \"What accuracy we got\".</p>\n<p><strong>Step 2 — Use the 6x6 rule as a starting point:</strong> roughly 6 bullet lines of roughly 6 words each. This is a widely used heuristic, not a standard — a guardrail against walls of text. A results table, a code snippet or a system diagram may break it completely and should. The absolute rule: <strong>if the audience is reading your slide, they are not listening to you.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Step 3 — Get the type sizes right.</strong> Title 32–40 pt; body 22–28 pt; captions and sources 16–18 pt; never below 14 pt. One or two fonts only, and a clean sans-serif (Calibri, Arial, Segoe UI, Helvetica) projects better at distance than a serif. Bold the one word you want landing, not whole paragraphs.</p>\n<p><strong>Step 4 — Control contrast and colour.</strong> Pick dark-on-light or light-on-dark and stay consistent. Never put text on a photograph without a solid or semi-transparent block behind it. Avoid red-on-green pairs — red-green colour blindness affects roughly 1 in 12 men and 1 in 200 women — and never let colour be the <em>only</em> signal; pair it with a label, shape or pattern. Three colours is plenty: dark for text, light for background, one accent. The 60-30-10 split (mostly background, some secondary, a little accent) is a handy convention, not a rule.</p>\n<p><strong>Step 5 — Choose the chart that matches the question in the audience's head.</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tbl-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Question</th><th>Use</th><th>Not this</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>\"How did it change over time?\"</td><td>Line chart</td><td>Pie chart</td></tr><tr><td>\"How do these categories compare?\"</td><td>Bar chart (horizontal for long labels)</td><td>Line chart</td></tr><tr><td>\"What is the split of the whole?\"</td><td>Pie or stacked bar, <strong>max ~5 slices</strong></td><td>Pie with 11 slices</td></tr><tr><td>\"Are these two things related?\"</td><td>Scatter plot</td><td>Bar chart</td></tr><tr><td>\"What is the exact value?\"</td><td>Table</td><td>Any 3-D chart</td></tr><tr><td>\"Where does the time go?\"</td><td>Gantt or timeline</td><td>Pie chart</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>Two more rules that buy marks: <strong>label axes with units</strong> (\"Accuracy (%), n = 12,000 images\") and <strong>delete 3-D, shadows, gradient fills and gridline clutter</strong>. Every decoration is a chance to look at the wrong thing.</p>\n<p><strong>Step 6 — Cut ruthlessly.</strong> Delete full sentences (keep keywords; you will say the sentence), the department logo on all nine slides (title slide is enough), animations and fly-ins (they cost rehearsal time and break flow), clip art and stock photos of handshakes, and any slide you would have to apologise for (\"this one is a bit small, sorry\").</p>\n<p><strong>Step 7 — Before/after critique.</strong> A real student slide:</p>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>BEFORE  Slide 4: Introduction\n- Our project is basically about developing a web based application which uses\n  machine learning algorithms, specifically a convolutional neural network, to\n  detect plant diseases from images uploaded by farmers, and this is very\n  important because agriculture is the backbone of India and farmers lose a lot\n  of money every year due to plant diseases, so we thought of doing this project\n  and we have also compared three models and the results are given later.</code></pre>\n<p>What is wrong, in order of damage: one slide carrying four ideas; 88 words at 12 pt, unreadable past row three, so the audience gives up; written to be <em>spoken</em> (\"basically\", \"so we thought of doing\"); a promise instead of content (\"results are given later\"); and not one number, so nothing is memorable.</p>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>AFTER   Slide 4: The problem in one line\nBIG     A farmer waits 3-7 days for a disease diagnosis.\nSMALL   Our app: one photo -&gt; answer in 5 seconds.\nFOOTER  Test set: 12,000 labelled leaf images (source: our dataset sheet)</code></pre>\n<p><strong>Why the after works:</strong> one idea; eleven words of main text; a before/after contrast the audience can <em>feel</em>; a footer that quietly proves real work; and room for you to say the sentences out loud, where they belong. Any figure you use must come from your own data sheet — never invent a number to make a slide look sharper.</p>\n<h4>The slide checklist (run before every submission)</h4>\n<ul><li>Title is a claim, not a label (\"Accuracy fell after 40 epochs\", not \"Results\").</li><li>One idea, sayable in one line.</li><li>Nothing below 18 pt in the body.</li><li>Six bullets or fewer, six words or fewer — or a justified exception (table, diagram, code).</li><li>Every chart: axis labels, units, and the source of the data.</li><li>Contrast checked from the back of the room, not from your chair.</li><li>No animation except one that reveals a comparison you are about to explain.</li></ul>\n<div class=\"card\"><h4>Example question</h4><p>Rewrite this slide for a three-minute project update, and say which chart replaces the accuracy sentence: <em>\"Results — We tested our model on three datasets and got accuracy of 94% on dataset A which had 8000 images, 89% on dataset B which had 5000 images and 91% on dataset C which had 3000 images, and also the training time was 40 minutes for A, 25 for B and 15 for C, and we also noticed that accuracy drops when the images are blurry which is a limitation.\"</em></p>\n<h4>Solution</h4>\n<p><em>Step 1 — Count the ideas:</em> two — accuracy across datasets, and the blurry-image limitation. So two slides, not one.</p>\n<p><em>Step 2 — Slide A:</em> title \"Accuracy holds above 89% across all three datasets\"; a <strong>grouped bar chart</strong> (x = dataset A, B, C; y = accuracy %) with data labels 94 / 89 / 91; footer \"Images: 8,000 / 5,000 / 3,000. Training: 40 / 25 / 15 min.\"</p>\n<p><em>Step 3 — Slide B:</em> title \"Blur is where the model breaks\"; big line \"Sharp images 94% → blurred images 71%\"; small line \"Next step: blur augmentation in training\". <em>(Use your own measured figure for the blurred case.)</em></p>\n<p><em>Step 4 — Justify the chart:</em> the audience's question is \"how do the three datasets compare?\" — comparison across categories → bar chart. A line chart would falsely imply the datasets sit on a continuum; a pie chart is meaningless because accuracies are not parts of a whole.</p>\n<p><em>Step 5 — Decide what to cut:</em> training times become a footer, not a third chart, because they are supporting detail. \"We noticed accuracy drops when images are blurry\" becomes a claim with a number, because without one it is an impression, not a result.</p>\n<p><strong>Why this works:</strong> every number survives, so nothing is lost for the examiner, but the audience gets two clear claims instead of one ninety-word sentence. Cutting information from the <em>slide</em> is not cutting it from the <em>presentation</em> — you say the rest.</p>\n<div class=\"note note--trap\"><span class=\"tag\">Common trap</span><p>reading the slide aloud. If the slide has the sentence and your mouth has the same sentence, the audience reads ahead, finishes before you and disengages. The mirror trap is 8-point text \"so that everything is included\" — that slide scores as <em>no slide at all</em>, because nobody can read it.</p></div></div>"}, {"kind": "concept", "num": "5.3", "title": "Verbal and non-verbal delivery", "html": "<h4>Intuition</h4>\n<p>Two people cook the same biryani from the same recipe; one is memorable, one is not. The recipe was identical — the <em>cooking</em> differed. Content is the recipe, delivery is the cooking. And delivery is not \"being a natural\": it is six adjustable knobs — <strong>pace, pause, volume, eye contact, gesture, posture</strong> — each of which improves in a week if you record yourself once and change one knob at a time.</p>\n<h4>The idea, step by step</h4>\n<p><strong>Step 1 — Pace: aim for 130–150 words per minute.</strong> Nervous speakers speed up, sometimes to 200 wpm, which is why they deliver ten minutes of content in six. The rule: <strong>slow down for numbers and for the sentence after a pause; speed up through familiar transitions.</strong> <em>Mini example:</em> \"Our accuracy is ninety-four per cent\", said about 20% slower than the paragraph around it, is the only line people will remember.</p>\n<h4>Step 2 — Pause: silence is punctuation.</h4>\n<div class=\"tbl-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Pause</th><th>Length</th><th>Where</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Micro-pause</td><td>0.5 s</td><td>Instead of \"um\"</td></tr><tr><td>Power pause</td><td>2–3 s</td><td>Right after your key claim</td></tr><tr><td>Transition pause</td><td>1–2 s</td><td>Between body points, while you move</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>The power pause feels like ten seconds to you and like confidence to them. It is also the cheapest cure for filler words: your mouth fills silence with \"um\", so give it a planned silence instead.</p>\n<p><strong>Step 3 — Volume and projection.</strong> Speak to the last row, not the first. Projection comes from breath support, not shouting: stand tall, breathe from the belly, aim your voice at a point on the back wall. With a microphone, keep it a hand-span from your mouth and do not turn your head away mid-sentence.</p>\n<p><strong>Step 4 — Eye contact: three anchors.</strong> Do not try to look at \"everyone\". Pick three anchor people — left, centre, right — and hold each for 3–5 seconds per sentence-group, sweeping slowly between them; everyone in that zone feels included. On a video call, look at the <strong>lens</strong>, not the faces on screen.</p>\n<p><strong>Step 5 — Gesture: inside the box.</strong> Keep hands in an imaginary box from waist to shoulders, in front of you. Open palms read as honest, palms down as calming, counting on fingers as structure (\"three reasons — one…\"). One gesture per idea is enough. Hands in pockets, crossed arms or a fiddled pen all read as nervousness, and pen-fiddling is worse than no gesture because it is visible rhythm noise.</p>\n<p><strong>Step 6 — Posture.</strong> Feet about shoulder-width, weight even, shoulders back and down, chin level. Stand still for a key point — walking while talking splits attention. Move deliberately between points, then stop and plant.</p>\n<p><strong>Step 7 — Filler words.</strong> The usual suspects: <em>um, uh, like, basically, actually, you know, I mean, so yeah</em>. They are not a character flaw; they are what your mouth does while your brain searches. Three fixes, best first: (1) <strong>replace with a pause</strong> — instant, free; (2) <strong>slow the sentence</strong> before the hard word; (3) <strong>plan your transitions</strong>, because fillers cluster exactly where you improvise a link. <em>Mini example:</em> \"So, basically, we, uh, used a CNN because, like, it works well on images\" → \"We used a CNN. Why? Because it is built for images.\" Shorter <em>and</em> stronger.</p>\n<p><strong>Step 8 — Handling nerves.</strong> Nerves are arousal, and arousal is fuel. <strong>Reframe</strong>: \"I'm excited\" beats \"I'm calm\" as a self-instruction, because both are arousal and one is labelled useful. <strong>Breathe 4-4-6</strong>: in for 4, hold 4, out for 6, five times, two minutes before you start — the long exhale slows the heart rate. <strong>Arrive early and walk the room</strong> so it stops being strange. <strong>Memorise only the first and last 60 seconds</strong>; everything between lives on keyword cards, because memorising the whole script is the fastest route to a blank mind — one forgotten word takes the whole paragraph with it. <strong>Use a physical anchor</strong>: feet planted, hands on the lectern for two seconds, one breath, begin.</p>\n<h4>The delivery checklist (five minutes before you speak)</h4>\n<ul><li>First and last 60 seconds memorised; nothing else.</li><li>Keyword cards only — maximum eight, one idea each, held in one hand.</li><li>Water within reach; sip before the first sentence, not during it.</li><li>Phone silent and off the lectern; clicker tested; standing position chosen.</li><li>Two power pauses marked on the cards with a slash.</li><li>Three eye-contact anchors chosen in the room.</li><li>One sentence decided in advance for \"I don't know\" (see 5.5).</li></ul>\n<div class=\"card\"><h4>Example question</h4><p>Diagnose the delivery of this opening and rewrite it with delivery notes: <em>\"So, um, hi everyone, my name is Rahul and, uh, today I'm going to basically talk about our project which is, like, a face-recognition attendance system, so, yeah, attendance is a big problem in colleges because, um, teachers waste a lot of time, and, actually, we thought, you know, that we could solve this with AI, so, uh, let's see.\"</em></p>\n<h4>Solution</h4>\n<p><em>Step 1 — Count the damage.</em> Eleven fillers in 45 seconds (<em>um</em> ×3, <em>uh</em> ×3, <em>like</em>, <em>basically</em>, <em>actually</em>, <em>you know</em>, <em>so yeah</em>). No hook — it opens with name plus \"I'm going to talk about\", the least interesting possible start. One run-on sentence with six commas and no full stop. It ends on \"let's see\", which signals doubt about your own content. Pace: 66 words in 45 s ≈ 88 wpm — slow <em>because</em> of the fillers, not deliberately, so the problem is not speed but <strong>absence of pacing</strong>.</p>\n<p><em>Step 2 — Rewrite (≈65 words ≈ 28 s at 140 wpm):</em></p>\n<div class=\"note note--why\"><span class=\"tag\">Note</span><p>\"Hi, I'm Rahul. [pause 1 s] In most of our classes, the first four minutes go to calling out roll numbers. [power pause 2 s] Ours takes forty seconds — one camera, no signatures, no proxies. [pause] I'll show you how it works, what it gets wrong, and what it would cost to run in one department.\"</p></div>\n<p><em>Step 3 — Delivery notes for the card:</em></p>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>[stand still, feet planted]   \"Hi, I'm Rahul.\"\n[PAUSE 1s]\n[slow, deliberate]            \"the first four minutes go to roll numbers\"\n[POWER PAUSE 2s - look at anchor L]\n[normal pace]                 \"forty seconds - one camera, no proxies\"\n[pause, then count on fingers] how it works / what it gets wrong / cost</code></pre>\n<p><em>Step 4 — The three knobs to change first:</em> replace every filler with a pause; open with the problem, not your name and agenda; end the segment on a promise (\"I'll show you three things\") instead of \"let's see\".</p>\n<p><strong>Why this works:</strong> the rewrite is 65 words against 66, so it is not shorter — it is <em>denser</em>. It swaps nine hedges for two designed pauses, which reads as control, and its last line gives the audience a map, which is 5.1 doing its job in the first thirty seconds.</p>\n<div class=\"note note--trap\"><span class=\"tag\">Common trap</span><p>practising by reading the script silently. Silent reading trains nothing — you cannot hear your own fillers or pace. <strong>Record once on your phone and listen at 1.25x speed</strong>: fillers become unmissable, and one recorded run fixes what three silent readings cannot.</p></div></div>"}, {"kind": "concept", "num": "5.4", "title": "Communicating technical content to diverse audiences", "html": "<h4>Intuition</h4>\n<p>You already do this daily: explaining UPI to your grandmother, to a classmate, and to a bank clerk are three performances of the same fact. You do not change the truth; you change the <strong>distance you start from</strong>. The failure mode is the <strong>curse of knowledge</strong> — once you understand something you cannot remember not understanding it, so you say \"we used a CNN with dropout and augmentation\" and genuinely believe that was clear. To a non-specialist it was three unknown words in a row.</p>\n<h4>The idea, step by step</h4>\n<h4>Step 1 — Do audience analysis on four questions.</h4>\n<div class=\"tbl-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Question</th><th>What it changes</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Who are they? (role, background)</td><td>Vocabulary, and how much background you give</td></tr><tr><td>What do they already know?</td><td>What you may skip without apology</td></tr><tr><td>What do they need from you?</td><td>Which of your five findings get airtime</td></tr><tr><td>What will they do afterwards?</td><td>Your ask and your close</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><em>Mini example:</em> the same project in three rooms. <strong>Guide's review</strong> wants method, validation, limitations. <strong>Placement panel</strong> wants what you built, what you learned, what you can do on day one. <strong>School outreach</strong> wants \"computers can help farmers\". Same content, three decks.</p>\n<p><strong>Step 2 — Build the analogy ladder.</strong> Write your project at three rungs and keep all three in your notes; you will use rung 3 in a viva, rung 2 with other branches, rung 1 at a family function or outreach talk.</p>\n<div class=\"tbl-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Rung</th><th>Audience</th><th>Rules</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1 — General</td><td>No CS background</td><td>No jargon. One everyday analogy. Only intuitive numbers.</td></tr><tr><td>2 — Peer</td><td>Same course, different project</td><td>Project-level jargon allowed if defined once. Compare with things they know.</td></tr><tr><td>3 — Expert</td><td>Guide, examiner, senior engineer</td><td>Full terminology, but state assumptions, baselines and limitations explicitly.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><strong>Step 3 — Handle jargon with three moves, in order.</strong> <strong>Replace</strong> if a plain word exists (\"we cached results\" → \"we saved the answer so we don't recompute it\"). <strong>Define on first use</strong> in one clause (\"a CNN, a type of model that learns patterns in images, …\") and move on — do not stop to teach. <strong>Park it</strong> if it needs real explanation: \"I'll use the term <em>inference</em>; it just means running the model on a new photo — I'll come back to it if anyone wants the detail.\"</p>\n<p><strong>Step 4 — Run three tests.</strong> The <strong>grandmother test</strong>: would a smart adult outside your field follow the first 60 seconds? The <strong>\"so what\" test</strong>: after each technical sentence, can you add \"which means…\" in plain words? If not, the sentence is decoration. The <strong>acronym audit</strong>: more than about five acronyms in a short talk means you are speaking to yourself — never assume API, GPU, CNN, LSTM or CRUD are free.</p>\n<p><strong>Step 5 — The full worked example: your project at three levels.</strong> Project: a face-recognition attendance system.</p>\n<p><em>Rung 1 — general (≈50 words):</em> \"In most classes the teacher spends the first few minutes calling roll numbers, and students answer for absent friends. Our system is a camera at the door: it looks at each face as you walk in, like a security guard who never forgets a face, and marks you present. Forty seconds for the whole class.\" <em>Where the analogy stops working:</em> a guard can be fooled by a photo held up to the camera — which is exactly the limitation you mention next.</p>\n<p><em>Rung 2 — peer (≈110 words):</em> \"A two-stage pipeline. Stage one is detection: a detector returns a bounding box per face in the frame. Stage two is recognition: each box becomes a 128-number signature — an embedding — which we compare against stored signatures for that class by distance, with a threshold. Closest match under the threshold is marked present; anything above goes into an 'unrecognised' bucket for the teacher. The interesting part is the threshold: too loose and proxies get in, too tight and real students get rejected for lighting. We tuned it on our own data and got 94% correct on 12,000 test images.\"</p>\n<p><em>Rung 3 — expert (≈160 words):</em> \"RetinaFace-style detection, then a FaceNet-style embedding with triplet loss, fine-tuned on our department's 210-student set. Each identity is stored as a 128-D embedding; matching is cosine distance at a threshold of 0.42, chosen on a held-out validation split by maximising F1 for the 'present' class. Baselines: raw pixel k-NN 71%, pre-trained without fine-tuning 86%, ours 94% — so fine-tuning contributes about eight points. Failure analysis: 62% of errors are low-light frames, 21% partially occluded faces. Our limitation is anti-spoofing: a printed photo can pass, so we do not claim liveness detection. The test split is by identity, not by image, to avoid leakage.\"</p>\n<p>Notice what changed across the rungs: the <em>claims</em> stayed identical. Only vocabulary, evidence density, and how explicitly limitations are stated changed. Rung 3 is the only one naming baselines and leakage — that is what an expert audience buys you: credit for precision.</p>\n<div class=\"card\"><h4>Example question</h4><p>Your project is an automatic MCQ answer-sheet evaluator. In <strong>2 minutes</strong>, explain it to a panel with one CS professor, one mathematics professor and one non-technical placement officer, so that all three stay with you. Write the script and mark where you switch levels.</p>\n<h4>Solution</h4>\n<p><em>Step 1 — Analysis:</em> three audiences in one room → one <strong>spine at rung 1</strong>, two short <strong>rung-3 asides</strong> for the CS professor, and one <em>accuracy</em> number for the maths professor. The placement officer needs the outcome, not the pipeline.</p>\n<p><em>Step 2 — Budget for 2 min (≈280 words):</em> rung-1 spine 120 words, two asides 60 each, close 40.</p>\n<p><em>Step 3 — Script:</em></p>\n<div class=\"note note--why\"><span class=\"tag\">Note</span><p><strong>[RUNG 1 — all three]</strong> \"Every semester our department evaluates roughly 6,000 MCQ sheets by hand. Each has 50 bubbles. Two people count, and they still disagree on about 2% of sheets — usually a faint pencil mark or a half-filled bubble. [<em>pause</em>] Our system photographs a stack of sheets, reads every bubble, and gives a score sheet in under a minute for a class of 60. Think of a scanner that knows the difference between 'filled' and 'almost filled' — that difference is exactly what humans argue about.\" <strong>[RUNG 2 — for the CS professor]</strong> \"Technically: we deskew the image, detect four corner fiducials to fix rotation, then sample mean intensity inside each bubble region and threshold it — adaptively per sheet, not globally, because photocopier contrast varies.\" <strong>[RUNG 3 — the number for the maths professor]</strong> \"On 500 sheets checked by hand we agreed with the human score on 496; all four disagreements were genuinely ambiguous half-filled bubbles. Mean absolute error: 0.4 marks out of 50.\" <strong>[RUNG 3 — one limitation, stated before it is asked]</strong> \"It cannot read handwriting, so descriptive answers are out of scope. And it needs the four corner marks printed — a sheet with a torn corner is rejected rather than guessed at.\" <strong>[CLOSE — rung 1]</strong> \"So: sixty sheets a minute, agreement with human marking on 99% of sheets, and it tells you which sheets it is unsure about instead of guessing. Thank you.\"</p></div>\n<p><em>Step 4 — Mark the switches:</em> four in two minutes is right for a mixed room. More and the non-specialists lose the thread; fewer and the CS professor concludes there is nothing technical inside.</p>\n<p><strong>Why this works:</strong> the non-technical listener gets a spine they never lose (counting sheets, saving time, settling arguments); the technical listeners get two precise asides and one honest limitation, which is what earns technical credibility; and the \"unsure\" behaviour is described in plain language <em>and</em> is a real design decision. Nobody is talked down to — the maths professor gets the most rigorous number in the talk.</p>\n<div class=\"note note--trap\"><span class=\"tag\">Common trap</span><p>confusing <strong>simplifying</strong> with <strong>dumbing down</strong>. Simplifying removes vocabulary; dumbing down removes the evidence. \"It's AI, it just works\" is dumbing down and is penalised heavily. Also: when you use an analogy, say where it breaks — \"like a security guard, except a guard can be fooled by a photograph, and so can we\" earns more than the analogy alone.</p></div></div>"}, {"kind": "concept", "num": "5.5", "title": "Handling questions", "html": "<h4>Intuition</h4>\n<p>Q&amp;A is not a test you can fail; it is the part where the audience tells you what they actually heard. Two presenters can give the same talk and the calmer one scores higher, because calm answering is <em>evidence</em> that the work is yours. Everyone has gaps — what is marked is not omniscience, it is <strong>how you behave when you don't know</strong>.</p>\n<h4>The idea, step by step</h4>\n<p><strong>Step 1 — Use the pause-repeat-answer-check loop (PRAC).</strong> Four moves, about fifteen seconds.</p>\n<div class=\"tbl-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Move</th><th>Time</th><th>What you do</th><th>Why</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>P</strong>ause</td><td>2 s</td><td>Stop, breathe, do not start talking immediately</td><td>Stops you answering the wrong question at speed</td></tr><tr><td><strong>R</strong>epeat</td><td>5 s</td><td>\"So you're asking whether the threshold was tuned per class\"</td><td>Everyone hears it; you confirm you understood</td></tr><tr><td><strong>A</strong>nswer</td><td>20–40 s</td><td>Direct answer first, then one reason or example</td><td>Answer-first is what a listener needs</td></tr><tr><td><strong>C</strong>heck</td><td>3 s</td><td>\"Does that cover it?\"</td><td>Catches the real question hiding behind the asked one</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><em>Mini example:</em> Q: \"Why didn't you use a transformer?\" A: \"We did try one. [<em>pause</em>] You're asking why we didn't ship it — [<em>repeat</em>] it was three points better but six times slower on CPU, so it lost on the constraint that mattered: a lab machine with no GPU. [<em>check</em>] Does that answer it, or did you mean the architecture choice?\"</p>\n<p><strong>Step 2 — Answer claim-first.</strong> Weak: \"Well, we thought about it, and there were several considerations, and eventually, after testing, we decided…\" — the listener waits twenty seconds for the answer. Strong: \"No. The CPU budget ruled it out. Here's the detail if you want it.\"</p>\n<p><strong>Step 3 — When you don't know, use one of four honest bridges.</strong> Never invent a number.</p>\n<div class=\"tbl-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Situation</th><th>Say this</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Data you don't have</td><td>\"I don't have that number with me. I can send it by tomorrow — may I note your email?\"</td></tr><tr><td>Outside your scope</td><td>\"That's outside what we tested. My guess would be X, but I'd want to verify before I claim it.\"</td></tr><tr><td>Needs a long answer</td><td>\"That deserves more than thirty seconds. Can I take it after the session, or send a short write-up?\"</td></tr><tr><td>You genuinely erred</td><td>\"You're right, that's an error on the slide. The correct figure is Y. Thank you.\"</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><strong>Step 4 — Handle hostile questions by separating person from point.</strong> A hostile question is usually a legitimate question in a bad tone. Do not mirror the tone; keep your volume and pace exactly where they were. Acknowledge the legitimate part (\"that's a fair challenge — if the baseline were unfair, the whole result falls\"), answer only that part, and time-box: one answer, not three rounds. <strong>Never try to win</strong> — being right and sounding aggressive loses marks.</p>\n<p><strong>Step 5 — Handle confused questions by re-framing.</strong> The question is often not the question. Ask a <strong>closed clarifying question</strong> — \"Do you mean the training data or the test data?\" — then answer the simpler version first, at rung 1 if needed, and offer the detail afterwards.</p>\n<p><strong>Step 6 — Split multi-part questions.</strong> \"Three things were asked: cost, timeline, privacy. Cost first — …\" Splitting is itself a mark-earning move, because it shows you were listening.</p>\n<p><strong>Step 7 — Manage the room.</strong> Repeat every question so the back row hears it. If five hands go up, \"let's take three, then I'll be around afterwards.\" Keep a <strong>parking lot</strong> — a corner of the board or a notes page for questions you will answer later — and say so out loud.</p>\n<h4>Three sample exchanges</h4>\n<div class=\"note note--why\"><span class=\"tag\">Note</span><p><strong>1 — hostile.</strong> Q: \"This is basically the same as the 2019 paper. What's actually new here?\" A: [<em>pause</em>] \"You're asking what's different from the 2019 work. Two things: that paper assumed a fixed threshold, and we tune it per class, which is where our eight-point gain comes from; and we report failure cases by lighting condition, which they don't. [<em>check</em>] If you meant the architecture, then yes — that part is the same, and I should have said so on slide 6.\"</p></div>\n<div class=\"note note--why\"><span class=\"tag\">Note</span><p><strong>2 — \"I don't know\".</strong> Q: \"What's the latency on a Raspberry Pi 4?\" A: [<em>pause</em>] \"On a Pi 4 specifically — I don't have that measurement. We ran on a laptop CPU at 210 milliseconds per frame. My expectation is the Pi would be several times slower, but I won't quote a number I haven't measured. I can run that test and mail you the result this week.\"</p></div>\n<div class=\"note note--why\"><span class=\"tag\">Note</span><p><strong>3 — confused.</strong> Q: \"So does it work offline or what?\" A: [<em>pause</em>] \"Let me answer the right thing — are you asking whether the app needs internet during class, or whether the model can be trained without it?\" Q: \"During class.\" A: \"Then yes, fully offline. The model runs on the phone; only the final attendance sheet syncs when there's network.\"</p></div>\n<div class=\"card\"><h4>Example question</h4><p>Answer both questions using the techniques above and explain what each answer is doing. (a) \"Your accuracy is 94% — but on how many students? That sounds like a toy dataset,\" asked sharply. (b) \"How does this compare to the commercial system the college already pays for?\" — you have never seen that system's internals.</p>\n<h4>Solution</h4>\n<p><em>(a)</em> Pause, do not react to the tone; repeat the factual core:</p>\n<div class=\"note note--why\"><span class=\"tag\">Note</span><p>\"You're asking how big the evaluation set is — whether 94% is meaningful. [<em>pause</em>] It's 210 students, 12,000 images, split by identity so no student appears in both train and test. You're right that 210 is small: one department, one semester. So I'd call 94% an internal number, not a general one — the honest claim is '94% on our own cohort', which is what the slide says. Generalising needs more identities, not more images.\"</p></div>\n<p><em>What it is doing:</em> concedes the true part (small dataset), refuses the false part (nothing was hidden), and upgrades the questioner's point into a limitation you already own. Tone is answered with calm, not defence; the marks come from the concession.</p>\n<p><em>(b)</em> Repeat, then split:</p>\n<div class=\"note note--why\"><span class=\"tag\">Note</span><p>\"You're asking how we compare to the system the college already runs. [<em>pause</em>] I can answer half of that honestly: I haven't seen their internals, so I can't compare architectures. What I measured is that our pipeline runs offline on a phone, and theirs, as far as I know from using it, needs the office network. On accuracy I can't make a claim, because I haven't tested theirs. If it's useful, I can ask the office for a demo sheet, run both on the same 500 images, and send you the comparison.\"</p></div>\n<p><em>What it is doing:</em> draws a clean line between <strong>what I measured</strong> and <strong>what I don't know</strong>, turns the gap into a deliverable, and refuses to invent a competitor's numbers. That behaviour is what separates a good viva from an excellent one.</p>\n<p><strong>Why these work:</strong> both start with the answer, not a story; both concede something real within two sentences, which lowers the temperature immediately; neither invents a figure; and both end with a check or an offer, so the exchange does not spiral.</p>\n<div class=\"note note--trap\"><span class=\"tag\">Common trap</span><p>three of them. Starting every answer with \"That's a very good question!\" — audible wallpaper that eats your thirty seconds. Answering a different, easier question out of nerves — examiners notice and ask again, harder. And arguing to win: you are not in a debate, you are being marked on composure, so \"you're right, I'll fix that slide\" scores higher than defending it.</p></div></div>"}, {"kind": "concept", "num": "5.6", "title": "Team and academic presentations", "html": "<h4>Intuition</h4>\n<p>A team presentation is a relay race, not four solo races back to back. In a relay the baton handover is practised — that is where races are won and lost — and in a team presentation the handover is the <strong>transition line</strong>, the one part almost every team forgets to rehearse. Audiences judge teams on two things that cannot be faked: does the story have one spine, and does everyone know what the others said.</p>\n<h4>The idea, step by step</h4>\n<p><strong>Step 1 — Split by content, not by slide count.</strong> Bad: \"you take slides 1–5, I take 6–10\" — that splits mid-idea. Good: \"you take the problem and the method, I take the results and the implications.\" Each person owns a <strong>complete thought</strong> with a beginning and an end.</p>\n<p><strong>Step 2 — Give everyone equal speaking time.</strong> For 12 minutes with four speakers: three minutes each. A rough convention that works: whoever owns the problem speaks the introduction, whoever owns the conclusion speaks the close, and the \"ask\" is delivered by the team lead. It is a convention, not a rule — what matters is that airtime is visibly balanced, because unbalanced airtime reads as unbalanced contribution and is often marked that way.</p>\n<p><strong>Step 3 — Rehearse the handover as a unit — the handoff sandwich.</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tbl-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Beat</th><th>Who</th><th>Example</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Land the last point</td><td>Speaker 1</td><td>\"So the gap is a practice gap, not a talent gap.\"</td></tr><tr><td>Introduce the next</td><td>Speaker 1</td><td>\"Which raises what we'd actually run. Aditi will take that.\"</td></tr><tr><td>Accept and confirm</td><td>Speaker 2</td><td>\"Thanks, Rahul. Yes — the design. Three decisions mattered.\"</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>Three sentences, six seconds, no \"over to you\" mumbling and no awkward clicker fumbling. Rehearse all of them; they are the cheapest marks in the deck.</p>\n<p><strong>Step 4 — One deck, one template, one style.</strong> One person owns the file; everyone uses the same slide master, fonts and accent colour. Nothing looks more like four separate projects than four different title styles.</p>\n<p><strong>Step 5 — Assign the Q&amp;A map in advance.</strong> Write down who answers which area: data questions → whoever built the dataset; method → the modeller; cost, timeline, next steps → the lead. If a question lands elsewhere, the wrong person must not answer it — \"That's Meera's area — Meera?\" is a <em>strength</em>, not an evasion.</p>\n<h4>Step 6 — Academic presentations: seminar and viva.</h4>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>SEMINAR (15-20 min + Q&amp;A)\n  title, context, why this topic       1.5 min\n  background / what is known           2.5 min\n  gap / research question              1.5 min\n  method (reproducible)                4.0 min\n  results (one chart per claim)        4.0 min\n  discussion: limitations              2.5 min\n  conclusion + future work             1.5 min\n  references slide (never read aloud)    -</code></pre>\n<p>A <strong>viva</strong> follows different rules: examiners <strong>interrupt</strong>, which is normal, not hostile. Expect \"why this method\", \"what would break it\", \"what did <em>you</em> personally do\", \"show me that number again\". Keep answers to thirty seconds unless invited to expand, and know your own contribution precisely — \"I built the detector and the evaluation harness; my teammate built the app.\" Vague ownership is the commonest viva failure.</p>\n<p><strong>Step 7 — The coordination checklist.</strong> One master deck file with a named owner and two backups · one shared template, identical fonts and colours · a time budget per speaker, everyone rehearsed to the clock · all transition lines scripted and rehearsed aloud · Q&amp;A map written down · two full team run-throughs, one in front of two live humans · files on a pen drive <em>and</em> emailed to yourself · one person owning the clicker and the one-minute warning signal · arrive twenty minutes early and test projector, audio, aspect ratio and fonts · agree dress code, water, and where each person stands.</p>\n<div class=\"card\"><h4>Example question</h4><p>Your four-member team has <strong>12 minutes</strong> to present \"AI in healthcare\" to a mixed panel of doctors and faculty. Give (i) the content split with timings, (ii) the exact transition lines between speakers 1 and 2, and (iii) the Q&amp;A map.</p>\n<h4>Solution</h4>\n<p><em>(i) Split — one spine, four complete thoughts:</em> A (lead) — hook, why healthcare, roadmap, then closing summary and the ask, 3.0 min, slides 1–3 and 9 · B — diagnosis support: imaging, what the models actually do, 3.0 min, slides 4–5 · C — operations: scheduling, triage, documentation, 3.0 min, slides 6–7 · D — the risks: bias, privacy, accountability, honest limits, 2.5 min, slide 8 · all — buffer 0.5 min. Total 12.0. Note A gets the bookends but no extra content: that is how you keep airtime equal while giving the story one voice.</p>\n<p><em>(ii) Transition lines:</em></p>\n<div class=\"note note--why\"><span class=\"tag\">Note</span><p><strong>A → B:</strong> \"So the opportunity is real, but every one of these three areas has failed somewhere. The one with the most evidence behind it is diagnosis. Bhavya will show you what the models actually do — and, importantly, what they don't.\" <strong>B:</strong> \"Thanks, Aarav. Yes — three things matter: what the model sees, what it was trained on, and what happens when it's wrong. Starting with what it sees…\"</p></div>\n<p><em>(iii) Q&amp;A map:</em> datasets, accuracy, validation → B · hospital workflow, cost, staffing → C · ethics, privacy, liability, bias → D · overall scope, next steps, collaboration → A. Standard out-of-turn line: \"That's Bhavya's area — Bhavya?\"</p>\n<p><strong>Why this works:</strong> each speaker owns a complete argument, so no handover lands mid-thought; the transition is a <em>content bridge</em> (\"the opportunity is real, but… the most evidence is in diagnosis\") rather than a stage direction, so the audience never notices the seam; and the Q&amp;A map means nobody is caught bluffing in someone else's area, which is the fastest way for a team to lose credibility as a unit.</p>\n<div class=\"note note--trap\"><span class=\"tag\">Common trap</span><p>the empty handover — \"…and now my teammate will continue.\" Six seconds of dead air, attention drops, and the next speaker starts cold. The other one: a single member speaking 60% of the time. Even if their content is best, the panel marks <em>teamwork</em>, and they can see the clock.</p></div></div>"}, {"kind": "concept", "num": "5.7", "title": "Rehearsal, feedback and self/peer assessment", "html": "<h4>Intuition</h4>\n<p>Nobody is born able to present, and nobody improves by presenting. People improve by <strong>rehearsing with feedback</strong> — attempt, observe, change one thing, attempt again — the same loop you use for a musical instrument or a sport. A presentation you never rehearsed is a first attempt being marked. The discipline that matters: <strong>one change per rehearsal</strong>, because change five things and you cannot tell which one helped.</p>\n<h4>The idea, step by step</h4>\n<h4>Step 1 — Follow a schedule, not \"I'll practise at home\".</h4>\n<div class=\"tbl-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>When</th><th>What</th><th>Focus</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>T–7 days</td><td>Content lock: outline fixed, slides drafted (2 h)</td><td>Structure only</td></tr><tr><td>T–5 days</td><td>Solo run, timed, standing, out loud</td><td>Does it fit the clock?</td></tr><tr><td>T–4 days</td><td>Record on phone, watch once at 1.25x (20 min)</td><td>Fillers, pace, eye contact</td></tr><tr><td>T–3 days</td><td>Fix the one worst thing, run again</td><td>One change only</td></tr><tr><td>T–2 days</td><td>Full run in front of two people, with Q&amp;A</td><td>Questions, handovers</td></tr><tr><td>T–1 day</td><td>Tech check: projector, fonts, video, clicker</td><td>Nothing new</td></tr><tr><td>Day</td><td>Warm-up: first and last 60 s aloud, plus breathing</td><td>Calm</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>Minimum viable version: <strong>three full timed runs</strong>, one recorded, one in front of a human. Fewer than three and you will overrun.</p>\n<p><strong>Step 2 — Rehearse in three passes, each with a different job.</strong> <strong>Content pass</strong> (sitting, no clock): is the argument right? Cut any point that does not serve the message. <strong>Timing pass</strong> (standing, clock, no slides): does it fit? If you overrun by two minutes, cut content — never speak faster. <strong>Delivery pass</strong> (standing, slides, recorded): fillers, pauses, eye contact, handovers.</p>\n<p><strong>Step 3 — Use a feedback rubric with marks.</strong> A workable 100-mark rubric for a ten-minute individual talk:</p>\n<div class=\"tbl-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Criterion</th><th>Marks</th><th>1 (weak)</th><th>3 (adequate)</th><th>5 (strong)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Content &amp; structure</td><td>30</td><td>No clear message; random order; no close</td><td>Message present; three points; thin close</td><td>Clear thesis; three signposted points; opening and close scripted and tight</td></tr><tr><td>Visual design</td><td>20</td><td>Text walls; &lt;14 pt; unlabelled charts</td><td>Readable; some clutter; charts mostly right</td><td>One idea per slide; chart matched to question; every number sourced</td></tr><tr><td>Delivery</td><td>25</td><td>Monotone, rushed, reads slides, no eye contact</td><td>Audible, some pauses, occasional eye contact</td><td>130–150 wpm; power pauses; three anchors; purposeful gestures</td></tr><tr><td>Engagement &amp; Q&amp;A</td><td>15</td><td>Ignores audience; off-target or bluffs</td><td>Some repetition; mostly on target</td><td>PRAC loop; concedes limits; never invents a number</td></tr><tr><td>Teamwork (teams only)</td><td>10</td><td>Unequal airtime; empty handovers</td><td>Balanced; functional handovers</td><td>Scripted content bridges; Q&amp;A map followed</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>Score each criterion 1–5 and multiply by its weight ÷ 5. Example: content 4 → 24/30, design 3 → 12/20, delivery 4 → 20/25, Q&amp;A 3 → 9/15, teamwork 4 → 8/10; total <strong>73</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Step 4 — Ask for feedback that can be acted on.</strong> \"How was it?\" earns \"it was good\". Ask instead: \"Where exactly did you lose track of me?\" · \"Which single slide would you cut?\" · \"Did I answer the question I was asked, or a different one?\" · \"Was the pace too fast at any point — which minute?\"</p>\n<p><strong>Step 5 — Convert vague comments into actions</strong> using <em>observation → specific moment → one change</em>.</p>\n<div class=\"tbl-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Vague</th><th>Actionable</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>\"You seemed nervous.\"</td><td>\"Your hands were in your pockets from 2:10 to 3:40. Next run: hands in the box, one gesture per point.\"</td></tr><tr><td>\"Slides are too busy.\"</td><td>\"Slide 6 has 14 bullets at 16 pt. Cut to six; move the rest to speaker notes.\"</td></tr><tr><td>\"Good energy but rushed.\"</td><td>\"You finished 2:40 early, so pace was about 175 wpm. Next run: three power pauses, and slow the numbers.\"</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><strong>Step 6 — Self-assessment, done properly:</strong> watch the recording three times, once per channel. <strong>Muted</strong> — body language only: posture, hands, movement, eye direction. <strong>Audio only, screen off</strong> — fillers, pace, pauses, volume; this changes behaviour fastest. <strong>Transcript or notes</strong> — structure: did you signpost, and did the claim come before the evidence?</p>\n<p><strong>Step 7 — Peer assessment, done fairly:</strong> score anonymously against the same rubric, then discuss. Two rules — comment on <strong>behaviour, not the person</strong> (\"the middle section had three claims on one slide\", not \"your slides were bad\"), and give <strong>one strength plus one change</strong>; more than one change per round is noise.</p>\n<div class=\"card\"><h4>Example question</h4><p>Your friend presents for ten minutes. You note: she finished in 7:20; she read most slides aloud; her three body points were good; she said \"basically\" 14 times; her hands were in her pockets; in Q&amp;A, asked about dataset size, she talked about model architecture. Fill the rubric (1–5 per criterion), compute the total out of 100, and write the three most useful pieces of feedback.</p>\n<h4>Solution</h4>\n<p><em>Step 1 — Score against the descriptors.</em></p>\n<div class=\"tbl-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Criterion</th><th>Score</th><th>Weighted</th><th>Reason</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Content &amp; structure</td><td>4/5</td><td>24/30</td><td>Three clear points in a good order, but 7:20 for a 10-minute slot means ~30% of content was cut live and the close was rushed</td></tr><tr><td>Visual design</td><td>2/5</td><td>8/20</td><td>Reading slides aloud is evidence they were text-heavy, not claim-based</td></tr><tr><td>Delivery</td><td>2/5</td><td>10/25</td><td>14 \"basically\" in seven minutes ≈ two per minute; hands in pockets; pace ~175 wpm</td></tr><tr><td>Engagement &amp; Q&amp;A</td><td>2/5</td><td>6/15</td><td>Answered a different question than the one asked — the classic nervous-substitution error</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><em>Step 2 — Total:</em> 24 + 8 + 10 + 6 = 48 out of 75 on the four individual criteria; scaled to 100 → 48 / 75 × 100 = <strong>64/100</strong>.</p>\n<p><em>Step 3 — Three highest-leverage fixes, ranked by marks recovered.</em> (1) <strong>Fix the clock</strong> (~10 marks): rehearse twice with a visible timer and put the close on a card so it is never what gets sacrificed. (2) <strong>Fix the Q&amp;A substitution</strong> (~6 marks): use pause-repeat before answering, because repeating the question forces her to hear what was asked; practise five questions read out by a friend. (3) <strong>Fix fillers and hands together</strong> (~8 marks): replace \"basically\" with a half-second pause, and stand with hands in the box for the whole of the next recorded run.</p>\n<p><em>Step 4 — What not to say:</em> not \"your slides were bad\" (personal, vague, no action), not \"you were nervous\" (a diagnosis she cannot act on), and not all eight problems at once — she can change one or two things per rehearsal.</p>\n<p><strong>Why this works:</strong> the feedback is <strong>scored, ranked and quantified</strong>, so it is arguable rather than a matter of taste; it targets the two or three changes with the biggest mark recovery instead of listing every flaw; and each item names a specific behaviour with a specific next action, which is the only kind of feedback that changes anything.</p>\n<div class=\"note note--trap\"><span class=\"tag\">Common trap</span><p>rehearsing in your head, or silently. Both feel like practice and are not. Second trap: collecting feedback from ten people and changing everything at once — the talk gets worse and you learn nothing. Third: rehearsing with slides only and never rehearsing the <strong>Q&amp;A</strong>, which is often 15% of the marks for three minutes of work.</p></div></div>"}, {"kind": "formula", "num": "", "title": "Formulas, rules and properties", "html": "<p>No formulas in this module — but here is the \"which rule when?\" guide you should recall in three seconds.</p>\n<div class=\"tbl-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>If the situation is…</th><th>Reach for…</th><th>Section</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>\"I must build a talk from scratch\"</td><td>One-line message → three points → 4-beat open → 3-beat close</td><td>5.1</td></tr><tr><td>\"10 minutes, 22 slides\"</td><td>1 slide per 1–2 min → cut to 7–10 slides</td><td>5.1, 5.2</td></tr><tr><td>\"My slide has too much text\"</td><td>One idea per slide, then 6x6 as a guardrail</td><td>5.2</td></tr><tr><td>\"Which chart?\"</td><td>Time → line; comparison → bar; share → pie (≤5); exact → table</td><td>5.2</td></tr><tr><td>\"I say 'um' constantly\"</td><td>Replace the filler with a micro-pause</td><td>5.3</td></tr><tr><td>\"I speed up when nervous\"</td><td>130–150 wpm; power pause after each claim; slow the numbers</td><td>5.3</td></tr><tr><td>\"The audience is not from my branch\"</td><td>Analogy ladder: general → peer → expert</td><td>5.4</td></tr><tr><td>\"They asked something I don't know\"</td><td>Four honest bridges; never invent a number</td><td>5.5</td></tr><tr><td>\"The question was hostile\"</td><td>Separate person from point; concede the true part</td><td>5.5</td></tr><tr><td>\"Our team talk feels disjointed\"</td><td>Content split + handoff sandwich + Q&amp;A map</td><td>5.6</td></tr><tr><td>\"I have a week to improve\"</td><td>Three timed runs, one recorded, one in front of a human</td><td>5.7</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"}, {"kind": "example", "num": "", "title": "Solved examples", "html": "<h4>Example 1 (easy) — cut the deck to fit the clock</h4>\n<p><strong>Task:</strong> You have 15 slides for a ten-minute slot: title, agenda, problem, three method slides, four result slides, two limitation slides, two future-work slides, comparison, conclusion, thank you. Reduce it to a correct deck.</p>\n<h4>Solution</h4><p>Ten minutes → 7–10 content slides plus title, so target <strong>nine including title</strong>. Keep: title; <strong>problem + roadmap</strong> (the agenda slide is almost always wasted airtime — put the roadmap as three words on the problem slide); <strong>one method diagram</strong> plus one text slide instead of three method slides; <strong>two results</strong> (accuracy and failure analysis), moving training curves to backup; <strong>one limitations</strong> slide with three bullets; <strong>conclusion + future work merged</strong> (they say the same thing); a minimal thank-you slide. Result: title, problem+roadmap, method diagram, method detail, results A, results B, limitations, conclusion+future, thank you = <strong>nine slides</strong>. Nine slides removed, zero claims removed — the training curves, extra method detail and comparison table become <strong>backup slides</strong> after the thank-you, used only if asked.</p>\n<div class=\"res\">nine slides for ten minutes, plus four to six backup slides in reserve. Deleting an agenda slide and a duplicated conclusion is the cheapest two minutes you will ever recover.</div>\n<h4>Example 2 (medium) — from paragraph to slide plus speaker notes</h4>\n<p><strong>Task:</strong> Convert this into a slide plus speaker notes: <em>\"Our system also has a teacher dashboard where the teacher can see who was marked present, who was marked absent, who was unrecognised, and can correct any entry manually, and all of this is stored in a database so it can be exported to Excel at the end of the month for the office.\"</em></p>\n<h4>Solution</h4><p><em>Find the single claim</em> — the dashboard is not a feature list; the claim is \"the teacher stays in control\".</p>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>Title:  The teacher can override anything\n[ PRESENT ]        [ ABSENT ]         [ UNSURE ]\n auto-marked        auto-marked        flagged for review\nfooter: Every entry editable. One-click export to Excel at month end.</code></pre>\n<p><em>Speaker notes (≈45 words, about 20 s):</em> \"The important design choice: we never make the system the final authority. Anyone unrecognised goes into an 'unsure' bucket, not an 'absent' one — because marking a present student absent is a far worse error than asking the teacher to look. The teacher can edit any row, and the month's sheet exports in one click.\"</p>\n<div class=\"res\">one slide carrying the claim (teacher in control), three labelled states instead of a bullet list, and the reasoning moved to speaker notes. The insight — <em>\"unsure\" is not \"absent\"</em> — becomes sayable because it is no longer buried inside a sentence.</div>\n<h4>Example 3 (exam level) — full marking-scheme self-evaluation</h4>\n<p><strong>Task:</strong> Score this 90-second opening against the 5.7 rubric (content 30, design 20, delivery 25, Q&amp;A 15), justify each score, and name the two changes that recover the most marks: <em>\"Okay so, um, good morning everyone, I'm Priya and this is my presentation on our project which is a chatbot for the college website. So basically the problem is that students ask a lot of questions to the office and the office is busy, so we made a chatbot. It uses NLP. Um, the chatbot answers questions about fees, dates, and hostel. We used a transformer model, and, uh, we got good results. So, yeah, in this presentation I will talk about the problem, the solution, the results, and the conclusion. Thank you.\"</em></p>\n<h4>Solution</h4><p><em>Content:</em> a problem-solution-results-conclusion roadmap genuinely exists, which earns credit, but there is no number anywhere (\"a lot of questions\", \"good results\" are impressions), the roadmap arrives at second 90 instead of second 10, and \"it uses NLP\" is a label, not a claim → <strong>2/5 = 12/30</strong>. <em>Design:</em> not judgeable from audio alone — state that, and score the observable proxy: a bare \"NLP\" label suggests the slide said only \"NLP\", and \"so yeah\" as a closer suggests no designed closing slide → <strong>2/5 = 8/20 (provisional)</strong>. <em>Delivery:</em> fillers — <em>um</em> ×3, <em>uh</em> ×1, <em>so / so yeah</em> ×3, <em>basically</em> ×1 = eight in 90 s; 105 words in 90 s ≈ <strong>70 wpm</strong>, far below 130–150, slow <em>and</em> filler-heavy, which usually means reciting from memory and stalling; no pause after any claim → <strong>2/5 = 10/25</strong>. <em>Q&amp;A:</em> none in the segment; engagement proxy — no \"you\", no rhetorical question, no reference to the audience's world → <strong>1/5 = 3/15</strong>.</p>\n<p><em>Total:</em> 12 + 8 + 10 + 3 = <strong>33/90 (≈37%)</strong>, a clear fail band — worth knowing, because students routinely assume a confident-sounding opening is a good one.</p>\n<p><em>Two highest-value changes.</em> (1) <strong>Put a number in the first 15 seconds and move the roadmap to second 10</strong> (~+12 across content and engagement): replace \"students ask a lot of questions\" with the real count — \"the office answers about 120 fee queries a month, and 70 of them are the same five questions\". (2) <strong>Halve the word count to remove the filler space</strong> (~+10 in delivery): cut \"so basically\", \"so yeah\", \"which is\", and the redundant \"in this presentation I will talk about the problem, the solution, the results, and the conclusion\" → \"three things: what we built, what it gets right, what it gets wrong.\"</p>\n<div class=\"res\">33/90 (≈37%) on the observed criteria; the two changes worth most are <em>quantify the hook and move the roadmap forward</em> (+12) and <em>cut the words to remove the filler space</em> (+10); projected post-fix score ≈55/90.</div>"}, {"kind": "trap", "num": "", "title": "Common traps and mistakes", "html": "<ul><li><strong>Content dump instead of argument.</strong> Listing everything you did instead of arguing three points costs Content marks and causes overrun, which costs Delivery marks. Fix: one-line message first.</li><li><strong>Improvising the opening and close.</strong> These carry the decision and the marks, yet they are what students leave unscripted. Fix: script both, 60 seconds each.</li><li><strong>No time budget.</strong> You discover the overrun live, then sprint. Fix: word budget = minutes × 140, then cut content — never speak faster.</li><li><strong>Text-wall slides read aloud.</strong> The audience reads ahead, finishes and disengages, and reading flattens your voice. Fix: claim on the slide, reasoning in your mouth.</li><li><strong>6x6 applied to everything.</strong> A results table or pipeline diagram legitimately breaks it. Fix: treat 6x6 as a guardrail against paragraphs, not a law.</li><li><strong>Wrong chart for the question.</strong> A nine-slice pie or a 3-D bar chart is a visible design error. Fix: match chart to the audience's question.</li><li><strong>Fillers treated as a personality problem.</strong> They are a pause problem. Fix: replace with silence; record and listen at 1.25x.</li><li><strong>Memorising the whole script.</strong> One forgotten word takes the paragraph with it. Fix: memorise only the first and last minute.</li><li><strong>Jargon with no definition.</strong> You lose the non-specialist half of the room and look unable to explain your own work. Fix: analogy ladder.</li><li><strong>Bluffing in Q&amp;A.</strong> The most damaging move available, because examiners ask follow-ups by design. Fix: four honest bridges.</li><li><strong>Arguing with a hostile questioner.</strong> Even when right, you lose composure marks. Fix: concede the true part, answer once, time-box.</li><li><strong>\"…and now my teammate will continue.\"</strong> Empty handover; the seam becomes visible. Fix: handoff sandwich.</li><li><strong>Unbalanced airtime.</strong> Visible on the clock and marked under teamwork. Fix: equal minutes, shared bookends.</li><li><strong>Rehearsing silently or in your head.</strong> Feels like practice, trains nothing. Fix: out loud, standing, timed, once recorded.</li><li><strong>Vague feedback loops.</strong> \"It was good\" teaches nothing. Fix: ask \"where did you lose me?\", change one thing per run.</li></ul>"}, {"kind": "revision", "num": "", "title": "Quick revision box", "html": "<ul><li><strong>Structure:</strong> one-line message → three body points → 4-beat opening (hook, connect, purpose, roadmap) → 3-beat close (summary, takeaway, ask). Say the message before, during and after.</li><li><strong>Time:</strong> 10 min = 1 open + 6.5 body + 1.5 close + 1 buffer; at 130–150 wpm that is 1,300–1,500 words; 7–10 content slides.</li><li><strong>Slides:</strong> one idea each, 6x6 as a guardrail, body ≥18 pt, high contrast, three colours, no 3-D, never read aloud.</li><li><strong>Charts:</strong> time → line; comparison → bar; share of whole → pie (≤5 slices); exact values → table. Label axes and units.</li><li><strong>Delivery:</strong> 130–150 wpm; power pause 2–3 s after each claim; three eye anchors held 3–5 s; hands in the waist-to-shoulder box; feet planted.</li><li><strong>Audiences:</strong> analogy ladder — general (no jargon), peer (defined jargon), expert (baselines + limitations). Say where an analogy breaks.</li><li><strong>Q&amp;A:</strong> pause → repeat → answer claim-first → check. Don't know? \"I don't have that number; I'll send it tomorrow.\" Never invent a figure.</li><li><strong>Teams &amp; rehearsal:</strong> split by complete ideas, equal minutes, handoff sandwich, written Q&amp;A map; three timed runs minimum, one recorded, one change per run.</li></ul>"}], "practice": [{"q": "Write the four beats of an opening for a five-minute talk on \"Why should first-years learn Git?\", with one line of actual script per beat.", "a": "<p><em>Hook:</em> \"Right now four of you in this room have a folder called <code>final<sub>final v2</sub></code>. That folder is why you need Git.\" <em>Connect:</em> \"Every group project this semester will lose work at least once, and it is entirely preventable.\" <em>Purpose:</em> \"In five minutes I'll show you the three commands that cover ninety per cent of what you'll ever need.\" <em>Roadmap:</em> \"First what version control actually does; then the three commands; then how not to break your teammate's work.\" <strong>Key step:</strong> the roadmap names the three points in order, in one breath, before you start point one.</p>", "tag": "easy", "qHtml": "Write the four beats of an opening for a five-minute talk on \"Why should first-years learn Git?\", with one line of actual script per beat."}, {"q": "Rewrite this bullet so the slide obeys one-idea-per-slide and 6x6: \"Our app has three modules, namely the login module which uses OTP authentication for security, the dashboard module which shows attendance statistics in graphical form, and the admin module which manages student records and can add or delete students and also export data to Excel.\"", "a": "<p>Three slides, one idea each. (1) <em>Title:</em> \"Logging in takes one OTP, not a password\" — body: \"OTP to registered number / no password to forget\". (2) <em>Title:</em> \"The dashboard answers one question: who is attending?\" — body: \"per-class bars / monthly trend\". (3) <em>Title:</em> \"Admins can fix any record\" — body: \"add, delete, export to Excel\". <strong>Key step:</strong> three modules are three ideas, so they are three slides; \"namely\" and \"and also\" are the tell-tale words that a slide is carrying more than one idea.</p>", "tag": "easy", "qHtml": "Rewrite this bullet so the slide obeys one-idea-per-slide and 6x6: \"Our app has three modules, namely the login module which uses OTP authentication for security, the dashboard module which shows attendance statistics in graphical form, and the admin module which manages student records and can add or delete students and also export data to Excel.\""}, {"q": "Your ten-minute talk ran to 12:30 in rehearsal and you cannot cut content. Give three legitimate options and the one option you must not take, with reasons.", "a": "<p>Options: (1) <strong>cut content</strong> — drop the weakest of the three body points and re-budget the clock; (2) <strong>compress within a point</strong> — move supporting detail to backup slides and answer it in Q&amp;A; (3) <strong>renegotiate scope out loud</strong> — if the slot is genuinely fixed and the content genuinely required, tell the organiser in advance, not the audience mid-talk. The thing you must not do is <strong>speak faster</strong> to fit: past about 175 wpm comprehension drops sharply and fillers increase, so you lose delivery marks <em>and</em> the audience. <strong>Key step:</strong> 12:30 against a ten-minute slot means roughly 350 words too many — about one body point's worth.</p>", "tag": "medium", "qHtml": "Your ten-minute talk ran to 12:30 in rehearsal and you cannot cut content. Give three legitimate options and the one option you must not take, with reasons."}, {"q": "A questioner says sharply: \"This is just a college project. Why should anyone care?\" Answer using the pause-repeat-answer-check loop, and name the one move that keeps your tone steady.", "a": "<p>\"You're asking whether this matters outside the college. [<em>pause</em>] Fair question. Three things: the method is the same one used in production attendance systems, the failure analysis is ours, and the cost number — zero rupees per classroom, because it runs on a phone — is the part a school would actually care about. If you meant 'is this novel', then no, and I've said so on slide 4.\" The move that keeps your tone steady: <strong>concede one true thing in the first two sentences</strong>, because concession costs nothing and removes the pressure to defend everything. <strong>Key step:</strong> answer once, then stop; do not open a second round.</p>", "tag": "medium", "qHtml": "A questioner says sharply: \"This is just a college project. Why should anyone care?\" Answer using the pause-repeat-answer-check loop, and name the one move that keeps your tone steady."}, {"q": "You are one of four speakers with 12 minutes total, before two faculty, one industry guest and one non-technical coordinator. Write the time split, the exact handover line from speaker 2 to speaker 3, and what each speaker must not do.", "a": "<p><em>Split:</em> three minutes each — Speaker 1 hook + problem + roadmap, Speaker 2 method, Speaker 3 results, Speaker 4 limitations + close + ask — with Speaker 1 returning for thirty seconds at the end to deliver the ask; state that explicitly. <em>Handover 2→3:</em> \"So the method is settled — two-stage, tuned per class. What that buys us is a number, and a number is only useful with its failures next to it. Kabir has both.\" <em>What each must not do:</em> Speaker 1 must not re-explain the problem in the close; Speaker 2 must not read the pipeline diagram aloud; Speaker 3 must not quote accuracy without the test-set size; Speaker 4 must not introduce new content on the limitations slide. <strong>Key step:</strong> the handover is a <em>content bridge</em> (method → the number it produces), not a stage direction.</p>", "tag": "exam", "qHtml": "You are one of four speakers with 12 minutes total, before two faculty, one industry guest and one non-technical coordinator. Write the time split, the exact handover line from speaker 2 to speaker 3, and what each speaker must <em>not</em> do."}, {"q": "Explain your own semester project at three levels (about 50 / 110 / 160 words), then add the single sentence you would use if an examiner asked \"what would break this?\"", "a": "<p>Model shape, substituting your own project. <em>General (~50 w):</em> \"It's a camera at the door that marks attendance by recognising faces, like your phone unlocking — but for a whole class in forty seconds.\" <em>Peer (~110 w):</em> \"Two stages: detect the face, then convert it into a 128-number signature and match it against the class list by distance, with a per-class threshold. The threshold is the whole game — loose and proxies get in, tight and real students get rejected.\" <em>Expert (~160 w):</em> \"RetinaFace-style detection plus a FaceNet-style embedding, triplet loss, fine-tuned on 210 identities; cosine distance, threshold 0.42 chosen on validation by F1. Baselines: pixel k-NN 71%, pre-trained 86%, ours 94%. 62% of residual errors are low light, 21% occlusion. No liveness detection, so a printed photo passes. Split by identity, to avoid leakage.\" <em>\"What would break this?\"</em> \"Any change in deployment conditions — a new camera angle, different lighting, or masked faces — because the threshold was tuned on one condition, and it would fail quietly rather than loudly, which is why low-confidence matches go to a review bucket instead of being marked absent.\" <strong>Key step:</strong> identical claims at all three rungs; only vocabulary, evidence density and the explicitness of limitations change.</p>", "tag": "exam", "qHtml": "Explain your own semester project at three levels (about 50 / 110 / 160 words), then add the single sentence you would use if an examiner asked \"what would break this?\""}], "file": "Semester_2/CSFE-CommunicationSkills/CSFE-M05.md"}, {"n": 6, "title": "Integration, Interviews & Certification (CO6)", "tagline": "Modules 1 to 5 taught four skills and then one applied skill.", "minutes": 60, "sections": [{"kind": "big", "num": "", "title": "Big picture, 30 seconds", "html": "<p>Modules 1 to 5 taught four skills and then one applied skill. This module does the thing that actually gets you hired: it makes the four skills <strong>operate together, under time pressure, in front of another person</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>The analogy:</strong> you have spent a year learning scales on a guitar — up, down, one string at a time. CO6 is the first time you play a song. Nothing new is being taught; the notes are the same. What is new is that they now have to arrive in the right order, at tempo, while someone watches.</p>\n<p><strong>Where you will use it:</strong> the campus placement process (aptitude test, GD, technical and HR interviews), internship applications, any certification exam for higher studies or a visa, and every working day after that — email, chat, meetings, feedback, escalation.</p>\n<p><strong>The one-line summary of the module:</strong> <em>listen to understand, speak to be understood, read to verify, write to be accountable — and do all four without freezing.</em></p>\n<p><strong>What marks actually come from.</strong> Interviewers and GD panels are not measuring how impressive your vocabulary is. They are measuring three things: can you be understood quickly, do you know what you are talking about, and would they be willing to sit in a room with you for eight hours. Keep those three in mind for everything below.</p>"}, {"kind": "basics", "num": "", "title": "Basics you must know first", "html": "<ul><li><strong>LSRW</strong> – Listening, Speaking, Reading, Writing: the four macro-skills of language.</li><li><strong>Integrated task</strong> – one task that forces more than one skill at once (listen to a talk, then write a summary). Almost all real communication is integrated.</li><li><strong>GD</strong> – group discussion: 6–10 candidates, 8–15 minutes, one topic, no assigned leader.</li><li><strong>STAR</strong> – Situation, Task, Action, Result: the standard shape of a behavioural interview answer.</li><li><strong>CEFR</strong> – Common European Framework of Reference, the six-level scale (A1 to C2) used across Europe and increasingly worldwide to describe language ability.</li><li><strong>Proficiency test</strong> – a standardised exam (IELTS, TOEFL, PTE) that reports your English level as a score or band.</li><li><strong>Portfolio</strong> – a collected set of your own work with evidence and reflection, used to show progress rather than claim it.</li><li><strong>Escalation</strong> – formally raising a problem to someone with more authority, because you cannot resolve it yourself.</li></ul>"}, {"kind": "concept", "num": "6.1", "title": "Integration of LSRW skills", "html": "<h4>Intuition</h4>\n<p>Think of a canteen conversation. You <strong>listen</strong> to your friend, <strong>speak</strong> a reply, <strong>read</strong> the screenshot they send of the notice, then <strong>write</strong> a message to the group confirming the time. Four skills, ninety seconds, no separation — nobody experiences these as four subjects.</p>\n<p>The classroom splits them because you cannot teach four things at once. Real life does not split them, and neither do exams — an interview is listening plus speaking, a GD is listening plus speaking plus reading the room, an assignment is reading plus writing.</p>\n<p><strong>The loop:</strong> input (listen, read) → processing (understand, evaluate) → output (speak, write) → feedback → back to input. Break any link and it degrades: the student who reads well but cannot summarise has a broken output link, and the student who speaks fluently but does not listen answers the wrong question — the commonest failure in interviews.</p>\n<h4>The idea, step by step</h4>\n<h4>Step 1 — See how each skill feeds the others.</h4>\n<div class=\"tbl-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>This skill…</th><th>…feeds this one</th><th>How</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Listening</td><td>Speaking</td><td>You borrow vocabulary, rhythm and structure from what you hear</td></tr><tr><td>Listening</td><td>Writing</td><td>Lecture notes become the raw material of your report</td></tr><tr><td>Reading</td><td>Speaking</td><td>Reading is where your range of sentence patterns comes from</td></tr><tr><td>Reading</td><td>Writing</td><td>Structure, cohesion and register are learned by imitation</td></tr><tr><td>Speaking</td><td>Writing</td><td>Saying an idea aloud first makes it easier to write clearly</td></tr><tr><td>Writing</td><td>Speaking</td><td>Writing an outline before a talk is the cheapest way to sound organised</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><strong>Step 2 — Use the one-task-four-skills template.</strong> Any integrated task should have four observable outputs:</p>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>1. LISTEN  -&gt; a talk, a podcast, a lecture, a meeting      (input)\n2. DISCUSS -&gt; 5 minutes in a group of 3-4, no notes allowed (speaking + listening)\n3. READ    -&gt; a short related text, 1-2 pages               (input)\n4. WRITE   -&gt; 150-250 word summary + one critical comment   (output)</code></pre>\n<div class=\"card\"><h4>Example question</h4><p>Design a 90-minute integrated LSRW task on the topic \"Should colleges ban AI-generated code in assignments?\" and state exactly what you would assess at each stage and how many marks each stage carries out of 20.</p>\n<h4>Solution</h4>\n<p><em>Step 1 — Set the four stages and their inputs.</em> Listen: a 10-minute recorded debate clip. Discuss: 15 minutes in groups of four, notes not allowed (this forces listening). Read: a 400-word opinion column taking the opposite view. Write: a 200-word summary plus a 100-word personal position.</p>\n<p><em>Step 2 — Build the time plan.</em> 10 min listen + 5 min individual notes · 15 min group discussion · 10 min read + 5 min notes · 30 min writing · 15 min feedback and self-assessment = <strong>90 minutes</strong>.</p>\n<p><em>Step 3 — Allocate marks out of 20.</em></p>\n<div class=\"tbl-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Stage</th><th>Marks</th><th>What earns them</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Listening notes</td><td>4</td><td>Five accurate key points; no invented ones</td></tr><tr><td>Discussion</td><td>6</td><td>Contributes at least twice; builds on someone else; brings in a quiet member; no repetition</td></tr><tr><td>Reading</td><td>3</td><td>Correctly identifies the column's thesis and one piece of evidence</td></tr><tr><td>Writing</td><td>7</td><td>200 words in own words; summary and opinion clearly separated; no copied sentences</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><em>Step 4 — Add the integration twist.</em> Require the written piece to quote <strong>one point made by a group member</strong> (attributed by name) — that single instruction forces listening to be carried into writing, which is exactly the transfer this sub-topic is about.</p>\n<p><strong>Why this works:</strong> every stage has an observable output, so nothing is assessed on impression; the \"no notes in discussion\" rule makes listening unavoidable; and the attribution requirement welds the four skills into one artefact instead of four separate exercises.</p>\n<div class=\"note note--trap\"><span class=\"tag\">Common trap</span><p>designing an integrated task that is really four separate tasks in a row — listen, then discuss something unrelated, then read something unrelated, then write a generic essay. Integration means the <em>same content</em> flows through all four stages. If your summary could have been written without the listening, the task is not integrated.</p></div></div>"}, {"kind": "concept", "num": "6.2", "title": "Group discussions (integrated practice)", "html": "<h4>Intuition</h4>\n<p>A GD is not a debate and not a conversation. It is a <strong>group of strangers trying to produce one decent answer in ten minutes while being watched</strong>. The panel is not counting who spoke most; they are watching whether the group got somewhere and whether you helped it get there.</p>\n<p>The classic image of a GD — everyone shouting, nobody listening — is what happens when ten people each try to be the leader. The candidates who get selected are usually the ones who made the <em>group</em> better, which sometimes means speaking fourth, not first.</p>\n<h4>The idea, step by step</h4>\n<p><strong>Step 1 — Know the anatomy.</strong> Typically 6–10 candidates, 8–15 minutes, a topic given on the spot (sometimes with 2–3 minutes to think), no assigned leader, no fixed conclusion required — but a group that reaches a position scores better than one that just stops. In campus placements the panel usually has 2–3 members with a marking sheet.</p>\n<h4>Step 2 — Know what is marked.</h4>\n<div class=\"tbl-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Criterion</th><th>Weight (typical)</th><th>What it looks like</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Content</td><td>30%</td><td>Facts, examples, a new angle; not repetition</td></tr><tr><td>Communication</td><td>30%</td><td>Clear, audible, structured; no fillers; listens visibly</td></tr><tr><td>Teamwork</td><td>25%</td><td>Builds on others, brings people in, does not dominate</td></tr><tr><td>Leadership / initiative</td><td>15%</td><td>Opens, summarises, resolves conflict, keeps time</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>Weights differ by institute — ask your placement cell for the actual sheet. The important point is that <strong>content is only about a third</strong>: three of the four criteria are about how you behave in a group.</p>\n<p><strong>Step 3 — Learn the five roles, and be ready to play more than one.</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tbl-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Role</th><th>What you say</th><th>Risk</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Initiator</td><td>\"Let's take three angles: cost, ethics, and practicality.\"</td><td>Starting badly is worse than not starting</td></tr><tr><td>Builder</td><td>\"Adding to what Rahul said about cost — the licence fee alone is ₹X.\"</td><td>Being invisible if you only ever add</td></tr><tr><td>Challenger</td><td>\"That works in theory; what happens when the network is down?\"</td><td>Sounding negative if you never build</td></tr><tr><td>Clarifier</td><td>\"Can we define 'ban' first — ban in exams, or ban in submissions?\"</td><td>Pedantic if overused</td></tr><tr><td>Summariser</td><td>\"So we agree on two points and disagree on one — the disagreement is about enforcement.\"</td><td>Summarising too early kills the discussion</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><strong>Step 4 — Have lines ready. This is the whole trick.</strong> GDs are won with five or six prepared sentence patterns, not with brilliant ideas.</p>\n<div class=\"note note--why\"><span class=\"tag\">Note</span><p><strong>Opening (only if the group is silent for 3+ seconds):</strong> \"We have about ten minutes. Shall we take it in three parts — where AI code helps, where it hurts, and what a fair rule would look like?\" <strong>Building:</strong> \"Building on that — if the point is learning, then the test matters more than the tool.\" <strong>Disagreeing politely:</strong> \"I see it differently, and here's why: …\" <strong>Bringing someone in:</strong> \"Priya, you were about to say something about the lab policy?\" <strong>Interrupting cleanly:</strong> \"Sorry to jump in — one quick point, then back to you.\" <strong>Summarising near the end:</strong> \"We seem to have converged on two things: disclosure, and oral defence. The open question is enforcement.\"</p></div>\n<p><strong>Step 5 — Run the full simulation.</strong> Topic: <em>\"Should AI-generated code be allowed in college assignments?\"</em> Four candidates, eight minutes.</p>\n<div class=\"note note--why\"><span class=\"tag\">Note</span><p><strong>A (initiator, 0:05):</strong> \"We have eight minutes. Can we take three parts — where AI code helps learning, where it breaks it, and what a workable rule looks like? I'll keep time.\" <em>(Sets structure, claims a useful role without dominating.)</em> <strong>B (builder, 0:40):</strong> \"Building on that — the useful distinction is <em>generation</em> versus <em>understanding</em>. If I can explain every line, the tool was a tutor; if I can't, it was a substitute for learning.\" <em>(Adds a frame everyone can use.)</em> <strong>C (challenger, 1:30):</strong> \"That's clean in theory, but nobody can verify 'I understand it' from a submitted file. So the rule has to attach to something checkable — an oral defence, or a marked in-lab component.\" <em>(Attacks the idea, not the person; offers a fix.)</em> <strong>D (silent so far, 2:15):</strong> \"…\" <strong>A (2:20):</strong> \"Dev, you've been quiet — what's your read on the enforcement side?\" <em>(Brings in a quiet member. This single move is worth more than two extra content points.)</em> <strong>D (2:25):</strong> \"Two things. One, our labs are graded on output, so students optimise for output. Two, if the rule is unclear, the honest students are penalised — they follow a rule nobody stated.\" <em>(Substantive, brief, uses \"two things\" structure.)</em> <strong>B (3:40):</strong> \"I'd push back slightly on banning it outright. Industry now expects AI-assisted coding; banning it in college makes us less ready, not more.\" <em>(Disagrees with a proposal, not with a person.)</em> <strong>C (4:20):</strong> \"Fair — so maybe the rule isn't 'ban' but 'disclose and defend'. Declare the tool, defend the code.\" <em>(Converts conflict into a formulation. Panels love this.)</em> <strong>A (6:30):</strong> \"We're near time. Let me try to summarise: we agree AI use should be disclosed; we agree assessment should include something the student can defend orally; we disagree on whether first-year labs should restrict it. That's the open question.\" <em>(Summarises accurately, names the disagreement honestly.)</em> <strong>B (7:10):</strong> \"Agreed, and I'd add one line — the disclosure rule should apply to faculty too, for fairness.\" <em>(One last value-add, then stops.)</em></p></div>\n<div class=\"card\"><h4>Example question</h4><p>The GD topic is \"Work from home: productivity or isolation?\" Eight candidates, ten minutes. You are the fourth to speak. Write your first contribution, one disagreement, one inclusion move, and your closing summary — and say what each is doing for your marks.</p>\n<h4>Solution</h4>\n<p><em>Step 1 — First contribution (about 25 seconds), after three people have spoken.</em></p>\n<div class=\"note note--why\"><span class=\"tag\">Note</span><p>\"Can I add a distinction that might organise this? We're mixing two different claims: whether people <em>work</em> well at home, and whether they <em>stay connected</em> at home. The evidence points different ways for each. Productivity data usually favours home for focused work; mentorship and onboarding clearly suffer. So the honest answer may be 'it depends on the task and the person's stage'.\" <em>(Content 26/30: introduces a frame, not a fact. Communication: signposted, one idea, no fillers. Teamwork: organises other people's points rather than replacing them.)</em></p></div>\n<p><em>Step 2 — One disagreement (about 15 seconds), after someone claims \"WFH always reduces collaboration\".</em></p>\n<div class=\"note note--why\"><span class=\"tag\">Note</span><p>\"I'd push back on 'always' — our own project ran entirely online last semester and the code review actually got faster, because everything moved to written comments. What broke was the informal help, the two-minute question at someone's desk. So it's specific kinds of collaboration that suffer, not all of it.\" <em>(Challenger role, but with evidence and a concession. Disagrees with the word \"always\", not with the person.)</em></p></div>\n<p><em>Step 3 — One inclusion move.</em></p>\n<div class=\"note note--why\"><span class=\"tag\">Note</span><p>\"Meera, you mentioned the internship batch earlier — was that different for them?\" <em>(Teamwork marks: costs you nothing, and panels notice it because almost nobody does it.)</em></p></div>\n<p><em>Step 4 — Closing summary (about 30 seconds), with about a minute left.</em></p>\n<div class=\"note note--why\"><span class=\"tag\">Note</span><p>\"Let me try to pull this together. We agree on two things: focused work is often better at home, and informal mentoring is worse. We disagree on whether that second point is fixable with scheduled check-ins or whether it needs physical presence. If we had to give one recommendation, it would be hybrid with fixed in-office days for the tasks that need proximity.\" <em>(Leadership marks: accurate, names the disagreement, produces a usable conclusion instead of trailing off.)</em></p></div>\n<p><strong>Why this works:</strong> every contribution is <em>shorter than the others and better shaped</em> — a distinction, a concession, a question, a summary. Nothing is a monologue. The frame in step 1 makes the rest of the discussion easier for everyone, which is what \"teamwork\" means on a marking sheet, and the summary converts eight minutes of talk into a decision, which is what \"leadership\" means.</p>\n<div class=\"note note--trap\"><span class=\"tag\">Common trap</span><p>four of them. <strong>Speaking five times with no content</strong> — frequency is not marked. <strong>Repeating the previous speaker in different words</strong> — panels hear it instantly and score it zero. <strong>Never conceding anything</strong> — a candidate who agrees with nothing reads as uncooperative, so concede one point explicitly and then add your difference. <strong>The fish-market trap</strong> — if the group is shouting, do not shout louder; pause, then use a calm \"let's take one at a time\" and you will stand out for the right reason.</p></div></div>"}, {"kind": "concept", "num": "6.3", "title": "Job, telephonic and video interviews", "html": "<h4>Intuition</h4>\n<p>An interview is not an interrogation; it is a <strong>structured conversation to reduce risk</strong>. The company has a problem (work that needs doing) and a risk (hiring the wrong person is expensive). Every question is a way of pricing that risk. \"Tell me about yourself\" is not small talk — it is \"give me a 90-second reason to keep listening\".</p>\n<p>Once you see it that way, the strategy becomes obvious: make the interviewer's job easy. Be clear, be specific, be brief, and give evidence instead of adjectives.</p>\n<h4>The idea, step by step</h4>\n<h4>Step 1 — Know the structure you are walking into.</h4>\n<div class=\"tbl-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Stage</th><th>Length</th><th>Who</th><th>What they decide</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Screening (often telephonic)</td><td>15–30 min</td><td>Recruiter / HR</td><td>Is this person real and roughly aligned?</td></tr><tr><td>Technical round 1</td><td>45–60 min</td><td>Engineer</td><td>Can they do the work?</td></tr><tr><td>Technical round 2 / managerial</td><td>45–60 min</td><td>Senior engineer / manager</td><td>Can they do it with us, at our level of ambiguity?</td></tr><tr><td>HR round</td><td>20–40 min</td><td>HR</td><td>Attitude, stability, fit, salary, joining date</td></tr><tr><td>Offer</td><td>—</td><td>HR</td><td>Terms</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><strong>Step 2 — Answer \"tell me about yourself\" with a 3-part spine: now → how → next.</strong></p>\n<ul><li><strong>Now (20 s):</strong> who you are, in one sentence, tied to the role.</li><li><strong>How (45 s):</strong> two or three pieces of evidence — a project, an internship, a competition — chosen to match the job.</li><li><strong>Next (15 s):</strong> why this role, why now.</li></ul>\n<p>Keep it to <strong>60–90 seconds</strong>. This is a convention of professional interviewing, not a rule, but going past two minutes loses the room.</p>\n<p><em>Full model answer (fresher, software role):</em></p>\n<div class=\"note note--why\"><span class=\"tag\">Note</span><p>\"I'm a second-year Computer Science student at [college], and I've spent the last year mostly building things rather than only studying them. [<em>Now</em>]  My main project is an attendance system that recognises faces — I built the detection and evaluation pipeline myself, and the part I'm proudest of is not the accuracy but the failure analysis: I found that most errors came from low light, so I added a review bucket instead of letting the system guess. Before that I interned for six weeks at a small startup, where I wrote the API layer for their booking flow and learned what it means to ship something someone else depends on. I also led a four-person team in our college hackathon, where we placed second — mostly because we cut our scope on day one. [<em>How</em>]  I'm looking for a role where I can keep working close to the code while learning from people better than me, which is exactly why this team's work on [specific area] is interesting to me. [<em>Next</em>]\"</p></div>\n<p><em>Why it works:</em> the \"now\" line is one sentence; the \"how\" gives three pieces of evidence, each with a <em>result</em> and one honest reflection; the \"next\" names the company specifically, which proves you read something. No adjectives about yourself (\"hardworking\", \"passionate\") — the evidence carries that.</p>\n<p><strong>Step 3 — Use STAR for behavioural questions.</strong> Behavioural questions start with \"tell me about a time when…\" and are asked because past behaviour predicts future behaviour.</p>\n<div class=\"tbl-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Letter</th><th>What goes in it</th><th>Share of the answer</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>S</strong>ituation</td><td>One or two sentences of context. Who, where, what was at stake.</td><td>15%</td></tr><tr><td><strong>T</strong>ask</td><td>What <em>you</em> were responsible for. Use \"I\", not \"we\".</td><td>10%</td></tr><tr><td><strong>A</strong>ction</td><td>What you actually did, in order, with decisions and reasons.</td><td>55%</td></tr><tr><td><strong>R</strong>esult</td><td>What happened, with a number if possible, plus what you learned.</td><td>20%</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><em>One complete STAR answer — \"Tell me about a time you missed a deadline.\"</em></p>\n<div class=\"note note--why\"><span class=\"tag\">Note</span><p><strong>S:</strong> \"Last semester our team of four had a database project due on a Friday, and on Wednesday our front-end person had to travel home for a family emergency. Two of the three screens weren't wired up.\" <strong>T:</strong> \"I was responsible for the backend, but as the one who had set the schema, I was the only person who understood how the data was shaped, so the integration became mine.\" <strong>A:</strong> \"I did three things. First, I cut scope with the team in fifteen minutes — we agreed to ship one screen properly instead of three badly, and I told the TA on Thursday morning rather than waiting for the deadline. Second, I wrote a thin adapter so the missing screens could be added later without touching the schema. Third, I stayed back Thursday night to test against the sample data the TA gave us, because our own data had been generated by the code that was broken.\" <strong>R:</strong> \"We submitted on time with one working screen, and we lost two marks out of twenty for the missing screens — but the TA used our adapter as an example for the next batch, and I learned that raising a risk two days early is worth more than a heroic night. In the next project I built the adapter first, and we never had that problem again.\"</p></div>\n<p><em>Why it works:</em> the Situation is 30 seconds, not three minutes; the Task uses \"I\"; the Action is the bulk of it and is <em>specific and sequenced</em> (\"first… second… third…\"); the Result is honest — it does not claim a triumph, it claims a lesson plus a later proof. Interviewers trust the answer that admits the two marks lost far more than one that claims everything went perfectly.</p>\n<p><strong>Step 4 — Communicate in technical interviews.</strong> What is assessed is your <em>thinking made audible</em>.</p>\n<ol><li><strong>Restate the question</strong> in your own words and confirm (\"so the input can be empty — should I handle that?\").</li><li><strong>State assumptions out loud</strong> (\"I'll assume the array fits in memory\").</li><li><strong>Give the brute-force idea first</strong>, in one sentence, then improve it (\"the obvious way is O(n²); can I do better? Yes, with a hash map\").</li><li><strong>Think aloud</strong> — silence for two minutes reads as stuck, even when you are not.</li><li><strong>State complexity</strong> when you finish, with a one-line reason (\"O(n) time because one pass; O(n) space for the map\").</li><li><strong>Test with a small example</strong> on paper, including an edge case.</li><li><strong>When stuck, say where you would look</strong> — \"I'd check whether a monotonic stack applies here; can I take thirty seconds?\" That is a legitimate move, not a failure. Bluffing is the failure.</li></ol>\n<h4>Step 5 — Telephonic etiquette.</h4>\n<ul><li>Take it in a <strong>quiet room</strong>, seated upright, with water, your resume, the job description and a notepad in front of you. Standing up actually helps your voice sound alert.</li><li>Confirm the caller's name and the role in the first thirty seconds: \"Thanks for calling — is this about the software engineer role?\"</li><li><strong>Spell your name</strong> if there is any doubt, and repeat numbers back: \"that's 9-8-7-6-5…, correct?\"</li><li>If you need time: \"May I take thirty seconds to think?\" is fine; dead air is not.</li><li>Ask one or two questions at the end, then confirm the <strong>next step and timeline</strong>: \"What are the next steps, and by when should I expect to hear?\"</li><li>End with a short thank-you, not a speech.</li></ul>\n<h4>Step 6 — Video interviews: setup and camera presence.</h4>\n<div class=\"tbl-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Element</th><th>Do this</th><th>Because</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Camera height</td><td>Lens at eye level (raise the laptop)</td><td>A low camera looks up your nose and reads as submissive; a high one reads as distant</td></tr><tr><td>Light</td><td>A window or lamp <strong>in front of</strong> you</td><td>Backlight turns you into a silhouette</td></tr><tr><td>Background</td><td>Plain wall, tidy, door behind you closed</td><td>Movement behind you steals attention</td></tr><tr><td>Framing</td><td>Head and shoulders, small gap above the head</td><td>Too close is aggressive; too far is a passport photo</td></tr><tr><td>Eyes</td><td>Look at the <strong>lens</strong> when speaking, the screen when listening</td><td>This is the single biggest video-interview mistake</td></tr><tr><td>Notes</td><td>Sticky notes at lens height, never a script</td><td>Reading is visible in the eyes</td></tr><tr><td>Dress</td><td>Full formal, including below the frame</td><td>You may have to stand up</td></tr><tr><td>Testing</td><td>Join the link 15 minutes early; keep a phone backup</td><td>Tech failure is the one excuse that never lands</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"card\"><h4>Example question</h4><p>Answer this one, in the format above: \"Why should we hire you?\"</p>\n<h4>Solution</h4>\n<p><em>(a) \"Why should we hire you?\" — three beats: what they need, what you have, the fit.</em></p>\n<div class=\"note note--why\"><span class=\"tag\">Note</span><p>\"From the job description, the two things this role needs most are solid Java and the willingness to debug other people's code. [<em>what they need</em>]  On the first: I've written Java for two years, and my last project was a 4,000-line library with tests, so I'm comfortable in a real codebase rather than only in assignments. On the second: in our hackathon, the bug that cost us four hours was in a teammate's module, and I found it by writing a failing test rather than by reading the code — which is now the first thing I try. [<em>what you have</em>]  What I'd add is that I'm early in my career and I know it. I'm looking for a team where I can be corrected quickly, and this team's code review culture is the reason I applied. [<em>the fit</em>]\"</p></div>\n<p><em>Why it works:</em> it answers the actual question (why <em>you</em>, not why the company is great), it quotes the job description so the answer is tailored rather than generic, and the last beat is honest about being junior — which reads as self-awareness, not weakness. Never say \"because I need a job\" and never list five adjectives.</p>\n<div class=\"note note--trap\"><span class=\"tag\">Common trap</span><p>three big ones. <strong>Reciting a memorised \"tell me about yourself\"</strong> — it sounds recited, and the follow-up question exposes it. Memorise the <em>spine</em>, improvise the words. <strong>STAR with no R</strong> — a story that stops at what you did, with no outcome, reads as unfinished; always end with a number or a lesson. <strong>\"We\" all through the A</strong> — the panel cannot tell what <em>you</em> did, so use \"I\" for your actions and \"we\" only for genuinely shared ones.</p></div></div>"}, {"kind": "concept", "num": "6.4", "title": "Workplace communication", "html": "<h4>Intuition</h4>\n<p>Workplace communication is not about being formal. It is about being <strong>easy to act on</strong>. Every message at work has a hidden second line: \"…and here is what I need from you\". The best colleagues are the ones whose messages you can read once, at speed, and know exactly what to do.</p>\n<p>The difference from college is <strong>volume and accountability</strong>. In college you write for one reader who already knows the context. At work you write for a reader with 60 unread messages who does not remember your project, and your message will be forwarded to someone you have never met.</p>\n<h4>The idea, step by step</h4>\n<h4>Step 1 — Structure email with BLUF: Bottom Line Up Front.</h4>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>Subject:   [Action needed by Thu 21 Nov] Lab 3 booking for coding club\nLine 1:    The ask, in one sentence.\nLine 2-4:  Why, and the minimum context needed.\nLine 5:    What I need from you, by when.\nSign-off:  Name, role, phone</code></pre>\n<p><strong>Subject lines carry the message.</strong> \"Regarding the thing we discussed\" is a subject that guarantees a delayed reply. \"Approval needed: Lab 3, Saturdays 4–5, from Dec\" is a subject that can be answered from the notification bar.</p>\n<p><em>Before/after rewrite:</em></p>\n<div class=\"note note--why\"><span class=\"tag\">Note</span><p><strong>Before:</strong> \"Respected Sir, I hope this mail finds you in good health. I am writing this mail to bring to your kind notice that we had a discussion last week regarding the coding club and you had told us to mail you the details, so I am mailing the details. We are planning to start a coding club in our college for the benefit of all the students so that they can improve their coding skills which will help them in placements. Kindly do the needful and please reply.\"  <strong>After:</strong> \"Subject: Approval needed by Fri 22 Nov — Lab 3, Saturdays 4–5, coding club  Sir, I need your approval on three points so we can start the coding club on 1 December.  1. Room: Lab 3, Saturdays 4–5 p.m. (currently unused at that time). 2. Mentors: two faculty, names attached. 3. Notice: one line on the department board.  The club runs one hour a week: basics, contests, interview sets. Cost to the department: zero. A one-page note and the signature sheet are attached.  Could you confirm by Friday 22 November? If any of the three is a problem, tell me which and I'll rework that part only.  Rahul Menon, 3rd year CSE, 98xxxxxxx\"</p></div>\n<p><em>Why the after works:</em> the subject is the ask plus a date; the first line states the decision needed; the three points are numbered so they can be answered \"yes to 1 and 3, no to 2\"; the deadline is explicit; and the close makes it easy to say no to one item instead of the whole thing. The before-version has no subject line, no ask, no deadline, and \"kindly do the needful\" — a phrase that transfers the thinking back to the reader.</p>\n<p><strong>Step 2 — Follow email norms.</strong> One topic per email. Reply within one working day — a common convention, not a law; if you need longer, send a one-line holding reply. Use \"reply all\" only when everyone needs it. Name attachments properly (\"CodingClub_Proposal_v2.pdf\", never \"doc1.pdf\"). Quote only the relevant part of a long thread. Never send an email when you are angry — write it, wait an hour, edit. Put the sensitive part in the <em>first</em> paragraph, because people stop reading.</p>\n<p><strong>Step 3 — Chat and IM norms.</strong> Chat is for quick, low-stakes, time-sensitive things; email is for decisions and records. <strong>No \"hi\" then waiting</strong> — send \"Hi, quick question about the deploy script: does it run on staging?\" in one message. One thread per topic. Respect status (\"in a meeting\" means expect a delay). Do not send bad news by IM — a missed deadline belongs in a call or an email where tone is not lost. And remember chat is a record: write as if it will be screenshotted, because it can be.</p>\n<p><strong>Step 4 — Behave well in meetings.</strong> Arrive on time; read the agenda beforehand. Speak once early if you have something — early contributions are heard, late ones are rushed. One person speaks at a time. Take your own action items down in the meeting, then send them in a two-line recap (\"Confirming: I'll send the dataset by Wednesday; Priya will check the licence\"). Do not hijack: if a topic is not yours, ask a question rather than starting a parallel discussion. If you have nothing to add, \"I'll take that offline\" is a complete contribution.</p>\n<p><strong>Step 5 — Give feedback with SBI, receive it without defending.</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tbl-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Model</th><th>Parts</th><th>Example</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>S</strong>ituation</td><td>When and where</td><td>\"In yesterday's review…\"</td></tr><tr><td><strong>B</strong>ehaviour</td><td>What was observable, not your interpretation</td><td>\"…the status update ran 12 minutes past the slot and two people's questions were cut.\"</td></tr><tr><td><strong>I</strong>mpact</td><td>The effect, on the work or the team</td><td>\"…so the risk item never got discussed, and we're now deciding it by email.\"</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>Notice what is <em>absent</em>: \"you are always disorganised\" (a judgement about a person), \"you don't care\" (a guess about motives). Feedback that contains a character judgement gets defended against; feedback that contains a behaviour gets acted on.</p>\n<p><em>Receiving feedback:</em> listen without interrupting, ask for a concrete example if none was given (\"can you point to the instance?\"), thank the person, and say what you will do differently — then actually do it. Do not explain why you did it; the explanation reads as a defence even when it is true.</p>\n<p><strong>Step 6 — Communicate across teams.</strong> Use the other team's vocabulary, not yours (\"the student record table\" not \"the <code>students</code> entity\"). Write a one-paragraph recap after any cross-team call, because shared understanding decays within a day. Always name a single owner for each action, never \"the team will\". And when two teams disagree, put the disagreement in writing with both positions — most cross-team conflicts are actually two different assumptions, and writing them down exposes that.</p>\n<p><strong>Step 7 — Escalate properly.</strong> Escalate when you are blocked and cannot unblock yourself, when a deadline is genuinely at risk, when a decision is above your authority, or when something is ethically wrong. Do not escalate to punish someone or to avoid a difficult conversation.</p>\n<p>The four-part escalation, in this order: <strong>facts → impact → options → ask.</strong></p>\n<div class=\"note note--why\"><span class=\"tag\">Note</span><p>\"The staging server has been down since 10 a.m. (facts). That blocks testing for the release on Friday (impact). Options: roll back last night's deploy, or use the backup instance which is a version behind (options). I'd like approval to roll back — it's the faster path, and it costs us one evening of re-testing (ask).\"</p></div>\n<p>Skip-level escalations (going above your manager) are legitimate but carry a cost: tell your manager first, always, even if you disagree with them.</p>\n<div class=\"card\"><h4>Example question</h4><p>Rewrite this message as a professional escalation, and say what you changed: <em>\"Hi, I have told you twice already that the API key is not working and nobody is replying, this is very unprofessional and because of this our whole module is delayed. Please look into it immediately.\"</em></p>\n<h4>Solution</h4>\n<p><em>Step 1 — Diagnose.</em> The facts are buried under emotion; there is no impact quantified, no options, no specific ask, and \"this is very unprofessional\" attacks a person, which guarantees a defensive reply. There is also no deadline, so it can be read tomorrow.</p>\n<p><em>Step 2 — Rewrite using facts → impact → options → ask.</em></p>\n<div class=\"note note--why\"><span class=\"tag\">Note</span><p>\"Subject: Blocked — staging API key failing since Monday (module due Fri 22)  Hi Sana,  The staging API key returns 401 on every call from our side; I've attached the request ID and the two timestamps. I raised it on the team channel on Monday and Wednesday, so I'm escalating to you as the owner of that service.  Impact: our payments module can't be integration-tested, and it's due Friday. If the key isn't sorted by Thursday noon, we'll release without the integration test and flag it as a known gap — which I'd rather avoid.  Two options on our side: (1) you issue a new key today and we retest tomorrow, or (2) we use the sandbox key with reduced scope. Option 1 is cleaner; option 2 unblocks us immediately.  Can you confirm which by Thursday 10 a.m.? Happy to jump on a five-minute call if it's faster.  Rahul Menon, CSE, 98xxxxxxx\"</p></div>\n<p><em>Step 3 — What changed.</em> Facts with evidence (401, timestamps, request ID) instead of \"not working\"; a timeline of prior attempts (so it is a record, not a complaint); impact with a consequence and a date; two options, with a recommendation; a specific ask with a time; and the accusation removed — the recipient is now a person who can solve a problem rather than a person being blamed.</p>\n<p><strong>Why this works:</strong> escalation is not a complaint, it is a <strong>decision request</strong>. Every part of the rewrite gives the reader something to act on, and the \"I'd rather avoid\" line makes the cost of inaction visible without threatening anyone.</p>\n<div class=\"note note--trap\"><span class=\"tag\">Common trap</span><p>\"kindly do the needful\" and \"please look into it\" — both transfer the thinking back to the reader and, in a busy inbox, they are the messages that get postponed. Also: escalating before you have tried the direct channel (which destroys trust), and putting a complaint in writing with no facts (which is a record of your frustration, not of the problem).</p></div></div>"}, {"kind": "concept", "num": "6.5", "title": "English-proficiency certification and CEFR levels", "html": "<h4>Intuition</h4>\n<p>CEFR is a <strong>ruler</strong>, not a prize. It answers one question: what can you actually <em>do</em> in English right now? The six levels are not percentages and not marks; they are descriptions of capability, and the point of them is that a university in Germany and an employer in Bengaluru can read the same label and mean the same thing.</p>\n<p>Proficiency tests are <strong>different rulers measuring the same height</strong>. IELTS, TOEFL and PTE each produce a score on their own scale, and all three publish an approximate conversion to CEFR. That is why a PTE 68 and an IELTS 6.5 can both mean \"B2\" — the number looks different because the ruler is different.</p>\n<h4>The idea, step by step</h4>\n<h4>Step 1 — Learn the six CEFR levels as \"can do\" statements.</h4>\n<div class=\"tbl-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Level</th><th>Name</th><th>Can do, in practice</th><th>Typical marker</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>A1</strong></td><td>Breakthrough</td><td>Understands and uses very basic phrases; introduces themselves; asks and answers simple questions about familiar topics if the other person speaks slowly</td><td>Can handle shop, directions, form-filling</td></tr><tr><td><strong>A2</strong></td><td>Waystage</td><td>Handles short, routine exchanges; describes their background in simple terms; understands short simple texts</td><td>Can manage a tourist trip; struggles with a work meeting</td></tr><tr><td><strong>B1</strong></td><td>Threshold</td><td>Copes with most everyday situations; describes experiences and gives short reasons; understands the main point of clear standard input</td><td>Can follow a familiar lecture with effort; writes simple connected text</td></tr><tr><td><strong>B2</strong></td><td>Vantage</td><td>Interacts with fluency and spontaneity in most situations; argues a viewpoint with advantages and disadvantages; understands technical discussion in their own field</td><td>Can work in English with support; can study in English</td></tr><tr><td><strong>C1</strong></td><td>Effective operational proficiency</td><td>Uses language flexibly and effectively for social, academic and professional purposes; produces well-structured, detailed text; understands implicit meaning</td><td>Can work independently in English; can present and negotiate</td></tr><tr><td><strong>C2</strong></td><td>Mastery</td><td>Understands with ease virtually everything heard or read; expresses themselves precisely, differentiating finer shades of meaning</td><td>Near-native command; handles high-stakes writing</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>Two honest notes. First, these are paraphrases of the official descriptors, not the official wording — the full \"can do\" tables are long, and exam boards publish their own. Second, <strong>levels are uneven across skills</strong>: a very common profile is B2 reading and writing with B1 speaking, which is why some universities ask for a minimum <em>per section</em> and not only an overall score.</p>\n<p><strong>Step 2 — Map the three big tests to CEFR.</strong> These are the test owners' own indicative mappings; treat them as approximate bands, not exact conversions.</p>\n<div class=\"tbl-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>CEFR</th><th>IELTS (overall band)</th><th>TOEFL iBT (total, out of 120)</th><th>PTE Academic (out of 90)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>C2</td><td>8.5–9.0</td><td>114–120</td><td>85–90</td></tr><tr><td>C1</td><td>7.0–8.0</td><td>95–113</td><td>76–84</td></tr><tr><td>B2</td><td>5.5–6.5</td><td>72–94</td><td>59–75</td></tr><tr><td>B1</td><td>4.0–5.0</td><td>42–71</td><td>43–58</td></tr><tr><td>A2</td><td>3.0–3.5</td><td>below 42</td><td>30–42</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>In brief: IELTS Academic is paper or computer, about 2 h 45 min, band 0–9 in half steps, with a face-to-face Speaking test. TOEFL iBT is computer-based in one sitting of about 2 hours, scored 0–120 (0–30 per section), with Speaking recorded. PTE Academic is fully computer-based, about 2 hours, scored 10–90, with several integrated tasks and machine scoring. Exact formats, section counts and timings are revised from time to time — check the official test website before you book, and check the specific requirement of the university or employer, because institutions set their own cut-offs (a common Master's requirement is \"IELTS 6.5 overall, no band below 6.0\"). Scores are typically valid for two years, and some institutions ask for a test taken within a shorter window.</p>\n<h4>Step 3 — Translate a band into \"what it means in practice\".</h4>\n<div class=\"tbl-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>IELTS band</th><th>What it usually means for you</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>5.5</td><td>You can survive daily life and simple coursework, but lectures and group work will be hard work</td></tr><tr><td>6.0–6.5</td><td>You can study in English, with effort; expect to re-read a lot and to be quiet in fast seminars</td></tr><tr><td>7.0</td><td>You can follow most lectures, argue a point, and write a coherent essay; occasional idiom gaps</td></tr><tr><td>8.0+</td><td>Comfortable across academic and professional registers; errors are rare and non-disruptive</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"card\"><h4>Example question</h4><p>A student has three score reports: IELTS overall 6.5 (with Writing 5.5), TOEFL iBT 90, and PTE Academic 68. (a) Place each on the CEFR scale. (b) A university asks for \"CEFR B2 overall, with no section below B2\". Does the student qualify on each report? (c) What would you advise?</p>\n<h4>Solution</h4>\n<p><em>Step 1 — Place each score using the mapping table.</em> IELTS 6.5 → <strong>B2</strong> (5.5–6.5). TOEFL 90 → <strong>B2</strong> (72–94). PTE 68 → <strong>B2</strong> (59–75). All three say the same thing in three different units, which is the point of CEFR.</p>\n<p><em>Step 2 — Check the per-section condition.</em> On the IELTS-to-CEFR mapping, 5.5 is the first band of B2, not B1 — so the CEFR label alone hides the problem. Universities usually write the rule against section <em>bands</em> (\"no band below 6.0\"), so the practical check is whether any section falls below that floor. Here Writing 5.5 is below a typical 6.0 floor, so on that wording the student <strong>fails the writing condition</strong> even though the overall band is fine. TOEFL 90 and PTE 68 are overall figures; the same per-section check applies to their section scores, which this question does not give.</p>\n<p><em>Step 3 — Advise.</em> Read the requirement literally and match it section by section, not by overall band. If Writing is the constraint, retake with a writing-focused plan (or choose the test whose writing task plays to your strength) rather than retaking blindly. And confirm with the admissions office in writing, because institutions convert scores using their own tables.</p>\n<div class=\"res\">(a) all three are B2 overall. (b) overall requirements are met on all three; the \"no section below\" condition fails on IELTS Writing 5.5 against a typical 6.0 floor, and cannot be verified for the other two without section scores. (c) advise a targeted retake or a formal written confirmation from admissions.</div>\n<div class=\"note note--trap\"><span class=\"tag\">Common trap</span><p>treating a band as a percentage (IELTS 7 is not \"70%\"), and treating the CEFR mapping as official for your institution. Mappings are indicative; universities publish their own conversion and their own cut-offs, and a claim like \"PTE 68 always equals IELTS 6.5\" is a convention, not a guarantee.</p></div></div>"}, {"kind": "concept", "num": "6.6", "title": "Practice tests and strategies", "html": "<h4>Intuition</h4>\n<p>A proficiency test does not measure your English; it measures <strong>your English under that test's specific rules, in that time limit</strong>. This is why a fluent speaker can score 6.0 while a less fluent classmate scores 7.0: the second one learned the format. Format knowledge is worth roughly half a band, and it is the cheapest half-band available.</p>\n<p>The second principle: <strong>practice tests are diagnostic tools, not workouts.</strong> Doing five tests without reviewing your mistakes teaches your mistakes to you. One test reviewed thoroughly is worth three tests done.</p>\n<h4>The idea, step by step</h4>\n<p><strong>Step 1 — Take one full test cold and mark it honestly.</strong> You need a baseline before a plan. Log every wrong answer with a <em>reason code</em>: spelling, misread question, distractor, ran out of time, unknown word, answered from opinion instead of text.</p>\n<h4>Step 2 — Apply section-wise strategy.</h4>\n<div class=\"tbl-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Section</th><th>Core strategy</th><th>The one habit that moves the score</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Listening</td><td>Read the questions before the audio; predict what kind of answer is coming (a number? a name? a reason?)</td><td>Write the answer as you hear it — do not wait for the end</td></tr><tr><td>Reading</td><td>Skim the passage in 90 seconds for the gist, then answer question by question, scanning for the location</td><td>Never read the whole passage at full speed first; you do not have the time</td></tr><tr><td>Writing</td><td>Spend 3–5 minutes planning before writing; one clear position, one idea per paragraph</td><td>Answer the exact question — off-topic essays score low however good the English</td></tr><tr><td>Speaking</td><td>Part 2 (IELTS): use the 1 minute to write 4 keywords, not sentences; then speak for the full 2 minutes</td><td>Keep talking; silence is scored as nothing</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><strong>Step 3 — Manage time explicitly.</strong> For IELTS Academic Reading: 40 questions in 60 minutes, so about <strong>20 minutes per passage</strong> including transfer — that is 90 seconds per question. If a question resists after 90 seconds, mark it, move on, come back. For IELTS Writing: Task 1 in 20 minutes, Task 2 in 40, because Task 2 carries about twice the weight of Task</p>\n<ol><li>In TOEFL, the sections are separately timed and you cannot borrow minutes between them, so a slow start is unrecoverable. In PTE, tasks are individually timed, so the discipline is per task, not per section.</li></ol>\n<p><strong>Step 4 — Know the common score-killers.</strong> These cost more marks across candidates than lack of vocabulary does.</p>\n<ul><li><strong>Spelling in Listening</strong> — \"accommodation\" misspelled is a wrong answer. Spelling counts.</li><li><strong>Word count</strong> — under the minimum in Writing costs marks directly (IELTS Task 1 needs 150 words, Task 2 needs 250).</li><li><strong>True / False / Not Given confusion</strong> — FALSE means the passage contradicts the statement; NOT GIVEN means the passage neither confirms nor contradicts it. If you had to reason it out from outside knowledge, it is NOT GIVEN.</li><li><strong>Memorised Speaking answers</strong> — examiners are trained to spot them and they score lower than imperfect spontaneous answers.</li><li><strong>Leaving blanks</strong> — there is no negative marking in IELTS and TOEFL, so a guess costs nothing and can gain a mark. Always answer.</li><li><strong>Ignoring the question type</strong> — \"discuss both views and give your opinion\" needs both views <em>and</em> your opinion; give only one and you cap your own band.</li><li><strong>Practising full tests without reviewing</strong> — the most expensive habit of all.</li></ul>\n<div class=\"card\"><h4>Example question</h4><p>A candidate has taken IELTS twice and scored 6.0 both times, with Listening 6.5, Reading 6.5, Writing 5.5, Speaking 5.5. They want 7.0 overall. Build a realistic six-week plan and say what is <em>not</em> worth doing.</p>\n<h4>Solution</h4>\n<p><em>Step 1 — Do the arithmetic first.</em> IELTS overall band is the average of the four sections, rounded to the nearest half band. Current: (6.5 + 6.5 + 5.5 + 5.5) / 4 = 24 / 4 = <strong>6.0</strong>. To reach 7.0 the average must be at least 6.75 (which rounds up to 7.0). So the four sections must total <strong>at least 27</strong>. That means gaining <strong>3 band points</strong> across the sections.</p>\n<p><em>Step 2 — Choose the cheapest three points.</em> Writing and Speaking are 5.5 each and are the weakest; Listening and Reading are already 6.5. A realistic target: Writing 5.5 → 6.5 (+1), Speaking 5.5 → 6.5 (+1), Listening 6.5 → 7.0 (+0.5), Reading 6.5 → 7.0 (+0.5). Total 27 → average 6.75 → <strong>overall 7.0</strong>. This is the plan; it does not require a 7.0 in any single section.</p>\n<p><em>Step 3 — Six-week plan.</em></p>\n<div class=\"tbl-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Week</th><th>Focus</th><th>Weekly hours</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1</td><td>Full diagnostic under exam conditions; build the error log; learn the Writing Task 2 question types</td><td>8</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>Writing: one Task 2 per day, 40 min timed, then rewrite after feedback; Speaking: record Part 2 answers, 2 min each, listen back</td><td>10</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>Writing Task 1 (data description language) + Speaking Part 3 (opinion and speculation); one Listening section test</td><td>10</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>Full test under exam conditions; review every error by type; fix the top two error types</td><td>10</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>Two timed Reading section tests + two Writing tasks; one Speaking mock with a teacher or a fluent friend</td><td>8</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>Light practice only; revise the error log; two Speaking mocks; sleep and timing routine</td><td>5</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><em>Step 4 — What is NOT worth doing.</em> Not more full tests (they diagnose, they do not teach). Not memorising essay templates — examiners discount them and they collapse when the question does not fit. Not learning advanced vocabulary lists; at 5.5 the problem is almost always <strong>task response and coherence</strong>, not word choice. Not ignoring Speaking because it feels uncomfortable — it is one of the two sections that must move.</p>\n<p><strong>Why this works:</strong> it starts from the arithmetic of the band calculation, so the target is derived rather than wished for; it attacks the two weakest sections where a full band is available, instead of grinding the two that are already close to target; and it separates <em>diagnostic</em> activity (tests) from <em>improvement</em> activity (targeted practice with feedback).</p>\n<div class=\"note note--trap\"><span class=\"tag\">Common trap</span><p>grinding full practice tests because they feel productive. They raise your <em>familiarity</em> and nothing else after the second one. Also: assuming overall band 7.0 requires 7.0 in every section — it does not, it requires an average of 6.75, which is why section-by-section arithmetic is the first thing to do.</p></div></div>"}, {"kind": "concept", "num": "6.7", "title": "LSRW portfolio development", "html": "<h4>Intuition</h4>\n<p>A portfolio is not a scrapbook; it is an <strong>argument about yourself, made with evidence</strong>. Anyone can write \"I have improved my speaking\". A portfolio says: here is a recording from August, here is one from November, here is my feedback sheet, here is what I changed, here is the difference.</p>\n<p>The principle is the same as a Git history. A claim is a commit message; a portfolio is the diff.</p>\n<h4>The idea, step by step</h4>\n<p><strong>Step 1 — Collect the right evidence, four or five items per skill.</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tbl-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Skill</th><th>What to collect</th><th>How much</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Listening</td><td>Listening logs: source, date, 5 key points, 1 new expression, 1 thing you disagreed with</td><td>6–8 logs</td></tr><tr><td>Speaking</td><td>Recordings of 2–3 minutes (presentation extract, GD contribution, interview answer)</td><td>4–5 recordings, spread over the semester</td></tr><tr><td>Reading</td><td>Summaries of 2–3 academic or news texts, 150–200 words each, plus one critical comment</td><td>4 summaries</td></tr><tr><td>Writing</td><td>One email, one short report or proposal, one argumentative paragraph, one corrected draft showing revision</td><td>4 pieces</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>Include the <strong>feedback</strong> as well as the work: a rubric score, a teacher's comment, a peer comment. Evidence of improvement needs a before and an after.</p>\n<p><strong>Step 2 — Write each entry in a fixed shape, so the portfolio reads consistently:</strong> title, task, what I did (3–4 specific lines), evidence (file, date, who gave feedback), what I learned (including what still fails), and one next action.</p>\n<p><strong>Step 3 — Build the document in 6–8 pages:</strong> cover page, contents, a 150-word introduction on where you started, the evidence table, four entries in the shape above, a 300-word reflection, and 150 words of goals for next semester.</p>\n<p><strong>Step 4 — Make the reflection do the work.</strong> The reflection is where marks are won, because it is the only part that cannot be copied. Use before/after pairs with specifics: \"In August I used about ten fillers a minute in recordings; in November it was three, measured by listening to both at 1.25x. What changed was that I started pausing instead.\"</p>\n<p><strong>Step 5 — Keep it honest.</strong> Do not include work you did not do, and do not describe a failure as a success. A portfolio that says \"this recording is still weak on eye contact; I am working on it\" is stronger than one that claims perfection, because it proves you can assess your own work — which is the actual skill being assessed.</p>\n<div class=\"card\"><h4>Example question</h4><p>Write the evidence table for a semester portfolio plus one full entry (Speaking), for a student who wants to show improvement in presentations.</p>\n<h4>Solution</h4>\n<p><em>Step 1 — The evidence table.</em></p>\n<div class=\"tbl-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Skill</th><th>Item</th><th>Date</th><th>Evidence</th><th>What it shows</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Listening</td><td>Listening log — technical talk on databases</td><td>5 Aug</td><td>log_01.md</td><td>Can extract five key points from a fast talk</td></tr><tr><td>Listening</td><td>Listening log — GD recording of my own group</td><td>20 Sep</td><td>log_04.md</td><td>Can hear my own interruptions; noticed I cut in twice</td></tr><tr><td>Speaking</td><td>Presentation extract, project review</td><td>28 Aug</td><td>speak_01.mp3</td><td>11 fillers/min, read from slides</td></tr><tr><td>Speaking</td><td>Presentation extract, second review</td><td>12 Nov</td><td>speak_04.mp3</td><td>3 fillers/min, three eye-contact anchors used</td></tr><tr><td>Reading</td><td>Summary — news article on AI in agriculture</td><td>15 Sep</td><td>read_02.md</td><td>Can separate claim from evidence</td></tr><tr><td>Reading</td><td>Summary — paper abstract with critique</td><td>30 Oct</td><td>read_04.md</td><td>Can question a method, not just summarise it</td></tr><tr><td>Writing</td><td>Email requesting lab access</td><td>2 Sep</td><td>write_01.md</td><td>Clear ask and deadline, but too long</td></tr><tr><td>Writing</td><td>Revised email after feedback</td><td>6 Sep</td><td>write_01_rev.md</td><td>Cut from 180 to 90 words; BLUF added</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><em>Step 2 — One full entry.</em></p>\n<div class=\"note note--why\"><span class=\"tag\">Note</span><p><strong>Speaking — project review presentation, 12 November</strong> <em>Task:</em> Present a five-minute project update to two faculty and answer questions. <em>What I did:</em> Scripted the first and last 60 seconds word for word, kept the middle on four keyword cards, and rehearsed three times with a timer, recording the second run. In Q&amp;A I used the pause-repeat-answer-check loop for both questions. <em>Evidence:</em> speak_04.mp3 (recording), rubric sheet scored 74/100 by the reviewer, my own self-assessment notes from 13 Nov. <em>What I learned:</em> Fillers dropped from 11 per minute in August to 3, mostly because I replaced them with a half-second pause. I also learned that my biggest remaining weakness is the middle section: without a card I drift, and the second body point ran 40 seconds long. The reviewer's comment \"you were much clearer on the opening than on the third point\" matched my own recording. <em>Next action:</em> Rehearse the middle with the same discipline as the opening — one card per claim, and a visible timer on the desk for the next review.</p></div>\n<p><strong>Why this works:</strong> the entry contains a <em>measurable</em> claim (11 → 3 fillers per minute), names its evidence so anyone can check it, admits the remaining weakness with a specific observation, and ends with one action rather than a wish. That is the difference between a portfolio and a folder.</p>\n<div class=\"note note--trap\"><span class=\"tag\">Common trap</span><p>a portfolio that is 40 pages of certificates with no reflection. Certificates prove attendance; the reflection proves learning. Also: collecting everything and selecting nothing — a portfolio is curated, so four good entries beat twenty unlabelled files.</p></div></div>"}, {"kind": "concept", "num": "6.8", "title": "Lifelong learning and integrated mock assessment", "html": "<h4>Intuition</h4>\n<p>Your English will not stay where it is. Two forces act on it: <strong>use</strong> (a job that makes you write daily will pull you up) and <strong>decay</strong> (a year of only reading textbooks will pull you down). Lifelong learning here does not mean courses; it means a <strong>maintenance routine</strong> small enough that you will actually keep doing it.</p>\n<p>The second idea is the mock assessment: you cannot improve what you have never measured under realistic conditions. A mock is a rehearsal of the <em>whole</em> loop — listen, discuss, read, write, interview — not of one skill in isolation.</p>\n<h4>The idea, step by step</h4>\n<p><strong>Step 1 — Build a maintenance routine you will keep.</strong> Thirty minutes three times a week beats a two-hour Sunday session that happens twice: 10 minutes listening (a talk with a transcript), 10 minutes speaking aloud (record 2 minutes on what you heard), 10 minutes writing (a 100-word summary). Add one hour a week of reading something longer than an article.</p>\n<p><strong>Step 2 — Practise deliberately, not just more.</strong> Pick one weakness, work on it with feedback, at the edge of your ability. \"Watch more English videos\" is exposure; \"record a 2-minute answer, listen for fillers, re-record\" is practice, because it has a target and a check.</p>\n<p><strong>Step 3 — Build feedback loops you can run yourself.</strong> A recording plus a rubric is one. So is an error log: the sentence, the correction, the <em>type</em> of error — three columns, and after a month the pattern is undeniable. AI tools help here (transcribing, counting fillers, tightening a sentence) as long as you treat the output as a draft opinion, not the truth.</p>\n<p><strong>Step 4 — Run an integrated mock assessment.</strong> Ninety minutes, four stages, scored.</p>\n<div class=\"tbl-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Stage</th><th>Time</th><th>Task</th><th>Marks</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Listening</td><td>15 min</td><td>6-minute talk, then write 5 key points + 1 question you would ask</td><td>15</td></tr><tr><td>Group discussion</td><td>20 min</td><td>4–6 people, one topic, marked on the 6.2 table</td><td>25</td></tr><tr><td>Reading + writing</td><td>30 min</td><td>1-page text, then a 200-word summary + 100-word opinion</td><td>25</td></tr><tr><td>Interview</td><td>20 min</td><td>8 min technical/HR questions + 5 min Q&amp;A + 5 min feedback</td><td>35</td></tr><tr><td>Self-assessment</td><td>5 min</td><td>Fill the self-rating sheet</td><td>—</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><strong>Step 5 — Fill a self-rating sheet, and date it.</strong> Rate 1–5, where 1 = \"I avoid this\", 3 = \"I can do it with preparation\", 5 = \"I can do it cold, under time\".</p>\n<div class=\"tbl-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>1</th><th>2</th><th>3</th><th>4</th><th>5</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>I can give a 2-minute impromptu answer with a clear structure</td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td></td></tr><tr><td>I can answer \"tell me about yourself\" in 90 seconds, tailored to the role</td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td></td></tr><tr><td>I can give a STAR answer with a quantified result</td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td></td></tr><tr><td>I can contribute three substantive points in a 10-minute GD</td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td></td></tr><tr><td>I can bring a quiet member into a discussion without awkwardness</td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td></td></tr><tr><td>I can write a professional email with the ask in the first line</td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td></td></tr><tr><td>I can follow a fast talk in an unfamiliar accent and take usable notes</td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td></td></tr><tr><td>I can handle a question I don't know without bluffing</td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td></td></tr><tr><td>I can receive critical feedback without defending myself</td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>The two lowest-scoring rows are your next month's plan. Re-rate after four weeks; the numbers should move, and if they do not, the practice was not deliberate.</p>\n<div class=\"card\"><h4>Example question</h4><p>A second-year student rates herself 2 on \"STAR answer with a quantified result\", 3 on \"GD with three substantive points\", and 4 or 5 on everything else. Build her 90-minute mock assessment plan for the next four weeks and say how she will know it worked.</p>\n<h4>Solution</h4>\n<p><em>Step 1 — Turn the two weak rows into two practice targets.</em> Target 1: produce six STAR answers with a real number in the R. Target 2: three substantive contributions per GD without repeating anyone.</p>\n<p><em>Step 2 — Weekly 90-minute mock, same shape every week so progress is comparable.</em></p>\n<div class=\"tbl-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Week</th><th>Mock focus</th><th>Extra drill during the week</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1</td><td>Full mock as a baseline; record the interview stage</td><td>Write 3 STAR stories from her own projects (deadline, conflict, failure)</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>Interview stage only, 20 min, 8 questions, scored on the rubric</td><td>Re-record the weakest STAR answer twice; add a number to each R</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>GD stage only, 20 min with 4 peers, marked on the 6.2 table</td><td>Prepare five \"building\" lines and use them; count repetitions in the recording</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>Full mock again, same conditions, same rubric</td><td>Compare week 1 and week 4 recordings side by side</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><em>Step 3 — Define \"it worked\" before starting.</em> Success is not \"felt better\". It is: (a) all six STAR answers contain a specific number and a lesson in the R; (b) in the week-4 GD recording, at least three of her contributions add something no one had said; (c) her self-rating on those two rows moves from 2 to 3, and a peer scores her at least 3 on the same items using the rubric.</p>\n<p><em>Step 4 — Build the feedback loop.</em> After each mock: one recording, one rubric, one written \"one thing to change\". No more than one change per week, or the comparison across weeks means nothing.</p>\n<p><strong>Why this works:</strong> the plan is derived from the two lowest self-ratings rather than from a generic syllabus; every week has a measurable output that can be compared across weeks; and success is defined in observable terms before the practice starts, which is what turns \"practice\" into \"deliberate practice\".</p>\n<div class=\"note note--trap\"><span class=\"tag\">Common trap</span><p>rating yourself 4 or 5 on everything, which makes the sheet useless — the sheet is only valuable where it is uncomfortable. And the related trap: practising only what you are already good at, because it feels productive. The two lowest rows are the whole point.</p></div></div>"}, {"kind": "formula", "num": "", "title": "Formulas, rules and properties", "html": "<p>No formulas in this module either — here is the \"which structure when?\" guide.</p>\n<div class=\"tbl-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>If the situation is…</th><th>Reach for…</th><th>Section</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>\"One task, all four skills\"</td><td>Listen → discuss → read → write, same content through all four</td><td>6.1</td></tr><tr><td>\"The GD has started and everyone is shouting\"</td><td>Pause, then propose a structure; bring in a quiet member</td><td>6.2</td></tr><tr><td>\"Tell me about yourself\"</td><td>Now → how → next, in 60–90 seconds</td><td>6.3</td></tr><tr><td>\"Tell me about a time when…\"</td><td>STAR, with 55% of the words in Action and a number in Result</td><td>6.3</td></tr><tr><td>\"I'm stuck in a technical question\"</td><td>Restate, state assumptions, brute force first, think aloud, state complexity</td><td>6.3</td></tr><tr><td>\"I need something from a busy person\"</td><td>BLUF email: ask in the subject, numbered points, a deadline</td><td>6.4</td></tr><tr><td>\"A colleague's work is causing a problem\"</td><td>SBI: situation, behaviour, impact — never a character judgement</td><td>6.4</td></tr><tr><td>\"I'm blocked and nobody is responding\"</td><td>Escalate with facts → impact → options → ask</td><td>6.4</td></tr><tr><td>\"What does my score mean?\"</td><td>CEFR band, then check the institution's own cut-off, per section</td><td>6.5</td></tr><tr><td>\"I have six weeks to raise my band\"</td><td>Do the band arithmetic first; attack the two weakest sections</td><td>6.6</td></tr><tr><td>\"How do I prove I improved?\"</td><td>Portfolio entry: task, action, evidence, learning, next action</td><td>6.7</td></tr><tr><td>\"What should I work on next?\"</td><td>The two lowest rows of the self-rating sheet</td><td>6.8</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"}, {"kind": "example", "num": "", "title": "Solved examples", "html": "<h4>Example 1 (medium) — the 90-second interview answer, rebuilt</h4>\n<p><strong>Task:</strong> A student's answer to \"tell me about yourself\" is: <em>\"Sir, I am Rahul from Chennai. I completed my 12th in 2023 with 89%. I am interested in coding and I have done many projects. I am a hardworking person and a quick learner. I like to play cricket. Thank you.\"</em> Rebuild it.</p>\n<h4>Solution</h4><p><em>Step 1 — Diagnose.</em> No structure (it is a list). No evidence (\"many projects\" — which ones?). Three adjectives doing the work that evidence should do (\"hardworking\", \"quick learner\"). Personal hobbies last and unexplained. No connection to the role. Roughly 25 seconds long — under-length, which reads as unprepared.</p>\n<p><em>Step 2 — Rebuild with now → how → next, in about 75 seconds (≈170 words).</em></p>\n<div class=\"note note--why\"><span class=\"tag\">Note</span><p>\"I'm a second-year CSE student in Chennai, and for the last year I've mostly been building things rather than only doing coursework. [<em>now, 15 s</em>]  The one I'd point to is a face-recognition attendance system — I wrote the detection and evaluation code myself, and the part I actually learned from was the failure analysis: most errors were low-light frames, so instead of pushing accuracy I added a review bucket so the system flags uncertainty instead of guessing. I also interned for six weeks at a startup, where I built the API layer for a booking flow and learned what it means when someone else's code depends on yours. And I led a four-person team in a college hackathon where we came second — mainly because we cut our scope on day one instead of day three. [<em>how, 45 s</em>]  I'm looking for a team where I can be corrected quickly while working close to the code, which is why this role interested me. [<em>next, 15 s</em>]\"</p></div>\n<p><em>Step 3 — Check against the diagnosis.</em> Structure: three labelled beats. Evidence: three specific items, each with a decision and a result. Adjectives: removed entirely — the hackathon placement and the failure-analysis decision <em>show</em> the qualities instead of claiming them. Role connection: the last line names what he wants and ties it to the role.</p>\n<div class=\"res\">15 s now + 45 s how + 15 s next, with three evidence items and no self-praise adjectives. The cricket line is not banned — it belongs in the HR round when asked \"what do you do outside work?\", not in the 90 seconds that decide whether the technical conversation happens.</div>\n<h4>Example 2 (exam level) — the mock interview, marked and diagnosed</h4>\n<p><strong>Task:</strong> You observe a 20-minute mock interview. The candidate: answered \"tell me about yourself\" for 3 minutes 40 seconds; gave a STAR answer where the Action was \"we discussed it and we fixed it\"; could not answer \"what's the time complexity of your solution?\" and said \"I don't remember, but it's fast\"; asked no questions at the end. Score him on the four criteria — structure and relevance, evidence and specificity, communication, ownership and judgement — out of 25 each, and give the three changes with the largest mark recovery.</p>\n<h4>Solution</h4><p><em>Step 1 — Score against behaviour, not impression.</em> <strong>Structure and relevance: 12/25</strong> — one answer four times the intended length (3:40 against 60–90 s), which is a structure failure, not a talking failure; the panel lost the thread and had to interrupt. <strong>Evidence and specificity: 10/25</strong> — the STAR answer's Action is \"we discussed it and we fixed it\", which contains no action, no decision and no sequence; the Result is absent. <strong>Communication: 16/25</strong> — clear and audible, but no listening signal: he did not use the pause-repeat loop and answered a complexity question with a claim about speed. <strong>Ownership and judgement: 9/25</strong> — \"we\" throughout the Action, and \"I don't remember, but it's fast\" is a bluff in disguise: it asserts a property without evidence. No questions at the end is a missed judgement signal, because it suggests no real interest in the role.</p>\n<p><em>Step 2 — Total:</em> 12 + 10 + 16 + 9 = <strong>47/100</strong>.</p>\n<p><em>Step 3 — Three changes, ranked by marks recovered.</em></p>\n<ol><li><strong>Enforce a 90-second ceiling on \"tell me about yourself\"</strong> (~+10). Rehearse with a timer and three keyword cards; if it runs over, cut the third evidence item, not the ending. Long answers are scored as unclear even when the content is fine.</li><li><strong>Rebuild the STAR answer so the Action is 55% of the words and the Result has a number</strong> (~+8). \"We discussed it and we fixed it\" must become three sequenced \"I\" actions and a measurable outcome with a lesson. This single answer type appears in half of all HR rounds.</li><li><strong>Replace \"I don't remember, but it's fast\" with a thinking-aloud move</strong> (~+6). Correct form: \"Let me reason it out — the outer loop runs n times and the inner one over a shrinking range, so it's O(n²) time and O(1) extra space. I'd verify with a quick timing test.\" A wrong-but-reasoned answer scores higher than a right-but-asserted one, because the panel is buying reasoning, not<p>recall.</p></li></ol>\n<p><em>Step 4 — The fourth change worth mentioning but not ranking:</em> prepare three questions to ask at the end. It is not a marks item on this sheet, but it changes the interviewer's memory of the candidate.</p>\n<div class=\"res\">47/100; the three highest-value fixes are <em>cap the self-introduction at 90 seconds</em> (+10), <em>rebuild STAR with sequenced actions and a quantified result</em> (+8), and <em>reason aloud instead of asserting</em> (+6). Projected post-fix score ≈71/100, which is the difference between \"maybe\" and \"shortlist\".</div>"}, {"kind": "trap", "num": "", "title": "Common traps and mistakes", "html": "<ul><li><strong>Treating the four skills as four subjects.</strong> Revision that drills reading only will not fix an interview, because interviews are listening plus speaking. Fix: practise integrated tasks.</li><li><strong>Never conceding a point.</strong> A candidate who agrees with nothing reads as uncooperative. Fix: concede one point explicitly, then add your difference.</li><li><strong>STAR with no Result.</strong> The story stops before the outcome and reads as unfinished. Fix: always end with a number or a lesson.</li><li><strong>Reciting a memorised self-introduction.</strong> It sounds recited, and the follow-up question exposes it. Fix: memorise the spine, improvise the words.</li><li><strong>\"We\" throughout the Action.</strong> The panel cannot see what you did, so they cannot score it. Fix: \"I\" for your own actions, \"we\" only for genuinely shared ones.</li><li><strong>Bluffing in a technical interview.</strong> \"It's fast\" instead of reasoning loses the round, because the panel is buying reasoning, not recall. Fix: reason aloud, state complexity, verify with an example.</li><li><strong>\"Kindly do the needful\" and \"please look into it\".</strong> Both hand the thinking back to the reader, and both get postponed. Fix: BLUF, numbered points, a deadline.</li><li><strong>Escalating before trying the direct channel.</strong> It destroys trust faster than the original problem did. Fix: direct first, then escalate with facts, impact, options, ask.</li><li><strong>Grinding full practice tests without reviewing errors.</strong> After the second test you gain only familiarity. Fix: one test, then an error log by type.</li><li><strong>A portfolio of certificates with no reflection.</strong> Certificates prove attendance, not learning. Fix: four curated entries with evidence and a next action.</li></ul>"}, {"kind": "revision", "num": "", "title": "Quick revision box", "html": "<ul><li><strong>Integration:</strong> listen → discuss → read → write, same content through all four stages; the test of a good integrated task is that the output cannot be produced from one input alone.</li><li><strong>GD:</strong> content ~30%, communication ~30%, teamwork ~25%, leadership ~15%. Contribute 3–4 times, build on others, bring in a quiet member, summarise near the end.</li><li><strong>\"Tell me about yourself\":</strong> now → how → next, 60–90 seconds, three evidence items, no self-praise adjectives, name the role.</li><li><strong>STAR:</strong> Situation 15%, Task 10%, Action 55%, Result 20%; \"I\" for your actions; always a number or a lesson in the Result.</li><li><strong>Technical rounds:</strong> restate, state assumptions, brute force first, think aloud, state complexity with a reason, test an edge case, say where you would look when stuck.</li><li><strong>Email:</strong> ask in the subject line, BLUF in line one, numbered points, a deadline, one topic per email. Escalate with facts → impact → options → ask.</li><li><strong>CEFR and tests:</strong> A1–C2 \"can do\" scale; IELTS 5.5–6.5 ≈ B2, 7.0–8.0 ≈ C1; TOEFL 72–94 ≈ B2; PTE 59–75 ≈ B2 — indicative only, and institutions set their own cut-offs per section.</li><li><strong>Mock and portfolio:</strong> define success in observable terms before practising; attack the two lowest self-ratings; one change per week, one recorded run, one rubric.</li></ul>"}], "practice": [{"q": "Name the four stages of an integrated LSRW task on \"electric vehicles in India\" and give one observable output for each stage.", "a": "<p><em>Listen:</em> a 10-minute talk on EV adoption — output: five key points plus one question you would ask. <em>Discuss:</em> 12 minutes in groups of four on whether subsidies are the right lever — output: a group position with two supporting reasons. <em>Read:</em> a one-page article with adoption data — output: the article's thesis and one piece of evidence underlined. <em>Write:</em> a 180-word summary of the talk plus 80 words on where the article and the talk disagree — output: a written piece that cites both sources by name. <strong>Key step:</strong> the written output must be impossible to produce from either input alone.</p>", "tag": "easy", "qHtml": "Name the four stages of an integrated LSRW task on \"electric vehicles in India\" and give one observable output for each stage."}, {"q": "Rewrite this subject line and first line for a BLUF email: subject \"Regarding the project\", first line \"I am writing to inform you about the status of our project which we discussed last week.\"", "a": "<p>Subject: \"Status + decision needed by Thu 21 Nov — project X\". First line: \"I need your decision on two items by Thursday so the build can start on Friday.\" <strong>Key step:</strong> the subject carries both the ask and the date, and line one states the decision required rather than the fact that an email exists.</p>", "tag": "easy", "qHtml": "Rewrite this subject line and first line for a BLUF email: subject \"Regarding the project\", first line \"I am writing to inform you about the status of our project which we discussed last week.\""}, {"q": "In a GD, another candidate says: \"Online exams are obviously useless.\" Write your response in 20 seconds that both disagrees and builds, and name the two techniques you used.", "a": "<p>\"I'd push back on 'obviously' — the honest version is that <em>unsupervised</em> online exams fail, not online exams. Proctored online exams with randomised question banks solve most of the cheating problem, and cost less than hall invigilation. So the argument isn't online versus offline; it's what safeguards we attach.\" Techniques: (1) attack the qualifier (\"obviously\"), not the person, so you disagree without hostility; (2) concede a real point (unproctored exams <em>do</em> fail) and then extend it — the \"yes, and\" build. <strong>Key step:</strong> a disagreement that ends with a constructive reformulation earns both content and teamwork marks.</p>", "tag": "medium", "qHtml": "In a GD, another candidate says: \"Online exams are obviously useless.\" Write your response in 20 seconds that both disagrees and builds, and name the two techniques you used."}, {"q": "Answer \"Tell me about a time you failed\" in STAR form, in under 120 words, keeping the Result honest rather than triumphant.", "a": "<p><strong>S:</strong> \"In my first hackathon, our team of four had 24 hours and we spent the first eight building a feature the judges didn't score.\" <strong>T:</strong> \"I was the one who had proposed it, so the call to drop it was mine.\" <strong>A:</strong> \"I raised it at hour eight instead of hour twenty, showed the scoring rubric to the team, and proposed we cut to one working flow. Then I rewrote the plan on the whiteboard with three milestones and took the demo script myself, because our presenter had not slept.\" <strong>R:</strong> \"We shipped one flow, placed nowhere — fifth of nine — but the next team I led used the same hour-eight checkpoint, and we placed second. The lesson was that I had optimised for the interesting feature instead of the scored one.\" <strong>Key step:</strong> the Result admits the failure and then shows the corrected behaviour later — that pairing is what makes a failure answer credible.</p>", "tag": "medium", "qHtml": "Answer \"Tell me about a time you failed\" in STAR form, in under 120 words, keeping the Result honest rather than triumphant."}, {"q": "A student has IELTS 6.0 (Listening 6.5, Reading 6.0, Writing 5.5, Speaking 6.0) and needs an overall 6.5 for a Master's application. Do the band arithmetic, state the minimum per-section gains needed, and name the two sections to prioritise.", "a": "<p><em>Arithmetic:</em> (6.5 + 6.0 + 5.5 + 6.0) / 4 = 24.0 / 4 = <strong>6.0</strong> now. Overall 6.5 needs an average of at least 6.25 (which rounds up to 6.5), so the sections must total <strong>at least 25</strong> — a gain of <strong>1.0 band point</strong> across the four. <em>Cheapest path:</em> Writing 5.5 → 6.0 (+0.5) and Reading 6.0 → 6.5 (+0.5) gives 25.0 → average 6.25 → <strong>6.5 overall</strong>. <em>Prioritise:</em> <strong>Writing</strong> (lowest, and half a band is very reachable from task-response work) and <strong>Reading</strong> (already close; time management alone often moves it half a band). Leave Listening and Speaking alone — they are at or above target and each extra half band there costs more effort than the same half band in Writing. <strong>Key step:</strong> check any per-section floor too — a 6.5 overall with Writing exactly 6.0 meets \"no band below 6.0\" only just, so confirm the wording.</p>", "tag": "exam", "qHtml": "A student has IELTS 6.0 (Listening 6.5, Reading 6.0, Writing 5.5, Speaking 6.0) and needs an overall 6.5 for a Master's application. Do the band arithmetic, state the minimum per-section gains needed, and name the two sections to prioritise."}, {"q": "You are blocked by another team's broken API two days before a release, and you have already messaged them twice with no reply. Write the escalation email in under 120 words and state which of the four parts (facts, impact, options, ask) each sentence belongs to.", "a": "<p>\"Subject: Blocked — staging API returning 500 since Mon 10:00 (release Fri 22) <em>(facts, in the subject)</em>. Hi Sana — the staging API has returned 500 on every call since Monday 10:00; request IDs and timestamps attached <em>(facts)</em>. I messaged the team channel on Monday and again Wednesday with no reply <em>(facts — the prior-attempt record)</em>. Our payments module cannot be integration-tested and the release is Friday <em>(impact)</em>. Two options: a new key today and we retest tomorrow, or the sandbox key with reduced scope to unblock now <em>(options)</em>; I recommend the first. Can you confirm by Thursday 10:00? <em>(ask)</em>\" <strong>Key step:</strong> facts before any statement of feeling, prior attempts documented rather than described as being ignored, and the email ends with a decision request and a time — which makes it an escalation, not a complaint.</p>", "tag": "exam", "qHtml": "You are blocked by another team's broken API two days before a release, and you have already messaged them twice with no reply. Write the escalation email in under 120 words and state which of the four parts (facts, impact, options, ask) each sentence belongs to."}], "file": "Semester_2/CSFE-CommunicationSkills/CSFE-M06.md"}];
